window.DARKTALE_SEARCH={"settings":{"duskworld":{"name":"Duskworld","url":"settings/duskworld/index.html"}},"entries":[["Rules","Rolls and results","Core Rules › Rolls and results","rules/rolls-and-results.html#rolls-and-results","Pool: Attribute + Skill in d6. Every 5 or 6 is a success. Untrained skills: When you roll a skill you have no dots in, take −1d for a Physical or Social skill, or −3d for a Mental skill. Chance die: If penalties reduce your pool to 0 dice or fewer, roll a single d6 instead. Only a 6 is a success. A 1 is a Complication with no success. Rerolls: Each die can be rerolled only once per roll. Exploding dice: When a rule says 6s explode, each 6 adds another die to the roll, which can explode too. Exploding dice are new dice, not rerolls. The stakes die and chance dice never explode. Contested rolls: Both sides roll. The side with more successes wins; the margin is the net successes. Ties go to the side that acts: the attacker, or whoever is trying to do something to the other. A tie is a win with 0 net successes, so an attack that ties is a hit with 0 net successes. Success with Opportunity: Any roll with 5 or more successes is a success with Opportunity. The stakes die can also give an Opportunity (below), even on a failed roll. Talents that \"expand the Opportunity range\" lower this threshold by 1 per step (to 4+, then 3+). The threshold never goes below 3. Stakes die: When a character ",""],["Rules","Pool","Core Rules › Rolls and results","rules/rolls-and-results.html#rolls-and-results","Pool: Attribute + Skill in d6. Every 5 or 6 is a success. ",""],["Rules","Untrained skills","Core Rules › Rolls and results","rules/rolls-and-results.html#rolls-and-results","Untrained skills: When you roll a skill you have no dots in, take −1d for a Physical or Social skill, or −3d for a Mental skill. ",""],["Rules","Chance die","Core Rules › Rolls and results","rules/rolls-and-results.html#rolls-and-results","Chance die: If penalties reduce your pool to 0 dice or fewer, roll a single d6 instead. Only a 6 is a success. A 1 is a Complication with no success. ",""],["Rules","Rerolls","Core Rules › Rolls and results","rules/rolls-and-results.html#rolls-and-results","Rerolls: Each die can be rerolled only once per roll. ",""],["Rules","Exploding dice","Core Rules › Rolls and results","rules/rolls-and-results.html#rolls-and-results","Exploding dice: When a rule says 6s explode, each 6 adds another die to the roll, which can explode too. Exploding dice are new dice, not rerolls. The stakes die and chance dice never explode. ",""],["Rules","Contested rolls","Core Rules › Rolls and results","rules/rolls-and-results.html#rolls-and-results","Contested rolls: Both sides roll. The side with more successes wins; the margin is the net successes. Ties go to the side that acts: the attacker, or whoever is trying to do something to the other. A tie is a win with 0 net successes, so an attack that ties is a hit with 0 net successes. ",""],["Rules","Success with Opportunity","Core Rules › Rolls and results","rules/rolls-and-results.html#rolls-and-results","Success with Opportunity: Any roll with 5 or more successes is a success with Opportunity. The stakes die can also give an Opportunity (below), even on a failed roll. Talents that \"expand the Opportunity range\" lower this threshold by 1 per step (to 4+, then 3+). The threshold never goes below 3. ",""],["Rules","Stakes die","Core Rules › Rolls and results","rules/rolls-and-results.html#rolls-and-results","Stakes die: When a character raises the stakes, they roll an extra d6 of a different colour along with their pool: 5–6: an Opportunity, but no success. 1–2: a Complication, which also counts as a success. 3–4: nothing. Overall, the stakes die succeeds as often as a normal die; what it adds is the swing. An Opportunity from the stakes die applies even if the roll fails: you don't get what you wanted, but you gain something else.",""],["Rules","Raising the stakes","Core Rules › Rolls and results","rules/rolls-and-results.html#rolls-and-results","Raising the stakes: The GM raises the stakes on a roll when the outcome could change the situation in a big way: a desperate leap, a lie to someone powerful, a blow that could end a fight. Once per day, a player can also choose to raise the stakes on their own roll, before rolling. Talents and effects can add more uses. A roll never has more than one stakes die. ",""],["Rules","Opportunities and Complications","Core Rules › Rolls and results","rules/rolls-and-results.html#rolls-and-results","Opportunities and Complications: On an Opportunity, the player chooses a benefit that fits the roll (an extra effect, a useful detail, a better position), and the GM approves it. On a Complication, the GM adds a setback that fits the roll (a lost item, an alerted guard, a slip). A Complication never cancels a success. A roll can show both. ",""],["Rules","Teamwork","Core Rules › Rolls and results","rules/rolls-and-results.html#rolls-and-results","Teamwork: Before a character rolls, a helper can roll a fitting Attribute + Skill (in combat, this costs the helper 1 action). With at least 1 success, the main roll gets +1d; on an Opportunity, +2d. If the helper's roll shows a Complication, the main roll suffers it too. Helpers give at most +2d to a single roll in total. ",""],["Rules","Specialties","Core Rules › Rolls and results","rules/rolls-and-results.html#rolls-and-results","Specialties: +1d or more when one applies (see Specialties). ",""],["Rules","Fixed difficulty","Core Rules › Rolls and results","rules/rolls-and-results.html#rolls-and-results","Fixed difficulty: When there's no opposing roll, the roll needs a number of successes: 2 is a hard task, 3 is a very hard one. ",""],["Rules","Modifiers","Core Rules › Modifiers","rules/rolls-and-results.html#modifiers","Situational bonuses and penalties to dice pools. They add together; if they bring a pool to 0 dice or fewer, roll a chance die. Advantage / disadvantage: +1d / −1d, for anything that helps or hinders a roll and has no rule of its own.",""],["Rules","Advantage / disadvantage","Core Rules › Modifiers","rules/rolls-and-results.html#modifiers","Advantage / disadvantage: +1d / −1d, for anything that helps or hinders a roll and has no rule of its own. ",""],["Rules","Light","Core Rules › Modifiers","rules/rolls-and-results.html#light","Rolls that rely on sight take −1d in dim light and −3d in darkness. In total darkness (no light at all, or unnatural darkness a setting defines), you're effectively Blinded: rolls that rely on sight use a chance die.",""],["Rules","Cover and concealment","Core Rules › Modifiers","rules/rolls-and-results.html#cover-and-concealment","Cover: Something solid blocks part of you. Ranged attacks against you take −1d. With full cover, you can't be targeted. Obscured: Smoke, fog or shadow makes you Obscured: rolls to see or target you take −1d, and you can try to hide with Stealth. Deeper darkness uses the Light rules.",""],["Rules","Cover","Core Rules › Cover and concealment","rules/rolls-and-results.html#cover-and-concealment","Cover: Something solid blocks part of you. Ranged attacks against you take −1d. With full cover, you can't be targeted. ",""],["Rules","Obscured","Core Rules › Cover and concealment","rules/rolls-and-results.html#cover-and-concealment","Obscured: Smoke, fog or shadow makes you Obscured: rolls to see or target you take −1d, and you can try to hide with Stealth. Deeper darkness uses the Light rules. ",""],["Rules","Position","Core Rules › Modifiers","rules/rolls-and-results.html#position","Prone: melee attacks against you get +1d; ranged attacks against you get −1d. Difficult terrain: −1d to Physical rolls, including attacks and defense rolls, while you stand in it (see Terrain and forced movement). Bracing: the Brace action gives +1d to your defense rolls while you're in cover.",""],["Rules","Prone","Core Rules › Position","rules/rolls-and-results.html#position","Prone: melee attacks against you get +1d; ranged attacks against you get −1d. ",""],["Rules","Difficult terrain","Core Rules › Position","rules/rolls-and-results.html#position","Difficult terrain: −1d to Physical rolls, including attacks and defense rolls, while you stand in it (see Terrain and forced movement). ",""],["Rules","Bracing","Core Rules › Position","rules/rolls-and-results.html#position","Bracing: the Brace action gives +1d to your defense rolls while you're in cover. ",""],["Rules","Other modifiers","Core Rules › Modifiers","rules/rolls-and-results.html#other-modifiers","Defined where they're used: Modifier Effect See Untrained skill −1d Physical or Social, −3d Mental Rolls and results Specialty +1d to +3d Specialties Pushing yourself +2d for 2 Stress Stress Teamwork +1d, or +2d on an Opportunity Rolls and results Set up +1d on your next roll against the target Actions Called shot −1d to −3d Called shots Parrying an armed attack unarmed −2d Defense and reactions Beyond a weapon's range −2d Weapon traits Heavy weapon without Strength 3 −1d per missing dot Weapon traits Medium or heavy armor −1d to Stealth and Athletics Armor Kit / no proper tools +1d / −1d Kits Treating yourself with First aid −1d Actions Exhausted −1d per stack Conditions",""],["Rules","Contested pools","Core Rules › Contested pools","rules/rolls-and-results.html#contested-pools","When an effect is \"contested by\" a pool, the target rolls the one that fits: Against Contested by Feints, tricks, lies, mental attacks Intelligence + Resolve Pressure, taunts, intimidation, fear Wits + Composure Physical effects that aren't attacks (shoves, poisons, traps) Dexterity + Stamina Noticing something hidden (a sneaking character, a concealed trap) Wits + Composure",""],["Rules","Extended actions","Core Rules › Extended actions","rules/rolls-and-results.html#extended-actions","An extended action is a task that takes many rolls over time: researching in an archive, repairing a machine, forging papers, tracking someone across open country. The GM sets: Pool: the Attribute + Skill that fits, as usual. Target: the total successes needed. Interval: how long each roll takes (minutes, hours or days). Task Target Rolls with a 6-die pool Quick 3 about 2 Standard 6 about 3 Long 10 about 5 Major 15 about 8 Rolling: each roll adds its successes to the total. When the total reaches the target, the task is done. Opportunity: choose a benefit as usual, such as finishing this interval in half the time, saving materials, or a useful discovery. An Opportunity never adds successes to the total. Complication: the GM adds a setback that fits: lose 1 success already gained, spend extra time or materials, or draw attention. No successes: take 1 Stress to press on, or the attempt fails. Trying again usually needs a new approach, new tools or new information. (During downtime, no Stress is paid: the work stops until a later Project action.) Pushing, the stakes die and specialties work on each roll as normal, except that downtime rolls can't be pushed. Teamwork works on each roll",""],["Rules","Pool","Core Rules › Extended actions","rules/rolls-and-results.html#extended-actions","Pool: the Attribute + Skill that fits, as usual. ",""],["Rules","Target","Core Rules › Extended actions","rules/rolls-and-results.html#extended-actions","Target: the total successes needed. ",""],["Rules","Interval","Core Rules › Extended actions","rules/rolls-and-results.html#extended-actions","Interval: how long each roll takes (minutes, hours or days). ",""],["Rules","Rolling","Core Rules › Extended actions","rules/rolls-and-results.html#extended-actions","Rolling: each roll adds its successes to the total. When the total reaches the target, the task is done. ",""],["Rules","Opportunity","Core Rules › Extended actions","rules/rolls-and-results.html#extended-actions","Opportunity: choose a benefit as usual, such as finishing this interval in half the time, saving materials, or a useful discovery. An Opportunity never adds successes to the total. ",""],["Rules","Complication","Core Rules › Extended actions","rules/rolls-and-results.html#extended-actions","Complication: the GM adds a setback that fits: lose 1 success already gained, spend extra time or materials, or draw attention. ",""],["Rules","No successes","Core Rules › Extended actions","rules/rolls-and-results.html#extended-actions","No successes: take 1 Stress to press on, or the attempt fails. Trying again usually needs a new approach, new tools or new information. (During downtime, no Stress is paid: the work stops until a later Project action.) ",""],["Rules","Pushing, the stakes die and specialties","Core Rules › Extended actions","rules/rolls-and-results.html#extended-actions","Pushing, the stakes die and specialties work on each roll as normal, except that downtime rolls can't be pushed. ",""],["Rules","Teamwork","Core Rules › Extended actions","rules/rolls-and-results.html#extended-actions","Teamwork works on each roll. If the GM agrees the task can be split (searching an archive, but not forging one document), several characters can instead work in parallel: each rolls their own pool every interval, and all their successes count toward the target. ",""],["Rules","Deadlines","Core Rules › Extended actions","rules/rolls-and-results.html#extended-actions","Deadlines: the GM can set one (\"before the curfew bell\"). If the target isn't reached in time, the task fails or only partly succeeds. ",""],["Rules","Crafting and repair","Core Rules › Extended actions","rules/rolls-and-results.html#extended-actions","Crafting and repair are extended actions with their own targets and upgrades (see Crafting). ",""],["Rules","Contested extended actions","Core Rules › Extended actions","rules/rolls-and-results.html#extended-actions","Contested extended actions (chases, races, a long debate): both sides roll each interval, and the first to reach the target wins. Ties go to the side that started it (the pursuer, the challenger). ",""],["Rules","Skills","Core Rules › Skills","rules/skills-and-specialties.html#skills","Every character uses the same 24 skills, eight in each category. Skills run 0–5: 1 below average, 2 normally skilled, 3 highly trained, 4 exceptional, 5 the best of the best. Settings can add power skills for characters with access to supernatural powers.",""],["Rules","Mental","Core Rules › Skills","rules/skills-and-specialties.html#mental","Skill Covers Example specialties Academics Learning from study: history, law, languages, theology History, Law, Theology Crafts Making, building and repairing physical things Smithing, Carpentry, Repairs Under Pressure Investigation Finding clues, solving puzzles, research Crime Scenes, Riddles, Research Medicine Treating wounds and illness, anatomy Surgery, Trauma, Pharmacology Occult Lore of the supernatural: rituals, folklore, curses Folklore, Rituals, Curses Politics Institutions, bureaucracy, and who holds power Bureaucracy, Scandals, Local Government Science How the world works in principle: natural philosophy, chemistry, engineering theory Chemistry, Engineering, Astronomy Technology Operating, understanding and subverting the most advanced machines of the setting Security Systems, Power Systems, Communication Systems Crafts, Science and Technology: Crafts makes and repairs things, Science understands how they work, and Technology operates and manipulates complex systems. What counts as \"advanced machines\" depends on the setting: computers in a modern world, runetech and kren machines in Duskworld.",""],["Rules","Physical","Core Rules › Skills","rules/skills-and-specialties.html#physical","Skill Covers Example specialties Athletics Running, climbing, swimming, jumping Climbing, Running, Swimming Brawl Unarmed fighting Grappling, Dirty Fighting, Boxing Drive Controlling any vehicle or mount, from horses and wagons to trains Wagons, Riding, Pursuit Ranged Bows, crossbows, firearms and thrown weapons Bows, Crossbows, Thrown Weapons Stealth Moving unseen and unheard Crowds, Shadowing, Rooftops Survival Living off the land, tracking, finding your way in the wild Foraging, Tracking, Navigation Thievery Locks, pickpocketing, security, sleight of hand Lockpicking, Pickpocketing, Safecracking Weaponry Fighting with melee weapons Swords, Knives, Spears",""],["Rules","Social","Core Rules › Skills","rules/skills-and-specialties.html#social","Skill Covers Example specialties Animal Ken Understanding, calming and training animals Dogs, Horses, Training Empathy Reading people's emotions and motives Lies, Emotions, Motives Expression Art, performance, speeches and writing Speeches, Music, Poetry Intimidation Threats and coercion Threats, Interrogation, Stare-downs Persuasion Convincing people by argument or charm Bargaining, Fast Talk, Oratory Socialize Fitting in, etiquette, working a crowd Etiquette, Parties, Carousing Streetwise The streets, the underworld and its rumors Rumors, Black Market, Gangs Subterfuge Lying, disguise, hiding your intentions Lying, Disguises, Hiding Emotions",""],["Rules","Power skills","Core Rules › Skills","rules/skills-and-specialties.html#power-skills","A setting's magic, psychic or other supernatural system can add power skills: skills for using that power, such as shaping dreams or bending shadows. The setting defines each one, including which attribute it's usually rolled with. Access: only a character with access to the power can have its power skills. Access comes from whatever the setting says grants the power: a talent, a racial trait, a community trait, or a story event. First dot free: when you gain access, you gain 1 permanent dot in the power skill. Outside the categories: power skills aren't Mental, Physical or Social. They aren't part of the 22 skill dots at creation and don't count against the 11/7/4 split. Improving: raise them through advancement like any other skill, at the same cost. No untrained rolls: without access, you can't roll a power skill at all. Everything else works as usual: specialties, the 0–5 scale, and the rule that talent prerequisites count only permanent dots.",""],["Rules","Access","Core Rules › Power skills","rules/skills-and-specialties.html#power-skills","Access: only a character with access to the power can have its power skills. Access comes from whatever the setting says grants the power: a talent, a racial trait, a community trait, or a story event. ",""],["Rules","First dot free","Core Rules › Power skills","rules/skills-and-specialties.html#power-skills","First dot free: when you gain access, you gain 1 permanent dot in the power skill. ",""],["Rules","Outside the categories","Core Rules › Power skills","rules/skills-and-specialties.html#power-skills","Outside the categories: power skills aren't Mental, Physical or Social. They aren't part of the 22 skill dots at creation and don't count against the 11/7/4 split. ",""],["Rules","Improving","Core Rules › Power skills","rules/skills-and-specialties.html#power-skills","Improving: raise them through advancement like any other skill, at the same cost. ",""],["Rules","No untrained rolls","Core Rules › Power skills","rules/skills-and-specialties.html#power-skills","No untrained rolls: without access, you can't roll a power skill at all. ",""],["Rules","Everything else works as usual","Core Rules › Power skills","rules/skills-and-specialties.html#power-skills","Everything else works as usual: specialties, the 0–5 scale, and the rule that talent prerequisites count only permanent dots. ",""],["Rules","Power pools","Core Rules › Skills","rules/skills-and-specialties.html#power-pools","Each power has its own pool, which the setting names (Investiture, Mana, Dread…). Its abilities spend from it. Size: 2 + the higher of two attributes the setting names for that power. Spending: each ability says its cost. A sustained ability costs its upkeep each round in combat, or each minute or hour outside it, as the ability says. Running empty: when your pool is empty, you can pay a cost by taking 2 Stress per point. Stress taken this way can overflow like any other (see Stress). Refilling: the setting says how a pool refills. By default, it refills fully at the end of a rest and at the end of a week of downtime. Reserved points: an effect's initial cost stays reserved while the effect is active: a refill doesn't return those points until the effect ends. Upkeep paid to sustain it is spent as usual and isn't reserved. Instant effects reserve nothing. You can end your own effect at any time, as a free action. More than one power: each power keeps its own pool.",""],["Rules","Size","Core Rules › Power pools","rules/skills-and-specialties.html#power-pools","Size: 2 + the higher of two attributes the setting names for that power. ",""],["Rules","Spending","Core Rules › Power pools","rules/skills-and-specialties.html#power-pools","Spending: each ability says its cost. A sustained ability costs its upkeep each round in combat, or each minute or hour outside it, as the ability says. ",""],["Rules","Running empty","Core Rules › Power pools","rules/skills-and-specialties.html#power-pools","Running empty: when your pool is empty, you can pay a cost by taking 2 Stress per point. Stress taken this way can overflow like any other (see Stress). ",""],["Rules","Refilling","Core Rules › Power pools","rules/skills-and-specialties.html#power-pools","Refilling: the setting says how a pool refills. By default, it refills fully at the end of a rest and at the end of a week of downtime. ",""],["Rules","Reserved points","Core Rules › Power pools","rules/skills-and-specialties.html#power-pools","Reserved points: an effect's initial cost stays reserved while the effect is active: a refill doesn't return those points until the effect ends. Upkeep paid to sustain it is spent as usual and isn't reserved. Instant effects reserve nothing. You can end your own effect at any time, as a free action. ",""],["Rules","More than one power","Core Rules › Power pools","rules/skills-and-specialties.html#power-pools","More than one power: each power keeps its own pool. ",""],["Rules","Compulsion","Core Rules › Skills","rules/skills-and-specialties.html#compulsion","Only supernatural powers can decide what a character does. Ordinary effects (threats, persuasion, fear) change dice, Stress and conditions; the player still chooses. When a power compels a target, these rules always apply unless the power says otherwise: Contested: the target resists with Intelligence + Resolve (commands, charms, false loyalties) or Wits + Composure (fear, overwhelming urges), as the power says. One command: the power imposes one short instruction, for the duration the power sets. Broken by harm: the compulsion ends at once if the target is harmed, or is ordered to harm themselves. Breaking free: a compelled character can take 2 Stress to act freely until the end of their turn, but they're Stunned for that turn: in combat, they take a slow turn with one action and no reaction. Aftermath: when it ends, the target knows something was done to them.",""],["Rules","Contested","Core Rules › Compulsion","rules/skills-and-specialties.html#compulsion","Contested: the target resists with Intelligence + Resolve (commands, charms, false loyalties) or Wits + Composure (fear, overwhelming urges), as the power says. ",""],["Rules","One command","Core Rules › Compulsion","rules/skills-and-specialties.html#compulsion","One command: the power imposes one short instruction, for the duration the power sets. ",""],["Rules","Broken by harm","Core Rules › Compulsion","rules/skills-and-specialties.html#compulsion","Broken by harm: the compulsion ends at once if the target is harmed, or is ordered to harm themselves. ",""],["Rules","Breaking free","Core Rules › Compulsion","rules/skills-and-specialties.html#compulsion","Breaking free: a compelled character can take 2 Stress to act freely until the end of their turn, but they're Stunned for that turn: in combat, they take a slow turn with one action and no reaction. ",""],["Rules","Aftermath","Core Rules › Compulsion","rules/skills-and-specialties.html#compulsion","Aftermath: when it ends, the target knows something was done to them. ",""],["Rules","Specialties","Core Rules › Specialties","rules/skills-and-specialties.html#specialties","A specialty is a narrow focus within a skill, written as Skill (Specialty): Weaponry (Swords), Survival (Tracking). The skill tables list examples. Requirement: you need at least 1 permanent dot in a skill to choose or buy a specialty in it. Granted specialties: a specialty granted by a talent or trait ignores that requirement. You gain it even with 0 dots in the skill, and it always gives at least +1d, which offsets part of the untrained penalty. Improving it past +1d still follows the cap below. Bonus: when a specialty applies to a roll, add its bonus: +1d for a new specialty. One per roll: a skill can have several specialties, but only one applies to any roll. Use the one with the highest bonus. Improving a specialty: instead of gaining a new specialty, you can improve one you already have. Each improvement adds +1d to its bonus, up to +3d. A specialty's bonus can't be higher than your permanent dots in its skill. An improvement costs the same as gaining another specialty in that skill (see Advancement). Duplicates: if a talent or trait grants a specialty you already have, improve that specialty by one step instead. Scope: a specialty must be narrower than its skill. Swords, not",""],["Rules","Requirement","Core Rules › Specialties","rules/skills-and-specialties.html#specialties","Requirement: you need at least 1 permanent dot in a skill to choose or buy a specialty in it. ",""],["Rules","Granted specialties","Core Rules › Specialties","rules/skills-and-specialties.html#specialties","Granted specialties: a specialty granted by a talent or trait ignores that requirement. You gain it even with 0 dots in the skill, and it always gives at least +1d, which offsets part of the untrained penalty. Improving it past +1d still follows the cap below. ",""],["Rules","Bonus","Core Rules › Specialties","rules/skills-and-specialties.html#specialties","Bonus: when a specialty applies to a roll, add its bonus: +1d for a new specialty. ",""],["Rules","One per roll","Core Rules › Specialties","rules/skills-and-specialties.html#specialties","One per roll: a skill can have several specialties, but only one applies to any roll. Use the one with the highest bonus. ",""],["Rules","Improving a specialty","Core Rules › Specialties","rules/skills-and-specialties.html#specialties","Improving a specialty: instead of gaining a new specialty, you can improve one you already have. Each improvement adds +1d to its bonus, up to +3d. A specialty's bonus can't be higher than your permanent dots in its skill. An improvement costs the same as gaining another specialty in that skill (see Advancement). ",""],["Rules","Duplicates","Core Rules › Specialties","rules/skills-and-specialties.html#specialties","Duplicates: if a talent or trait grants a specialty you already have, improve that specialty by one step instead. ",""],["Rules","Scope","Core Rules › Specialties","rules/skills-and-specialties.html#specialties","Scope: a specialty must be narrower than its skill. Swords, not Melee Weapons; Tracking, not Wilderness. The GM approves each one. ",""],["Rules","Stress","Core Rules › Stress","rules/stress-and-madness.html#stress","Stress track: Your current Tenacity + 2 boxes (see Tenacity and madness). As you take Tenacity damage, your Stress track shrinks. Shrinking track: If your Stress track shrinks below the Stress you already have, the Stress that no longer fits is lost. It doesn't overflow again. Push yourself: Before a roll, take 2 Stress to add +2d to it. Extra reaction: Once your reaction for the round is spent, take 2 Stress to react again. You can repeat this as long as you can pay. Recovery roll: Resolve + Composure. Clear 1 Stress per success. Clearing Stress: Stress doesn't clear on its own with time. It clears at a rest (see Rest), at the end of a week of downtime, by catching your breath (see Actions), through talents, by surrendering while Beaten Down, and when it overflows (below). Overflow: You can still take Stress when your track is full, whether you chose to or an effect forced it on you. When Stress doesn't fit, it overflows: Each point that doesn't fit becomes 1 Tenacity damage. Your Stress track shrinks accordingly, and stays full. You roll one Bout of Madness for the incident (see Tenacity and madness). If you survive the Bout, clear half your Stress, rounded up. The madness itself",""],["Rules","Stress track","Core Rules › Stress","rules/stress-and-madness.html#stress","Stress track: Your current Tenacity + 2 boxes (see Tenacity and madness). As you take Tenacity damage, your Stress track shrinks. ",""],["Rules","Shrinking track","Core Rules › Stress","rules/stress-and-madness.html#stress","Shrinking track: If your Stress track shrinks below the Stress you already have, the Stress that no longer fits is lost. It doesn't overflow again. ",""],["Rules","Push yourself","Core Rules › Stress","rules/stress-and-madness.html#stress","Push yourself: Before a roll, take 2 Stress to add +2d to it. ",""],["Rules","Extra reaction","Core Rules › Stress","rules/stress-and-madness.html#stress","Extra reaction: Once your reaction for the round is spent, take 2 Stress to react again. You can repeat this as long as you can pay. ",""],["Rules","Recovery roll","Core Rules › Stress","rules/stress-and-madness.html#stress","Recovery roll: Resolve + Composure. Clear 1 Stress per success. ",""],["Rules","Clearing Stress","Core Rules › Stress","rules/stress-and-madness.html#stress","Clearing Stress: Stress doesn't clear on its own with time. It clears at a rest (see Rest), at the end of a week of downtime, by catching your breath (see Actions), through talents, by surrendering while Beaten Down, and when it overflows (below). ",""],["Rules","Overflow","Core Rules › Stress","rules/stress-and-madness.html#stress","Overflow: You can still take Stress when your track is full, whether you chose to or an effect forced it on you. When Stress doesn't fit, it overflows: Each point that doesn't fit becomes 1 Tenacity damage. Your Stress track shrinks accordingly, and stays full. You roll one Bout of Madness for the incident (see Tenacity and madness). If you survive the Bout, clear half your Stress, rounded up. The madness itself still plays out.",""],["Rules","Tenacity and madness","Core Rules › Tenacity and madness","rules/stress-and-madness.html#tenacity-and-madness","Tenacity measures a character's sanity. Your Tenacity track has Resolve + Composure + 3 boxes. Tenacity damage: Comes from Stress overflow and from effects that say so. Bout of Madness: Each incident that deals Tenacity damage triggers one Bout of Madness roll, however many points it cost. Roll 3d6, add the dice together, and subtract the total Tenacity you're missing. Some Scars add a further penalty. Look up the result below. Recovery: Tenacity doesn't heal on its own. It heals only through the Recuperate downtime action (see Downtime). Result Effect 0 or less Death. Your heart stops and you die. 1 Catatonia. You fall Prone and can't act or react. At the end of each hour, roll 1d; a success ends the madness. 2 Stricken. You become Stunned. At the end of each minute, roll 1d; a success ends the madness. 3 Sickened. You vomit uncontrollably and are Disoriented until the madness ends. At the end of each round, roll 1d; a success ends the madness. 4 Hallucinations. You believe vermin infest your body. Until the madness ends, you must use an action each turn to cut or claw yourself, unless you're prevented. Each time, you take 1 bashing damage. At the end of each round, roll 1d; a suc",""],["Rules","Tenacity","Core Rules › Tenacity and madness","rules/stress-and-madness.html#tenacity-and-madness","Tenacity measures a character's sanity. Your Tenacity track has Resolve + Composure + 3 boxes. ",""],["Rules","Tenacity damage","Core Rules › Tenacity and madness","rules/stress-and-madness.html#tenacity-and-madness","Tenacity damage: Comes from Stress overflow and from effects that say so. ",""],["Rules","Bout of Madness","Core Rules › Tenacity and madness","rules/stress-and-madness.html#tenacity-and-madness","Bout of Madness: Each incident that deals Tenacity damage triggers one Bout of Madness roll, however many points it cost. Roll 3d6, add the dice together, and subtract the total Tenacity you're missing. Some Scars add a further penalty. Look up the result below. ",""],["Rules","Recovery","Core Rules › Tenacity and madness","rules/stress-and-madness.html#tenacity-and-madness","Recovery: Tenacity doesn't heal on its own. It heals only through the Recuperate downtime action (see Downtime). ",""],["Rules","Turns and actions","Core Rules › Turns and actions","rules/turns-and-actions.html#turns-and-actions","Rounds: a round is about 6 seconds of in-world time. Fast and slow turns: Each player chooses at the start of every round. The round runs: fast PCs (2 actions), fast NPCs (2 actions), slow PCs (3 actions), slow NPCs (3 actions). Within a phase, players choose their own order. The GM assigns NPC speeds. Once per turn: Each action type can be taken once per turn, so one attack per turn by default. Talents that allow more say so explicitly. Move (1 action): move up to your Speed in meters (see Character Creation). Move is an exception to the once-per-turn rule: you can take it as many times as you have actions, so spending two actions moves you up to twice your Speed. Free actions cost no actions. Reaction: One per round. It refreshes at the start of your turn. Ambush: A character who is unaware of you when you attack or act against them is Surprised until the end of their next turn. A character is unsuspecting while Surprised or unaware of you.",""],["Rules","Rounds","Core Rules › Turns and actions","rules/turns-and-actions.html#turns-and-actions","Rounds: a round is about 6 seconds of in-world time. ",""],["Rules","Fast and slow turns","Core Rules › Turns and actions","rules/turns-and-actions.html#turns-and-actions","Fast and slow turns: Each player chooses at the start of every round. The round runs: fast PCs (2 actions), fast NPCs (2 actions), slow PCs (3 actions), slow NPCs (3 actions). Within a phase, players choose their own order. The GM assigns NPC speeds. ",""],["Rules","Once per turn","Core Rules › Turns and actions","rules/turns-and-actions.html#turns-and-actions","Once per turn: Each action type can be taken once per turn, so one attack per turn by default. Talents that allow more say so explicitly. ",""],["Rules","Move","Core Rules › Turns and actions","rules/turns-and-actions.html#turns-and-actions","Move (1 action): move up to your Speed in meters (see Character Creation). Move is an exception to the once-per-turn rule: you can take it as many times as you have actions, so spending two actions moves you up to twice your Speed. ",""],["Rules","Free actions","Core Rules › Turns and actions","rules/turns-and-actions.html#turns-and-actions","Free actions cost no actions. ",""],["Rules","Reaction","Core Rules › Turns and actions","rules/turns-and-actions.html#turns-and-actions","Reaction: One per round. It refreshes at the start of your turn. ",""],["Rules","Ambush","Core Rules › Turns and actions","rules/turns-and-actions.html#turns-and-actions","Ambush: A character who is unaware of you when you attack or act against them is Surprised until the end of their next turn. A character is unsuspecting while Surprised or unaware of you. ",""],["Rules","Usage limits","Core Rules › Turns and actions","rules/turns-and-actions.html#usage-limits","Limited abilities (talents, traits, a player's daily stakes raise) and durations are measured in in-world time. Limit Refreshes Once per round At the start of each round Once per minute One minute after use Once per hour One hour after use Once per day At the end of a rest",""],["Rules","Actions","Core Rules › Turns and actions","rules/turns-and-actions.html#actions","Action Cost Effect Attack 1 A weapon or unarmed attack (see Attacks). Once per turn. Move 1 Move up to your Speed. Can be taken more than once per turn. Disengage 1 Move 1 meter without provoking Reactive Strikes. Set up 1 Create an opening against a target (below). Brace 1 +1d to your defense rolls while you're in cover. Use a skill 1+ Pick a lock, give first aid, work a machine. The GM sets how many actions it takes. Interact 1 Draw or stow a weapon, open a door, grab an item. Stand up 1 Stand up from Prone. Grapple 1 Start or act in a grapple; counts as your attack (see Grappling). First aid 2 Stop an adjacent character's Bleeding (below). Catch your breath 2 Roll Composure and clear 1 Stress per success. Once per day. Set up: Describe how you create an opening, then roll an Attribute + Skill that fits, contested by the target. On a success, you get +1d on your next roll against that target before the end of your next turn. The target defends with the contested pool that fits: Intelligence + Resolve against feints, tricks and studying their weaknesses; Wits + Composure against taunts, pressure and intimidation; Dexterity + Stamina against physical maneuvers. First aid: Treat an ",""],["Rules","Success","Core Rules › Actions","rules/turns-and-actions.html#actions","Success: the target stops Bleeding. First aid doesn't heal damage, and an unconscious target stays unconscious (see Waking up). ",""],["Rules","Opportunity","Core Rules › Actions","rules/turns-and-actions.html#actions","Opportunity: choose a benefit as usual, such as +1d to the next Recover on the target at a rest. ",""],["Rules","Complication","Core Rules › Actions","rules/turns-and-actions.html#actions","Complication: the GM adds a setback that fits, such as using one more charge. ",""],["Rules","Searching someone","Core Rules › Turns and actions","rules/turns-and-actions.html#searching-someone","The searcher makes one roll, usually Wits + Investigation. If the person being searched is actively hiding something, it's contested by their Dexterity + Thievery or Subterfuge; otherwise it needs a fixed number of successes set by the GM. Ordinary hidden items are found if the search succeeds. Discreet items need 1 more success: 1 extra success against a fixed difficulty, or at least 1 net success in a contest. One roll can find the pistol in your coat and still miss the knife in your boot.",""],["Rules","Ordinary hidden items","Core Rules › Searching someone","rules/turns-and-actions.html#searching-someone","Ordinary hidden items are found if the search succeeds. ",""],["Rules","Discreet items","Core Rules › Searching someone","rules/turns-and-actions.html#searching-someone","Discreet items need 1 more success: 1 extra success against a fixed difficulty, or at least 1 net success in a contest. ",""],["Rules","Ranges","Core Rules › Ranges","rules/turns-and-actions.html#ranges","On a battlemap, one square is 1 meter. Talents and rules use four fixed ranges: Range Distance Used for Adjacent 1 meter (the next square) Touching, melee, and moving \"a short distance\" Close Within 5 meters Anything \"close to you\": commands, aid and most support talents Near Within 10 meters (about one Move) Anything \"near\" a character, shouted orders, small areas Far Beyond 10 meters Sight and ranged weapons A short distance is 1 meter: one adjacent square. A small area is a 3 × 3 meter square (9 squares on a map). Talents can extend ranges. For example, Authority doubles the range of your Leader talents, so their \"close\" becomes 10 meters.",""],["Rules","A short distance","Core Rules › Ranges","rules/turns-and-actions.html#ranges","A short distance is 1 meter: one adjacent square. ",""],["Rules","A small area","Core Rules › Ranges","rules/turns-and-actions.html#ranges","A small area is a 3 × 3 meter square (9 squares on a map). ",""],["Rules","Talents can extend ranges","Core Rules › Ranges","rules/turns-and-actions.html#ranges","Talents can extend ranges. For example, Authority doubles the range of your Leader talents, so their \"close\" becomes 10 meters. ",""],["Rules","Terrain and forced movement","Core Rules › Ranges","rules/turns-and-actions.html#terrain-and-forced-movement","Difficult terrain (rubble, deep mud, a crowd, an entangling trap): moving through it costs double, so a Move covers half your Speed there. You take −1d to Physical rolls while standing in it: any roll using a Physical attribute or skill, including attacks and defense rolls (Dodge, Parry or rolled Defense). Dangerous terrain (fire, spikes, Dusk-blighted ground): terrain that deals damage when you enter it or end your turn in it. The source sets the damage. Pushed back: the effect that pushes you says how far. You move that distance straight away from the source. If something solid stops you early, you fall Prone. Being pushed doesn't provoke Reactive Strikes. Armor can take 1 Wear to ignore a push (see Armor).",""],["Rules","Difficult terrain","Core Rules › Terrain and forced movement","rules/turns-and-actions.html#terrain-and-forced-movement","Difficult terrain (rubble, deep mud, a crowd, an entangling trap): moving through it costs double, so a Move covers half your Speed there. You take −1d to Physical rolls while standing in it: any roll using a Physical attribute or skill, including attacks and defense rolls (Dodge, Parry or rolled Defense). ",""],["Rules","Dangerous terrain","Core Rules › Terrain and forced movement","rules/turns-and-actions.html#terrain-and-forced-movement","Dangerous terrain (fire, spikes, Dusk-blighted ground): terrain that deals damage when you enter it or end your turn in it. The source sets the damage. ",""],["Rules","Pushed back","Core Rules › Terrain and forced movement","rules/turns-and-actions.html#terrain-and-forced-movement","Pushed back: the effect that pushes you says how far. You move that distance straight away from the source. If something solid stops you early, you fall Prone. Being pushed doesn't provoke Reactive Strikes. Armor can take 1 Wear to ignore a push (see Armor). ",""],["Rules","Attacks","Core Rules › Attacks","rules/attacks.html#attacks","Attack pools: Strength + Weaponry for melee weapons, Strength + Brawl for unarmed attacks, and Dexterity + Ranged for ranged and thrown weapons. Talents can change the attribute for some weapons. Hits: Every attack is contested (see Defense and reactions). It hits if the attacker's successes equal or beat the defender's; a tie is a hit with 0 net successes, dealing the weapon's damage alone. Damage: A hit deals damage equal to the net successes plus the weapon's damage, of the weapon's damage type. Armor reduces it (see Armor). Opportunity on attacks is based on the attacker's total successes, not net successes. Off-hand attack: while holding a Light weapon in your other hand, take 1 Stress to make one extra attack with it (1 action). This is an exception to the one-attack rule. Extra attacks: each attack after your first in a turn takes a cumulative −2d (−2d on the second, −4d on the third), whatever grants it. A single action that attacks several targets, such as Unstoppable Charge, counts as one attack.",""],["Rules","Attack pools","Core Rules › Attacks","rules/attacks.html#attacks","Attack pools: Strength + Weaponry for melee weapons, Strength + Brawl for unarmed attacks, and Dexterity + Ranged for ranged and thrown weapons. Talents can change the attribute for some weapons. ",""],["Rules","Hits","Core Rules › Attacks","rules/attacks.html#attacks","Hits: Every attack is contested (see Defense and reactions). It hits if the attacker's successes equal or beat the defender's; a tie is a hit with 0 net successes, dealing the weapon's damage alone. ",""],["Rules","Damage","Core Rules › Attacks","rules/attacks.html#attacks","Damage: A hit deals damage equal to the net successes plus the weapon's damage, of the weapon's damage type. Armor reduces it (see Armor). ",""],["Rules","Opportunity on attacks","Core Rules › Attacks","rules/attacks.html#attacks","Opportunity on attacks is based on the attacker's total successes, not net successes. ",""],["Rules","Off-hand attack","Core Rules › Attacks","rules/attacks.html#attacks","Off-hand attack: while holding a Light weapon in your other hand, take 1 Stress to make one extra attack with it (1 action). This is an exception to the one-attack rule. ",""],["Rules","Extra attacks","Core Rules › Attacks","rules/attacks.html#attacks","Extra attacks: each attack after your first in a turn takes a cumulative −2d (−2d on the second, −4d on the third), whatever grants it. A single action that attacks several targets, such as Unstoppable Charge, counts as one attack. ",""],["Rules","Called shots","Core Rules › Attacks","rules/attacks.html#called-shots","Before rolling an attack, you can aim at a specific spot. Take the penalty, and on a hit, add the effect: Target Penalty Effect on a hit Arm or hand −1d The target drops what it's holding Leg −1d The target is Slowed Head −2d +1 damage Gap in armor −3d Ignore the target's armor rating; the 1-bashing floor still applies",""],["Rules","Grappling","Core Rules › Grappling","rules/attacks.html#grappling","Grapple (1 action, counts as your attack): start or act in a grapple. Starting a grapple: Roll Strength + Brawl, contested like any attack (Dodge, Parry or Defense). On a hit, you and the target are both Grappled. It deals no damage. Acting in a grapple: While Grappled, roll Strength + Brawl, contested by the opponent's Strength + Brawl. On a success, choose one move, or two on an Opportunity: Break free: you're no longer Grappled. Damage: deal bashing damage equal to your net successes, or lethal damage equal to your net successes + weapon damage if you control a Light weapon. Control weapon: take control of a weapon in the grapple, or draw your own Light weapon. Disarm: a weapon you control drops or is thrown away. Drop Prone: you both fall Prone. You can instead drop only the opponent and break free. Hold: until your next turn, the opponent can only take the Grapple action, to break free. Restrain: with rope, cuffs or a hold that locks, the opponent is Immobilized even after you let go. Requires Hold first. Take cover: until your next turn, ranged attacks against you hit the opponent instead.",""],["Rules","Grapple","Core Rules › Grappling","rules/attacks.html#grappling","Grapple (1 action, counts as your attack): start or act in a grapple. ",""],["Rules","Starting a grapple","Core Rules › Grappling","rules/attacks.html#grappling","Starting a grapple: Roll Strength + Brawl, contested like any attack (Dodge, Parry or Defense). On a hit, you and the target are both Grappled. It deals no damage. ",""],["Rules","Acting in a grapple","Core Rules › Grappling","rules/attacks.html#grappling","Acting in a grapple: While Grappled, roll Strength + Brawl, contested by the opponent's Strength + Brawl. On a success, choose one move, or two on an Opportunity: Break free: you're no longer Grappled. Damage: deal bashing damage equal to your net successes, or lethal damage equal to your net successes + weapon damage if you control a Light weapon. Control weapon: take control of a weapon in the grapple, or draw your own Light weapon. Disarm: a weapon you control drops or is thrown away. Drop Prone: you both fall Prone. You can instead drop only the opponent and break free. Hold: until your next turn, the opponent can only take the Grapple action, to break free. Restrain: with rope, cuffs or a hold that locks, the opponent is Immobilized even after you let go. Requires Hold first. Take cover: until your next turn, ranged attacks against you hit the opponent instead.",""],["Rules","Defense and reactions","Core Rules › Defense and reactions","rules/defense-and-reactions.html#defense-and-reactions","Defense = the lower of Dexterity and Wits. Defending: You contest an attack with a reaction (Dodge or Parry). If you can't or don't react, you still contest it by rolling your Defense. Dodge, Parry and rolled Defense are all defense rolls. Bonuses and penalties: an effect that changes your Defense itself (such as heavy armor's −1) changes every defense roll, twice over on a Dodge. Most bonuses instead add dice to a specific roll and are written that way: \"+1d to Parry\", \"+1d to Dodge\", or \"+1d to defense rolls\" (any of the three, added once). Dodge (reaction): Roll twice your Defense with the stakes die. A Complication leaves you Prone. An Opportunity lets you Disengage as a free action. Dodging spends your Defense: until the start of your next turn, you can't Parry or roll Defense against attacks, and attacks against you are uncontested. Parry (reaction, melee only): Roll Defense + Weaponry if you're holding a weapon, or Defense + Brawl if you're unarmed. Parrying an armed attack unarmed takes −2d. Graze: When an attack misses, take 1 Stress to deal 1 damage of one severity lower than the attack's (lethal becomes bashing, aggravated becomes lethal). Bashing attacks can't graze. Re",""],["Rules","Defense","Core Rules › Defense and reactions","rules/defense-and-reactions.html#defense-and-reactions","Defense = the lower of Dexterity and Wits. ",""],["Rules","Defending","Core Rules › Defense and reactions","rules/defense-and-reactions.html#defense-and-reactions","Defending: You contest an attack with a reaction (Dodge or Parry). If you can't or don't react, you still contest it by rolling your Defense. Dodge, Parry and rolled Defense are all defense rolls. ",""],["Rules","Bonuses and penalties","Core Rules › Defense and reactions","rules/defense-and-reactions.html#defense-and-reactions","Bonuses and penalties: an effect that changes your Defense itself (such as heavy armor's −1) changes every defense roll, twice over on a Dodge. Most bonuses instead add dice to a specific roll and are written that way: \"+1d to Parry\", \"+1d to Dodge\", or \"+1d to defense rolls\" (any of the three, added once). ",""],["Rules","Dodge","Core Rules › Defense and reactions","rules/defense-and-reactions.html#defense-and-reactions","Dodge (reaction): Roll twice your Defense with the stakes die. A Complication leaves you Prone. An Opportunity lets you Disengage as a free action. Dodging spends your Defense: until the start of your next turn, you can't Parry or roll Defense against attacks, and attacks against you are uncontested. ",""],["Rules","Parry","Core Rules › Defense and reactions","rules/defense-and-reactions.html#defense-and-reactions","Parry (reaction, melee only): Roll Defense + Weaponry if you're holding a weapon, or Defense + Brawl if you're unarmed. Parrying an armed attack unarmed takes −2d. ",""],["Rules","Graze","Core Rules › Defense and reactions","rules/defense-and-reactions.html#defense-and-reactions","Graze: When an attack misses, take 1 Stress to deal 1 damage of one severity lower than the attack's (lethal becomes bashing, aggravated becomes lethal). Bashing attacks can't graze. ",""],["Rules","Reactive Strike","Core Rules › Defense and reactions","rules/defense-and-reactions.html#defense-and-reactions","Reactive Strike (reaction): When an enemy leaves your melee reach, make a melee attack against them. ",""],["Rules","Feints and mental attacks","Core Rules › Defense and reactions","rules/defense-and-reactions.html#defense-and-reactions","Feints and mental attacks: Attacks contested by Intelligence + Resolve can't be Dodged or Parried, and the defender doesn't spend a reaction. ",""],["Rules","Armor","Core Rules › Armor","rules/armor-weapons-and-kits.html#armor","Armor rating: When an attack hits or grazes you, subtract your armor rating from the damage. Damage that gets through keeps its type. If armor absorbs all of it, you still take 1 bashing damage. Wear: Armor has a Wear track with boxes equal to its current rating. It takes 1 Wear when a hit gets past it (damage beyond the 1-bashing floor) or when the attack scores an Opportunity, at most 1 Wear per hit. You can also have your armor take 1 Wear to ignore one condition a hit would cause, such as Prone, Bleeding or Stunned, or being pushed back. Battered: When the Wear track fills, the armor's rating drops by 1. A full Wear track takes no more Wear from hits. You can still choose to have it take Wear to ignore a condition; each point that doesn't fit lowers the rating by 1 more. Prepare at a rest clears Wear. If a rest ends with the Wear track still full, the rating drops by 1 more. Lost rating comes back only when the armor is repaired during downtime. Broken: Armor reduced to rating 0 is broken. It gives no protection until repaired during downtime. Armor Rating Penalty Bulk Light (leather, padded coat) 1 None 1 Medium (mail, brigandine) 2 −1d to Stealth and Athletics 2 Heavy (plate)",""],["Rules","Armor rating","Core Rules › Armor","rules/armor-weapons-and-kits.html#armor","Armor rating: When an attack hits or grazes you, subtract your armor rating from the damage. Damage that gets through keeps its type. If armor absorbs all of it, you still take 1 bashing damage. ",""],["Rules","Wear","Core Rules › Armor","rules/armor-weapons-and-kits.html#armor","Wear: Armor has a Wear track with boxes equal to its current rating. It takes 1 Wear when a hit gets past it (damage beyond the 1-bashing floor) or when the attack scores an Opportunity, at most 1 Wear per hit. You can also have your armor take 1 Wear to ignore one condition a hit would cause, such as Prone, Bleeding or Stunned, or being pushed back. ",""],["Rules","Battered","Core Rules › Armor","rules/armor-weapons-and-kits.html#armor","Battered: When the Wear track fills, the armor's rating drops by 1. A full Wear track takes no more Wear from hits. You can still choose to have it take Wear to ignore a condition; each point that doesn't fit lowers the rating by 1 more. Prepare at a rest clears Wear. If a rest ends with the Wear track still full, the rating drops by 1 more. Lost rating comes back only when the armor is repaired during downtime. ",""],["Rules","Broken","Core Rules › Armor","rules/armor-weapons-and-kits.html#armor","Broken: Armor reduced to rating 0 is broken. It gives no protection until repaired during downtime. ",""],["Rules","Weapons","Core Rules › Weapons","rules/armor-weapons-and-kits.html#weapons","Each setting chooses which weapons exist. Weapon Damage Traits Bulk Unarmed 0 bashing — — Club 1 bashing Stunning 1 Sap 1 bashing Stunning, Discreet — Knife 1 lethal Light, Discreet, Throwable, Precise — Rapier 1 lethal Light, Piercing 1, Precise 1 Short sword, machete 2 lethal — 1 Long sword 2 lethal Versatile 1 Spear 2 lethal Reach, Two-handed 2 Warhammer 3 lethal Heavy, Knockdown 2 Greatsword 4 lethal Heavy, Two-handed 3 Maul, sledgehammer 4 lethal Heavy, Two-handed, Knockdown 3 Small shield 0 bashing Shield (1) 1 Large shield 1 bashing Shield (2), Heavy 2 Bow 2 lethal Range 30 m, Two-handed 2 Crossbow 3 lethal Range 30 m, Slow, Two-handed 2 Pistol 2 lethal Range 20 m 1 Rifle 4 lethal Range 60 m, Slow, Two-handed, Precise 2",""],["Rules","Weapon traits","Core Rules › Weapons","rules/armor-weapons-and-kits.html#weapon-traits","Deadly: An Opportunity on the attack adds +1 lethal damage. Discreet: When someone searches you, finding this item takes 1 more success than finding anything else (see Searching someone). Heavy: Needs Strength 3; −1d per missing dot. Knockdown: On an Opportunity, the target falls Prone. Light: Small and one-handed. Usable while Grappled. Piercing X: Ignores X points of armor rating. Precise: On called shots, 6s explode. Quickdraw: Drawing or stowing it is a free action. Range: Attacks beyond it take −2d, up to double the range. Reach: Attack a target up to 2 meters away. Shield (X): +X dice to Parry while held. With Shield (2) or higher, it also counts as cover against ranged attacks. Slow: Reloading takes 1 action. Stunning: On an Opportunity, the target is Stunned until the end of their next turn. Throwable: Can be thrown with Dexterity + Ranged, Range 10 m. Two-handed: Needs both hands, so you can't hold a shield, a second weapon or a light source while attacking with it. Versatile: Can be used one- or two-handed; +1 damage when used two-handed.",""],["Traits","Deadly","Core Rules › Weapon traits","rules/armor-weapons-and-kits.html#weapon-traits","Deadly: An Opportunity on the attack adds +1 lethal damage. ",""],["Traits","Discreet","Core Rules › Weapon traits","rules/armor-weapons-and-kits.html#weapon-traits","Discreet: When someone searches you, finding this item takes 1 more success than finding anything else (see Searching someone). ",""],["Traits","Heavy","Core Rules › Weapon traits","rules/armor-weapons-and-kits.html#weapon-traits","Heavy: Needs Strength 3; −1d per missing dot. ",""],["Traits","Knockdown","Core Rules › Weapon traits","rules/armor-weapons-and-kits.html#weapon-traits","Knockdown: On an Opportunity, the target falls Prone. ",""],["Traits","Light","Core Rules › Weapon traits","rules/armor-weapons-and-kits.html#weapon-traits","Light: Small and one-handed. Usable while Grappled. ",""],["Traits","Piercing X","Core Rules › Weapon traits","rules/armor-weapons-and-kits.html#weapon-traits","Piercing X: Ignores X points of armor rating. ",""],["Traits","Precise","Core Rules › Weapon traits","rules/armor-weapons-and-kits.html#weapon-traits","Precise: On called shots, 6s explode. ",""],["Traits","Quickdraw","Core Rules › Weapon traits","rules/armor-weapons-and-kits.html#weapon-traits","Quickdraw: Drawing or stowing it is a free action. ",""],["Traits","Range","Core Rules › Weapon traits","rules/armor-weapons-and-kits.html#weapon-traits","Range: Attacks beyond it take −2d, up to double the range. ",""],["Traits","Reach","Core Rules › Weapon traits","rules/armor-weapons-and-kits.html#weapon-traits","Reach: Attack a target up to 2 meters away. ",""],["Traits","Shield (X)","Core Rules › Weapon traits","rules/armor-weapons-and-kits.html#weapon-traits","Shield (X): +X dice to Parry while held. With Shield (2) or higher, it also counts as cover against ranged attacks. ",""],["Traits","Slow","Core Rules › Weapon traits","rules/armor-weapons-and-kits.html#weapon-traits","Slow: Reloading takes 1 action. ",""],["Traits","Stunning","Core Rules › Weapon traits","rules/armor-weapons-and-kits.html#weapon-traits","Stunning: On an Opportunity, the target is Stunned until the end of their next turn. ",""],["Traits","Throwable","Core Rules › Weapon traits","rules/armor-weapons-and-kits.html#weapon-traits","Throwable: Can be thrown with Dexterity + Ranged, Range 10 m. ",""],["Traits","Two-handed","Core Rules › Weapon traits","rules/armor-weapons-and-kits.html#weapon-traits","Two-handed: Needs both hands, so you can't hold a shield, a second weapon or a light source while attacking with it. ",""],["Traits","Versatile","Core Rules › Weapon traits","rules/armor-weapons-and-kits.html#weapon-traits","Versatile: Can be used one- or two-handed; +1 damage when used two-handed. ",""],["Rules","Kits","Core Rules › Kits","rules/armor-weapons-and-kits.html#kits","A kit is a set of tools for one kind of work. It gives +1d to the rolls it covers. Working with no proper tools at all gives −1d. Charges: a kit has 3 charges. Spend 1 each time you take its +1d. A Complication on that roll spends 1 more. Empty kit: at 0 charges you still have the tools (no −1d), but you get no bonus. Refilling: each Prepare at a rest refills 1 charge on each of your kits. In a settlement you can buy extra rest actions (see Rest) to Prepare again. A week of downtime refills every kit to full. Kit Covers Medical kit Medicine: First aid, Recover Tool kit Crafts: repairs, building Thieves' tools Thievery: locks, safes Climbing gear Athletics: climbing, rope work Survival kit Survival: camping, foraging, tracking Disguise kit Subterfuge: disguises Writing case Academics or Expression: records, correspondence, forgery Other consumables (ammunition, lamp oil, rations, bandages beyond a kit) aren't counted. If you've used Prepare, you have them; a Complication can still use them up.",""],["Rules","Charges","Core Rules › Kits","rules/armor-weapons-and-kits.html#kits","Charges: a kit has 3 charges. Spend 1 each time you take its +1d. A Complication on that roll spends 1 more. ",""],["Rules","Empty kit","Core Rules › Kits","rules/armor-weapons-and-kits.html#kits","Empty kit: at 0 charges you still have the tools (no −1d), but you get no bonus. ",""],["Rules","Refilling","Core Rules › Kits","rules/armor-weapons-and-kits.html#kits","Refilling: each Prepare at a rest refills 1 charge on each of your kits. In a settlement you can buy extra rest actions (see Rest) to Prepare again. A week of downtime refills every kit to full. ",""],["Rules","Encumbrance","Core Rules › Encumbrance","rules/armor-weapons-and-kits.html#encumbrance","Every item has a bulk from 1 to 3, or no bulk at all. Add up the bulk of everything you carry, including worn armor, to find your load. Bulk Examples None Papers, money in handfuls, clothing, a knife, a sap and other Discreet items 1 One-handed weapons, a pistol, a small shield, each kit, a lantern, light armor, a bag of wealth 2 Two-handed weapons, one-handed heavy weapons, bows, crossbows, rifles, a large shield, medium armor 3 Two-handed heavy weapons, heavy armor, a chest of wealth The weapon and armor tables list each item's bulk. Load limits depend on Strength: a light load is up to Strength + 1, a normal load up to twice that, and a heavy load up to three times that. Strength Light Normal Heavy 1 2 4 6 2 3 6 9 3 4 8 12 4 5 10 15 5 6 12 18 Light load: +1d to Athletics and Stealth. Normal load: no effect. Heavy load: −1d to Athletics and Stealth, −2 Speed, and you can't take fast turns. Each day of travel under a heavy load gives 1 stack of Exhausted. Over a heavy load: you can't take the Move action, only Disengage, until you drop something.",""],["Rules","Light load","Core Rules › Encumbrance","rules/armor-weapons-and-kits.html#encumbrance","Light load: +1d to Athletics and Stealth. ",""],["Rules","Normal load","Core Rules › Encumbrance","rules/armor-weapons-and-kits.html#encumbrance","Normal load: no effect. ",""],["Rules","Heavy load","Core Rules › Encumbrance","rules/armor-weapons-and-kits.html#encumbrance","Heavy load: −1d to Athletics and Stealth, −2 Speed, and you can't take fast turns. Each day of travel under a heavy load gives 1 stack of Exhausted. ",""],["Rules","Over a heavy load","Core Rules › Encumbrance","rules/armor-weapons-and-kits.html#encumbrance","Over a heavy load: you can't take the Move action, only Disengage, until you drop something. ",""],["Rules","NPCs","Core Rules › NPCs","rules/npcs-and-companions.html#npcs","Each NPC has a single rating from 1 to 5. They roll double the rating for things they're proficient in, the rating as-is for things they know, and a chance die for everything else. Enemy types can add modifiers. When a talent says a roll is \"contested by\" a pool (such as Intelligence + Resolve), an NPC rolls their rating by the rule above instead. Stress: Minions have none. Important NPCs have Stress equal to their rating. Bosses have double their rating. No Tenacity: NPCs have no Tenacity track, so their Stress doesn't overflow. An NPC with a full Stress track can't pay Stress costs. Minions count as having a full Stress track, so they can't pay Stress costs. Health: Important NPCs have Health equal to Size + rating. Bosses have custom Health, usually double an important NPC's. Minions have no Health track (below). Beaten Down: Important NPCs and bosses are Beaten Down when their total damage exceeds half their Health, rounded up. Taking out minions: Any lethal or aggravated damage takes a minion out: they're down and bleeding to death. Bashing damage takes a minion out only if a single hit deals bashing at least equal to its rating; smaller hits are shrugged off. A minion taken o",""],["Rules","Stress","Core Rules › NPCs","rules/npcs-and-companions.html#npcs","Stress: Minions have none. Important NPCs have Stress equal to their rating. Bosses have double their rating. ",""],["Rules","No Tenacity","Core Rules › NPCs","rules/npcs-and-companions.html#npcs","No Tenacity: NPCs have no Tenacity track, so their Stress doesn't overflow. An NPC with a full Stress track can't pay Stress costs. ",""],["Rules","Minions","Core Rules › NPCs","rules/npcs-and-companions.html#npcs","Minions count as having a full Stress track, so they can't pay Stress costs. ",""],["Rules","Health","Core Rules › NPCs","rules/npcs-and-companions.html#npcs","Health: Important NPCs have Health equal to Size + rating. Bosses have custom Health, usually double an important NPC's. Minions have no Health track (below). ",""],["Rules","Beaten Down","Core Rules › NPCs","rules/npcs-and-companions.html#npcs","Beaten Down: Important NPCs and bosses are Beaten Down when their total damage exceeds half their Health, rounded up. ",""],["Rules","Taking out minions","Core Rules › NPCs","rules/npcs-and-companions.html#npcs","Taking out minions: Any lethal or aggravated damage takes a minion out: they're down and bleeding to death. Bashing damage takes a minion out only if a single hit deals bashing at least equal to its rating; smaller hits are shrugged off. A minion taken out by bashing is unconscious, surrenders or flees, whichever fits the scene. ",""],["Rules","Defense","Core Rules › NPCs","rules/npcs-and-companions.html#npcs","Defense: Minions roll their rating to defend against attacks; they don't Dodge or Parry. Important NPCs and bosses have a Defense set by their type and use the same rules as PCs. ",""],["Rules","Armor","Core Rules › NPCs","rules/npcs-and-companions.html#npcs","Armor: A minion's armor is a static rating with no Wear track. Important NPCs and bosses track Wear like PCs. ",""],["Rules","Damage","Core Rules › NPCs","rules/npcs-and-companions.html#npcs","Damage: NPC attacks deal damage like PCs': net successes plus weapon damage. ",""],["Rules","Companions","Core Rules › Companions","rules/npcs-and-companions.html#companions","A companion is an animal that works with you. It's an NPC with a rating (1–5) and rolls like one: double its rating for what it's proficient in, its rating for what it knows, and a chance die for everything else. Health: Size + rating. It heals like a character; Recover on an animal uses Animal Ken instead of Medicine. No Stress: it counts as having a full Stress track, so it can't pay Stress costs. Defense: it rolls its rating to defend, like a minion. It doesn't Dodge or Parry. Acting: it has no turn of its own. On your turn, you can spend your own actions to have it act. Talents such as Animal Bond give it actions for free. Orders: outside a fight, unusual or dangerous orders need an Animal Ken roll. Getting one: Animal Bond gives a rating 2 animal of your choice: one from the table, or another animal you and the GM build at rating 2. Otherwise, a trained animal costs a bag. Companion Rating Size Health Speed Attack Proficient in Hound 2 4 6 13 Bite, 1 lethal Tracking, noticing, biting Wolfhound 3 5 8 12 Bite, 2 lethal Biting, tracking, intimidating Bird of prey 2 2 4 20, flying Talons, 1 lethal Noticing, scouting, harrying Horse 2 7 9 20 Kick, 2 bashing Running, carrying, kicki",""],["Rules","Health","Core Rules › Companions","rules/npcs-and-companions.html#companions","Health: Size + rating. It heals like a character; Recover on an animal uses Animal Ken instead of Medicine. ",""],["Rules","No Stress","Core Rules › Companions","rules/npcs-and-companions.html#companions","No Stress: it counts as having a full Stress track, so it can't pay Stress costs. ",""],["Rules","Defense","Core Rules › Companions","rules/npcs-and-companions.html#companions","Defense: it rolls its rating to defend, like a minion. It doesn't Dodge or Parry. ",""],["Rules","Acting","Core Rules › Companions","rules/npcs-and-companions.html#companions","Acting: it has no turn of its own. On your turn, you can spend your own actions to have it act. Talents such as Animal Bond give it actions for free. ",""],["Rules","Orders","Core Rules › Companions","rules/npcs-and-companions.html#companions","Orders: outside a fight, unusual or dangerous orders need an Animal Ken roll. ",""],["Rules","Getting one","Core Rules › Companions","rules/npcs-and-companions.html#companions","Getting one: Animal Bond gives a rating 2 animal of your choice: one from the table, or another animal you and the GM build at rating 2. Otherwise, a trained animal costs a bag. ",""],["Rules","Damage and Health","Core Rules › Damage and Health","rules/damage-and-health.html#damage-and-health","Health track: Size + Stamina boxes. Damage types: From least to most severe: bashing, lethal and aggravated. Filling the track: The track stays sorted by severity: aggravated damage fills from the left, then lethal, then bashing. New damage that is more severe pushes less severe damage to the right. Overflow: When your Health track is full, new bashing damage becomes lethal, and new lethal damage becomes aggravated. Last box: Bashing in your last Health box: you fall Unconscious. Lethal in your last Health box: you fall Unconscious and start Bleeding. Aggravated can only reach your last box once the whole track is aggravated: you die. Waking up: You wake from damage-caused unconsciousness when your last Health box clears, whether through natural healing or an ally's healing. At the start of your turn, unless you chose to Avoid Death, you can also push yourself to wake up instead of gaining +2d. Waking doesn't heal you: any damage still in your last box keeps affecting you (Bleeding continues), and one more point of damage knocks you out again. Beaten Down threshold: Stamina + Resolve. When your total damage, of any type, exceeds it, you are Beaten Down.",""],["Rules","Health track","Core Rules › Damage and Health","rules/damage-and-health.html#damage-and-health","Health track: Size + Stamina boxes. ",""],["Rules","Damage types","Core Rules › Damage and Health","rules/damage-and-health.html#damage-and-health","Damage types: From least to most severe: bashing, lethal and aggravated. ",""],["Rules","Filling the track","Core Rules › Damage and Health","rules/damage-and-health.html#damage-and-health","Filling the track: The track stays sorted by severity: aggravated damage fills from the left, then lethal, then bashing. New damage that is more severe pushes less severe damage to the right. ",""],["Rules","Overflow","Core Rules › Damage and Health","rules/damage-and-health.html#damage-and-health","Overflow: When your Health track is full, new bashing damage becomes lethal, and new lethal damage becomes aggravated. ",""],["Rules","Last box","Core Rules › Damage and Health","rules/damage-and-health.html#damage-and-health","Last box: Bashing in your last Health box: you fall Unconscious. Lethal in your last Health box: you fall Unconscious and start Bleeding. Aggravated can only reach your last box once the whole track is aggravated: you die.",""],["Rules","Waking up","Core Rules › Damage and Health","rules/damage-and-health.html#damage-and-health","Waking up: You wake from damage-caused unconsciousness when your last Health box clears, whether through natural healing or an ally's healing. At the start of your turn, unless you chose to Avoid Death, you can also push yourself to wake up instead of gaining +2d. Waking doesn't heal you: any damage still in your last box keeps affecting you (Bleeding continues), and one more point of damage knocks you out again. ",""],["Rules","Beaten Down threshold","Core Rules › Damage and Health","rules/damage-and-health.html#damage-and-health","Beaten Down threshold: Stamina + Resolve. When your total damage, of any type, exceeds it, you are Beaten Down. ",""],["Rules","Avoiding death","Core Rules › Avoiding death","rules/damage-and-health.html#avoiding-death","When damage knocks you Unconscious, you choose what happens next. Avoid Death: take a Death's Mark. You stop Bleeding, and further damage can't kill you while you're unconscious. You can't be targeted by attacks unless an opponent specifically targets unconscious creatures (a creature's description says so). You wake only when an ally heals 1 or more of your damage, or when the party finishes a rest. You can't push yourself to wake up: you've chosen to step out of the fight, and the only other way to act is a Blaze of Glory. If you don't avoid death, the normal rules apply: you may keep Bleeding, and further damage can kill you (see Waking up). Blaze of Glory: each time your turn comes while you're still unconscious, whether or not you avoided death, you can choose to go out in a Blaze of Glory. Take one turn as normal (fast or slow). Each roll you make counts as already having one success with Opportunity. At the end of the turn, you cross through the veil of death.",""],["Rules","Avoid Death","Core Rules › Avoiding death","rules/damage-and-health.html#avoiding-death","Avoid Death: take a Death's Mark. You stop Bleeding, and further damage can't kill you while you're unconscious. You can't be targeted by attacks unless an opponent specifically targets unconscious creatures (a creature's description says so). You wake only when an ally heals 1 or more of your damage, or when the party finishes a rest. You can't push yourself to wake up: you've chosen to step out of the fight, and the only other way to act is a Blaze of Glory. ",""],["Rules","If you don't avoid death,","Core Rules › Avoiding death","rules/damage-and-health.html#avoiding-death","If you don't avoid death, the normal rules apply: you may keep Bleeding, and further damage can kill you (see Waking up). ",""],["Rules","Blaze of Glory","Core Rules › Avoiding death","rules/damage-and-health.html#avoiding-death","Blaze of Glory: each time your turn comes while you're still unconscious, whether or not you avoided death, you can choose to go out in a Blaze of Glory. Take one turn as normal (fast or slow). Each roll you make counts as already having one success with Opportunity. At the end of the turn, you cross through the veil of death. ",""],["Rules","Scars","Core Rules › Scars","rules/damage-and-health.html#scars","A Scar is a lasting mark on a character. Scars don't heal with rest or downtime; only the story can remove one. Some Scars build up in stages and are written with their stage, Name N: for example, Duskworld's Dusking 2. Settings define these, including what each stage does and what happens at the last one. They're separate from Death's Marks and don't count toward their limit.",""],["Rules","Death's Marks","Core Rules › Scars","rules/damage-and-health.html#deaths-marks","Death's Marks are the Scars you take when you Avoid Death. Choose one of these penalties. Each Death's Mark must be a different one, so a character can avoid death at most three times; the fourth is always the last. Permanently reduce your Health by 1. Permanently reduce your Tenacity by 1. Your Stress track shrinks with it. Permanently reduce one attribute by 1. Derived values that use it change too. Cross through the veil of death. You die.",""],["Rules","Permanently reduce your Health by 1","Core Rules › Death's Marks","rules/damage-and-health.html#deaths-marks","Permanently reduce your Health by 1. ",""],["Rules","Permanently reduce your Tenacity by 1","Core Rules › Death's Marks","rules/damage-and-health.html#deaths-marks","Permanently reduce your Tenacity by 1. Your Stress track shrinks with it. ",""],["Rules","Permanently reduce one attribute by 1","Core Rules › Death's Marks","rules/damage-and-health.html#deaths-marks","Permanently reduce one attribute by 1. Derived values that use it change too. ",""],["Rules","Cross through the veil of death","Core Rules › Death's Marks","rules/damage-and-health.html#deaths-marks","Cross through the veil of death. You die. ",""],["Rules","Persistent damage","Core Rules › Persistent damage","rules/damage-and-health.html#persistent-damage","Persistent damage (such as Bleeding) is applied at the end of the affected character's turn, so they always get to act first. Endure (reaction): Before taking the damage, roll Stamina + Resolve. If the successes equal or exceed the number of rounds the effect has left, you take no damage that round. The effect still continues.",""],["Rules","Endure","Core Rules › Persistent damage","rules/damage-and-health.html#persistent-damage","Endure (reaction): Before taking the damage, roll Stamina + Resolve. If the successes equal or exceed the number of rounds the effect has left, you take no damage that round. The effect still continues. ",""],["Rules","Hazards","Core Rules › Hazards","rules/damage-and-health.html#hazards","Common dangers of the world. Settings add their own, such as Duskworld's Dusk exposure. Armor doesn't reduce hazard damage. Hazard Effect Falling 1 bashing damage per 3 meters fallen, or lethal if you land on something sharp. Roll Dexterity + Athletics as you fall (no action): each success reduces the damage by 1. A fall of 30 meters or more is fatal unless something breaks it, such as deep water or a canopy. Fire Lethal damage at the end of each of your turns in it: 1 for a torch or small fire, 2 for a bonfire, 3 for a burning building or worse. Drowning and suffocation You can hold your breath for rounds equal to 5 × your Stamina. After that, take 1 lethal damage at the end of each of your turns until you can breathe. Extreme cold or heat Without protection, after hours equal to your Stamina, take 1 bashing damage each hour, or 1 lethal in severe conditions (a blizzard, a desert at noon). Poison A poison has a rating from 1 to 5. It deals 1 lethal damage each hour (each round for a fast poison) for intervals equal to its rating. Before each, roll Stamina + Resolve: with successes equal to or above its rating, you take no damage that interval. Hunger, thirst and sleeplessness A da",""],["Rules","Natural healing","Core Rules › Natural healing","rules/damage-and-health.html#natural-healing","Wounds heal one at a time, least severe first. Damage Heals Bashing 1 per hour Lethal 1 per rest Aggravated 1 per week of downtime Longer stretches of free time use the Downtime rules: a week of downtime heals all bashing and lethal damage, and 1 aggravated wound.",""],["Rules","Rest","Core Rules › Rest","rules/rest.html#rest","A rest is a night's sleep and the free time around it, or a few hours of real relaxation, usually once per day. At the end of a rest, every character: clears 1 Stress, heals all bashing damage and 1 lethal wound (natural healing), removes 1 stack of Exhausted, refreshes their once-per-day abilities. Each character also takes two rest actions. You can take the same action more than once, but each character can receive Recover only once per rest. In a settlement, a character can pay 2 handfuls for each extra rest action. Recover: Roll Intelligence + Medicine on a patient. On a success, they heal 1 lethal wound. On a success with Opportunity, they heal 2 lethal wounds instead, or 1 aggravated wound becomes lethal. You can treat yourself, but if you're Beaten Down, you take 1 Stress. Prepare: Replenish your consumables, clear all your armor's Wear, and refill 1 charge on each of your kits. Broken gear and lost armor rating must be repaired during downtime. Respite: Make a recovery roll (Resolve + Composure). Clear 1 extra Stress per success when the rest ends. Forage: Roll Wits + Survival. Each success supplies one character for the day. On a success with Opportunity, you also find som",""],["Rules","Recover","Core Rules › Rest","rules/rest.html#rest","Recover: Roll Intelligence + Medicine on a patient. On a success, they heal 1 lethal wound. On a success with Opportunity, they heal 2 lethal wounds instead, or 1 aggravated wound becomes lethal. You can treat yourself, but if you're Beaten Down, you take 1 Stress. ",""],["Rules","Prepare","Core Rules › Rest","rules/rest.html#rest","Prepare: Replenish your consumables, clear all your armor's Wear, and refill 1 charge on each of your kits. Broken gear and lost armor rating must be repaired during downtime. ",""],["Rules","Respite","Core Rules › Rest","rules/rest.html#rest","Respite: Make a recovery roll (Resolve + Composure). Clear 1 extra Stress per success when the rest ends. ",""],["Rules","Forage","Core Rules › Rest","rules/rest.html#rest","Forage: Roll Wits + Survival. Each success supplies one character for the day. On a success with Opportunity, you also find something useful, such as herbs that give +1d to the next Recover. ",""],["Rules","Scout","Core Rules › Rest","rules/rest.html#rest","Scout: Roll Wits + Investigation or Stealth. On a success, learn one fact about the area ahead. On a success with Opportunity, the party can't be Surprised by the first threat they meet in that area. ",""],["Rules","Conditions","Core Rules › Conditions","rules/conditions.html#conditions","A condition doesn't stack unless it says so (Exhausted does). Gaining a condition you already have keeps whichever duration is longer. Condition Effect Beaten Down Making an attack costs 1 Stress, unless you have no chance to surrender (the enemy won't or can't accept it, such as a mindless beast). If you surrender, clear 1 Stress. Ends when your total damage drops to your Beaten Down threshold or below. Bleeding Persistent damage: 1 lethal damage at the end of each of your turns, until the duration runs out or the wound is treated (with First aid, Field Medicine or Avoid Death). Blinded Rolls that rely on sight use a chance die. Attacking or acting against a target you can hear, such as an adjacent enemy, takes −3d instead, as in darkness. You can't Dodge, and your other defense rolls take −2d. Deafened Rolls that rely on hearing use a chance die, and you take −1d to notice anything around you. You can't benefit from anything that needs you to hear someone, such as a shouted order or a command, unless you can see them. Determined Your next Stress cost is reduced by 1. Disoriented You can't use reactions. Exhausted −1d to all rolls. Exhausted can stack, giving −1d for each stack. W",""],["Rules","Steps","Character Creation › Steps","character-creation.html#steps","Race and heritage: gain two racial traits. Community: gain the key trait of the community you grew up in, and put 1 skill dot in one of its skills. Vocation: gain the vocation's key talent, and put 1 skill dot in one of the vocation's skills. Attributes: distribute attribute dots. Skills, specialties and a free pick: spend the rest of your skill dots, choose three specialties, then take one extra talent or community trait. Derived values and gear: work out Health, Tenacity, Stress, Defense and the rest, and pick starting equipment. At the end, a starting character has 2 racial traits, 1 community trait (the key trait), 1 talent (the vocation's key talent), three specialties, and one free pick (a talent or a community trait).",""],["Rules","Race and heritage","Character Creation › Steps","character-creation.html#steps","Race and heritage: gain two racial traits. ",""],["Rules","Community","Character Creation › Steps","character-creation.html#steps","Community: gain the key trait of the community you grew up in, and put 1 skill dot in one of its skills. ",""],["Rules","Vocation","Character Creation › Steps","character-creation.html#steps","Vocation: gain the vocation's key talent, and put 1 skill dot in one of the vocation's skills. ",""],["Rules","Attributes","Character Creation › Steps","character-creation.html#steps","Attributes: distribute attribute dots. ",""],["Rules","Skills, specialties and a free pick","Character Creation › Steps","character-creation.html#steps","Skills, specialties and a free pick: spend the rest of your skill dots, choose three specialties, then take one extra talent or community trait. ",""],["Rules","Derived values and gear","Character Creation › Steps","character-creation.html#steps","Derived values and gear: work out Health, Tenacity, Stress, Defense and the rest, and pick starting equipment. ",""],["Rules","1. Race and heritage","Character Creation › 1. Race and heritage","character-creation.html#1-race-and-heritage","Your race is what you are. Your heritage is another bloodline in you: a parent of a different race, or old ancestry that has resurfaced generations later. Each race has racial traits: a primary trait and usually a secondary trait. Choose a race and take its primary trait. Then choose another or the same race as your heritage and take its secondary trait. Some races have only a primary trait. Their blood is dominant: anyone with that bloodline is of that race. They can be chosen as a race but not as a heritage, so a character of that race always takes a different race as their heritage. Settings add their own races. The core rules include humans.",""],["Rules","Human","Character Creation › 1. Race and heritage","character-creation.html#human","Primary: Driven. When you push yourself, you gain +3d instead of +2d. Secondary: Fortune's Favor. You can raise the stakes on your own rolls twice per day instead of once.",""],["Traits","Driven","Character Creation › Human","character-creation.html#human","Primary: Driven. When you push yourself, you gain +3d instead of +2d. ",""],["Traits","Fortune's Favor","Character Creation › Human","character-creation.html#human","Secondary: Fortune's Favor. You can raise the stakes on your own rolls twice per day instead of once. ",""],["Rules","2. Community","Character Creation › 2. Community","character-creation.html#2-community","A community is a group of people you belong to: where you live, who looks out for you, and what that life teaches. At character creation, you choose the community you grew up in. Communities are defined by each setting. Each community is a small tree of community traits, read like a vocation's talent tree (see Talents): Key trait: every member of that community gains it. Further traits: one or two more, each with an after tag and other prerequisites (skills, attributes, race). At character creation, you gain your community's key trait. Its further traits come later through advancement, or you can take one with your free pick in step 5. Community skills: each community lists two or three skills. Put 1 skill dot in one of them. Key traits are about as strong as a secondary racial trait, and lean toward social and story hooks. A further trait costs the same as a talent, at creation and in advancement.",""],["Rules","Key trait","Character Creation › 2. Community","character-creation.html#2-community","Key trait: every member of that community gains it. ",""],["Rules","Further traits","Character Creation › 2. Community","character-creation.html#2-community","Further traits: one or two more, each with an after tag and other prerequisites (skills, attributes, race). ",""],["Rules","At character creation","Character Creation › 2. Community","character-creation.html#2-community","At character creation, you gain your community's key trait. Its further traits come later through advancement, or you can take one with your free pick in step 5. ",""],["Rules","Community skills","Character Creation › 2. Community","character-creation.html#2-community","Community skills: each community lists two or three skills. Put 1 skill dot in one of them. ",""],["Rules","Joining another community","Character Creation › 2. Community","character-creation.html#joining-another-community","Characters can join other communities during play, the way they can train into another vocation. Gain its key trait through advancement, at the cost of a talent. After that, its further traits are open to you like your own community's. No skill dot. The community skill dot is only for the community you grew up in. You keep your first community. Joining a new one adds to who you are; it doesn't replace it. The cost is the only requirement, but joining a community should mean something in the story. GMs are encouraged to work with the player on an in-world reason and a chance to roleplay it: a season travelling with a caravan, a debt repaid to a guild, a winter sheltered on a Frontier farmstead. A character can also start play as a member of a second community, using the free pick in step 5. Their backstory covers how they came to belong to it.",""],["Rules","Gain its key trait","Character Creation › Joining another community","character-creation.html#joining-another-community","Gain its key trait through advancement, at the cost of a talent. After that, its further traits are open to you like your own community's. ",""],["Rules","No skill dot","Character Creation › Joining another community","character-creation.html#joining-another-community","No skill dot. The community skill dot is only for the community you grew up in. ",""],["Rules","You keep your first community","Character Creation › Joining another community","character-creation.html#joining-another-community","You keep your first community. Joining a new one adds to who you are; it doesn't replace it. ",""],["Rules","3. Vocation","Character Creation › 3. Vocation","character-creation.html#3-vocation","Your vocation is what you do: your calling and your training. Vocations are in the core rules: Agent, Envoy, Hunter, Leader, Scholar and Warrior. Gain your vocation's key talent. Other talents come through advancement, or with your free pick in step 5. Vocation skills: each vocation lists three skills. Put 1 skill dot in one of them.",""],["Rules","Vocation skills","Character Creation › 3. Vocation","character-creation.html#3-vocation","Vocation skills: each vocation lists three skills. Put 1 skill dot in one of them. ",""],["Rules","4. Attributes","Character Creation › 4. Attributes","character-creation.html#4-attributes","All nine attributes start at 1. Rank the Mental, Physical and Social categories. Put 5 dots in the first, 4 in the second and 3 in the third. Cap at creation: no attribute can have more than 4 dots. A 5th dot comes only through advancement.",""],["Rules","Cap at creation","Character Creation › 4. Attributes","character-creation.html#4-attributes","Cap at creation: no attribute can have more than 4 dots. A 5th dot comes only through advancement. ",""],["Rules","Skills","Character Creation › 5. Skills, specialties and a free pick","character-creation.html#skills","You have 22 skill dots, split by category: rank Mental, Physical and Social, and put 11, 7 and 4 dots in them. The community and vocation dots from steps 2 and 3 are part of these 22. Each comes out of the pool for its skill's category, so you have 20 left to spend freely. The community and vocation dots can go in the same skill. Cap at creation: no skill can have more than 3 permanent dots. Higher ratings come through advancement. Temporary dots from talents (such as the Scholar's Erudition) don't count toward the cap, so they can take a skill above 3.",""],["Rules","Cap at creation","Character Creation › Skills","character-creation.html#skills","Cap at creation: no skill can have more than 3 permanent dots. Higher ratings come through advancement. ",""],["Rules","Temporary dots","Character Creation › Skills","character-creation.html#skills","Temporary dots from talents (such as the Scholar's Erudition) don't count toward the cap, so they can take a skill above 3. ",""],["Rules","Specialties","Character Creation › 5. Skills, specialties and a free pick","character-creation.html#specialties","Choose three specialties (see Specialties for the rules and examples): One from your community: in the skill where you put your community dot. One from your vocation: in the skill where you put your vocation dot. One free: in any skill with at least 1 permanent dot. If your community and vocation dots went in the same skill, take two different specialties in it. All three start at +1d; specialties can only be improved through advancement.",""],["Rules","One from your community","Character Creation › Specialties","character-creation.html#specialties","One from your community: in the skill where you put your community dot. ",""],["Rules","One from your vocation","Character Creation › Specialties","character-creation.html#specialties","One from your vocation: in the skill where you put your vocation dot. ",""],["Rules","One free","Character Creation › Specialties","character-creation.html#specialties","One free: in any skill with at least 1 permanent dot. ",""],["Rules","Free pick","Character Creation › 5. Skills, specialties and a free pick","character-creation.html#free-pick","Once your skills are set, take one of the following. Its prerequisites must be met. Another talent from your vocation, to go further along one of its trees. A second vocation's key talent, to start play with two vocations. A further trait of your community. A second community's key trait, to start play as a member of two communities. Your backstory explains how you came to belong to it. You don't get its skill dot. The free pick comes after attributes and skills, so a talent or trait that requires either can be taken as long as your final ratings meet it. With the skill cap at 3, talents that require a skill of 4+ can't be taken at creation.",""],["Rules","Another talent","Character Creation › Free pick","character-creation.html#free-pick","Another talent from your vocation, to go further along one of its trees. ",""],["Rules","A second vocation's key talent","Character Creation › Free pick","character-creation.html#free-pick","A second vocation's key talent, to start play with two vocations. ",""],["Rules","A further trait","Character Creation › Free pick","character-creation.html#free-pick","A further trait of your community. ",""],["Rules","A second community's key trait","Character Creation › Free pick","character-creation.html#free-pick","A second community's key trait, to start play as a member of two communities. Your backstory explains how you came to belong to it. You don't get its skill dot. ",""],["Rules","6. Derived values and gear","Character Creation › 6. Derived values and gear","character-creation.html#6-derived-values-and-gear","Value Formula Size 5 for an adult of any core race. Races or effects can change it. Health Size + Stamina Tenacity Resolve + Composure + 3 Stress Current Tenacity + 2 Defense The lower of Dexterity and Wits Beaten Down threshold Stamina + Resolve Speed Strength + Dexterity + Size + species factor, in meters Speed is how far you can move with one move action. The species factor is 0 by default; a setting can give a race or creature a different one (for example, +2 for a fast runner, −2 for a slow one). Because Size is part of it, smaller characters are slower and larger ones are faster.",""],["Rules","Starting gear","Character Creation › 6. Derived values and gear","character-creation.html#starting-gear","You start with: Your community's gear: clothes, everyday items and anything the setting ties to it (listed with each community). Your vocation's gear: weapons, armor and a kit (listed with each vocation). 2 handfuls of wealth (see Wealth). Only the first vocation and the community you grew up in give gear; a second vocation or community from the free pick doesn't. Settings can add rules about who may carry what (Duskworld: firearms).",""],["Rules","Your community's gear","Character Creation › Starting gear","character-creation.html#starting-gear","Your community's gear: clothes, everyday items and anything the setting ties to it (listed with each community). ",""],["Rules","Your vocation's gear","Character Creation › Starting gear","character-creation.html#starting-gear","Your vocation's gear: weapons, armor and a kit (listed with each vocation). ",""],["Rules","2 handfuls","Character Creation › Starting gear","character-creation.html#starting-gear","2 handfuls of wealth (see Wealth). ",""],["Rules","Wealth","Character Creation › Wealth","character-creation.html#wealth","Money is abstract: nobody counts coins. Wealth comes in three units, each worth 10 of the one below it. Unit Worth What it buys Handful — A night at an inn, a bribe to a clerk, a set of tools Bag 10 handfuls A good weapon, light or medium armor, passage with a caravan, a serious bribe Chest 10 bags Heavy armor, a horse and wagon, a favor from the powerful Each setting names the units to fit its world (Duskworld: handfuls of boxings, slabs and shells; see the Duskworld Overview). The rules always use handful, bag and chest. On your sheet: 9 handful boxes, 9 bag boxes, and a count of chests. When you'd mark a 10th handful, clear your handfuls and mark a bag instead; bags roll up into a chest the same way. Paying: clear the boxes for the price. If you don't have enough of a smaller unit, break a bigger one: clear 1 bag and mark 10 handfuls, then pay. Small purchases: anything worth less than a handful (a meal, a drink, a fare) isn't tracked. Selling: you get about half the price back, at the GM's call. Restocking: Prepare restocks consumables for free. In a settlement, each extra rest action costs 2 handfuls, so you can Prepare again to refill more kit charges (see Rest). Rewards: an ",""],["Rules","On your sheet","Character Creation › Wealth","character-creation.html#wealth","On your sheet: 9 handful boxes, 9 bag boxes, and a count of chests. When you'd mark a 10th handful, clear your handfuls and mark a bag instead; bags roll up into a chest the same way. ",""],["Rules","Paying","Character Creation › Wealth","character-creation.html#wealth","Paying: clear the boxes for the price. If you don't have enough of a smaller unit, break a bigger one: clear 1 bag and mark 10 handfuls, then pay. ",""],["Rules","Small purchases","Character Creation › Wealth","character-creation.html#wealth","Small purchases: anything worth less than a handful (a meal, a drink, a fare) isn't tracked. ",""],["Rules","Selling","Character Creation › Wealth","character-creation.html#wealth","Selling: you get about half the price back, at the GM's call. ",""],["Rules","Restocking","Character Creation › Wealth","character-creation.html#wealth","Restocking: Prepare restocks consumables for free. In a settlement, each extra rest action costs 2 handfuls, so you can Prepare again to refill more kit charges (see Rest). ",""],["Rules","Rewards","Character Creation › Wealth","character-creation.html#wealth","Rewards: an ordinary job pays a handful or a few; a big job pays a bag. Chests are story rewards, not wages. ",""],["Talents","Opportunist","Agent › Key talent","vocations/agent.html#key-talent","Once per round, reroll your stakes die.",""],["Rules","Agent","Vocations","vocations/agent.html","Agents live by their wits and their luck: investigators, spies and thieves who turn risk into opportunity. Their key talent lets them reroll the stakes die, so they thrive when the stakes are high.",""],["Talents","Rousing Presence","Envoy › Key talent","vocations/envoy.html#key-talent","1 action · Take 1 Stress. An ally near you becomes Inspired for one hour.",""],["Rules","Envoy","Vocations","vocations/envoy.html","Envoys inspire, steady and protect the people around them, and turn words into weapons against their enemies. Their key talent makes allies Inspired, so the whole party fails less often.",""],["Talents","Seek Quarry","Hunter › Key talent","vocations/hunter.html#key-talent","1 action · Choose a character you can see, or whose trail or traces you've found, as your quarry. You get +1d on rolls to find, attack or study them. They stay your quarry until you choose another.",""],["Rules","Hunter","Vocations","vocations/hunter.html","Hunters track, study and bring down a chosen quarry. Patient and precise, they're as comfortable in the wild as in a crowded street, as long as they know who they're after.",""],["Talents","Decisive Command","Leader › Key talent","vocations/leader.html#key-talent","1 action · Take 1 Stress. An ally close to you adds your command dice to their next roll.",""],["Rules","Leader","Vocations","vocations/leader.html","Leaders make everyone around them better. Their command dice add to their allies' rolls, and their talents give the party extra actions, protection and direction.",""],["Talents","Erudition","Scholar › Key talent","vocations/scholar.html#key-talent","Gain a temporary specialty and +1 dot in two Mental skills. Reassign them at the end of a rest with access to research (a library, archives, the internet).",""],["Rules","Scholar","Vocations","vocations/scholar.html","Scholars treat knowledge as a tool. Their key talent, Erudition, gives them expertise they can reassign after a rest with access to research, so they can prepare for whatever comes next.",""],["Talents","Vigilant Stance","Warrior › Key talent","vocations/warrior.html#key-talent","1 action · Enter Vigilant Stance. While in it, your first extra reaction each round costs 1 Stress instead of 2 when used to Parry or make a Reactive Strike, and you can enter other stances as a free action.",""],["Rules","Warrior","Vocations","vocations/warrior.html","Masters of weapons and fighting stances, warriors are at their best in the thick of melee. They hold the line, trade blows with the toughest enemies, and make it costly for anyone to get past them.",""],["Talents","Dreamer's Blood","Dreamshaper › Key talent","vocations/dreamshaper.html#key-talent","1 action · Gain the Dreamshaping power skill and a Reverie pool. Waking Dream: spend 1 Reverie to create a brief image or sound no bigger than you, or a small object with no bulk and no moving parts that's plainly unreal to the touch. It lasts until the end of your next turn. Waking Dream is a nudge, not a full dreamcraft: you don't roll.","duskworld"],["Rules","Dreamshaper","Vocations","vocations/dreamshaper.html","Some kehars carry the blood of the First Dreamers and can reach into the Everdream, the dream that dreamed the world, to make their own dreams real. Their gift is Dreamshaping, and the effects they create are called dreamcrafts.","duskworld"],["Rules","Dreamcrafts","Dreamshaper › Dreamcrafts","vocations/dreamshaper.html#dreamcrafts","Power skill: Dreamshaping. Each dreamcraft names the attribute it's rolled with. Reverie: the pool is 2 + the higher of Wits or Presence. It refills at the end of a rest only if you slept, and at the end of a week of downtime. When it's empty, you can pay with 2 Stress per point, like any power pool. Reverie paid for a dreamcraft that's still active stays reserved until it ends. Always a gamble: every dreamcraft roll raises the stakes.","duskworld"],["Rules","Power skill","Dreamshaper › Dreamcrafts","vocations/dreamshaper.html#dreamcrafts","Power skill: Dreamshaping. Each dreamcraft names the attribute it's rolled with. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Reverie","Dreamshaper › Dreamcrafts","vocations/dreamshaper.html#dreamcrafts","Reverie: the pool is 2 + the higher of Wits or Presence. It refills at the end of a rest only if you slept, and at the end of a week of downtime. When it's empty, you can pay with 2 Stress per point, like any power pool. Reverie paid for a dreamcraft that's still active stays reserved until it ends. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Always a gamble","Dreamshaper › Dreamcrafts","vocations/dreamshaper.html#dreamcrafts","Always a gamble: every dreamcraft roll raises the stakes. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Control","Dreamshaper › Dreamcrafts","vocations/dreamshaper.html#control","The roll decides what the dreamcraft costs, not just whether it works. Result Effect Reverie cost Success with Opportunity Works Half, rounded up Success Works Full Failure with Opportunity Doesn't work None: the Everdream lets you go cleanly Failure Doesn't work 1 Failure with Complication Doesn't work Half, rounded up, and a dream phenomenon breaks loose A Complication on a successful dreamcraft is an ordinary setback, chosen by the GM.","duskworld"],["Rules","Materializing","Dreamshaper › Dreamcrafts","vocations/dreamshaper.html#materializing","A dreamcraft can be cast in the moment or materialized slowly. The time you take sets the unit it's measured in: it lasts one unit per success. Way Time Unit Or instead In the moment The actions it lists Rounds — Meditation 1 minute Minutes +1d, lasting rounds Deep meditation 1 hour Hours +2d, lasting rounds Dreamed A night's sleep Days +3d, lasting rounds Never quick: casting a dreamcraft in the moment takes at least 2 actions. Shaping a dream is slower than swinging a sword. Longer or stronger: when you materialize slowly, choose either the longer unit or the bonus dice. A dreamcraft with an instant effect always takes the bonus. Sustaining: when a dreamcraft would end, you can pay 1 Reverie to keep it for one more unit, as often as you like. That Reverie is spent as usual; only the dreamcraft's initial cost stays reserved. Dreamed dreamcrafts are paid when you wake, from the Reverie the rest just refilled, and stay reserved for as long as they last: a Dreamveil kept over a hideout for days costs its dreamshaper that much Reverie every day. Some dreamcrafts can only be dreamed, or only be dreamed or deep-meditated.","duskworld"],["Rules","Never quick","Dreamshaper › Materializing","vocations/dreamshaper.html#materializing","Never quick: casting a dreamcraft in the moment takes at least 2 actions. Shaping a dream is slower than swinging a sword. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Longer or stronger","Dreamshaper › Materializing","vocations/dreamshaper.html#materializing","Longer or stronger: when you materialize slowly, choose either the longer unit or the bonus dice. A dreamcraft with an instant effect always takes the bonus. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Sustaining","Dreamshaper › Materializing","vocations/dreamshaper.html#materializing","Sustaining: when a dreamcraft would end, you can pay 1 Reverie to keep it for one more unit, as often as you like. That Reverie is spent as usual; only the dreamcraft's initial cost stays reserved. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Dreamed dreamcrafts","Dreamshaper › Materializing","vocations/dreamshaper.html#materializing","Dreamed dreamcrafts are paid when you wake, from the Reverie the rest just refilled, and stay reserved for as long as they last: a Dreamveil kept over a hideout for days costs its dreamshaper that much Reverie every day. Some dreamcrafts can only be dreamed, or only be dreamed or deep-meditated. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Phenomena, nightmares and nexuses","Dreamshaper › Dreamcrafts","vocations/dreamshaper.html#phenomena-nightmares-and-nexuses","Dream phenomena: odd breezes, strange noises, objects moving on their own, a sudden chill, runetech flickering out for a few minutes, small things from the Everdream appearing or vanishing. The GM chooses, scaled to the dreamcraft. Nightmares: in Heavy or Deep Dusk exposure, any Complication on a dreamcraft roll turns it into a nightmare, even on a success. The GM twists the effect against you or your allies. Dream Nexuses: some places touch the Everdream. A minor nexus gives +1d to dreamcraft rolls, a major one +2d. At a nexus, any Complication also releases a phenomenon.","duskworld"],["Rules","Dream phenomena","Dreamshaper › Phenomena, nightmares and nexuses","vocations/dreamshaper.html#phenomena-nightmares-and-nexuses","Dream phenomena: odd breezes, strange noises, objects moving on their own, a sudden chill, runetech flickering out for a few minutes, small things from the Everdream appearing or vanishing. The GM chooses, scaled to the dreamcraft. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Nightmares","Dreamshaper › Phenomena, nightmares and nexuses","vocations/dreamshaper.html#phenomena-nightmares-and-nexuses","Nightmares: in Heavy or Deep Dusk exposure, any Complication on a dreamcraft roll turns it into a nightmare, even on a success. The GM twists the effect against you or your allies. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Dream Nexuses","Dreamshaper › Phenomena, nightmares and nexuses","vocations/dreamshaper.html#phenomena-nightmares-and-nexuses","Dream Nexuses: some places touch the Everdream. A minor nexus gives +1d to dreamcraft rolls, a major one +2d. At a nexus, any Complication also releases a phenomenon. ","duskworld"],["Talents","Watchful Eye","Agent › Investigator","vocations/agent.html#investigator-watchful-eye","reaction · Investigation 2+ · Use Opportunist on the stakes die of a willing ally close to you. This counts as your use of Opportunist this round.",""],["Talents","Get 'Em Talking","Agent › Investigator","vocations/agent.html#investigator-get-em-talking","2 actions · Empathy 3+ · Take 1 Stress and roll Wits + Investigation, contested by Wits + Composure, to learn the target's motivation. For the next hour, you can raise the stakes on rolls that leverage it.",""],["Talents","Quick Analysis","Agent › Investigator","vocations/agent.html#investigator-quick-analysis","free action · Investigation 3+ · Once per round, take 2 Stress to gain 2 extra actions this turn, usable only for Mental rolls to use a skill, take a set-up action or use an Agent talent that isn't an attack. These can repeat actions you've already taken, but set-ups against the same target don't stack.",""],["Talents","Baleful","Agent › Investigator","vocations/agent.html#investigator-baleful","passive · When you win a contested Mental or Social roll against a target, they also take 1 Stress.",""],["Talents","Gather Evidence","Agent › Investigator","vocations/agent.html#investigator-gather-evidence","Empathy 3+ · Gain an Academics (Law) specialty. Once per round, when you succeed on a Mental roll against a target, you become Determined.",""],["Talents","Hardy","Agent › Investigator","vocations/agent.html#investigator-hardy","passive · +2 Health.",""],["Talents","Sleuth's Instincts","Agent › Investigator","vocations/agent.html#investigator-sleuths-instincts","Investigation 4+ · +1d on Mental rolls against characters whose motivation you know. You know when those characters lie to you.",""],["Talents","Close the Case","Agent › Investigator","vocations/agent.html#investigator-close-the-case","3 actions · Investigation 4+ · Take 3 Stress and roll Intelligence + Investigation, contested by Intelligence + Resolve, with +1d if you know the target's motivation. On a success, they must answer one question truthfully or concede the point publicly, their choice, and they take −2d on rolls against you for one hour. On a failure, they get +1d on their next roll against you.",""],["Talents","Sure Outcome","Agent › Spy","vocations/agent.html#spy-sure-outcome","Empathy 3+ · When you use Opportunist, take 2 Stress to turn an Opportunity into a Complication that counts as 2 successes instead of 1, or turn any Complication into an Opportunity.",""],["Talents","Plausible Excuse","Agent › Spy","vocations/agent.html#spy-plausible-excuse","reaction · Subterfuge 2+ · Gain a Thievery (Sleight of Hand) specialty. When you're caught skulking, take 2 Stress to feign innocence.",""],["Talents","Collected","Agent › Spy","vocations/agent.html#spy-collected","passive · +1d to resist Mental and Social rolls.",""],["Talents","Cover Story","Agent › Spy","vocations/agent.html#spy-cover-story","passive · Gain a false identity and a Socialize specialty in its culture or group.",""],["Talents","Subtle Takedown","Agent › Spy","vocations/agent.html#spy-subtle-takedown","2 actions · Empathy 4+ · Make an unarmed attack with Dexterity + Empathy against an unsuspecting target, raising the stakes. On a hit, you and the target are Grappled (see Grappling), and while you hold them, they can't speak or call out.",""],["Talents","Mighty","Agent › Spy","vocations/agent.html#spy-mighty","passive · When you hit with a weapon or unarmed attack, deal +1 damage per action spent on the attack.",""],["Talents","Mercurial Facade","Agent › Spy","vocations/agent.html#spy-mercurial-facade","Subterfuge 4+ · Disguise yourself with Subterfuge, without physical supplies. The first character to see through the disguise is Surprised.",""],["Talents","High Society Contacts","Agent › Spy","vocations/agent.html#spy-high-society-contacts","patron in high society · Gain a Socialize (High Society) specialty. Take 2 Stress to gain an Opportunity on a roll to interact in high society.",""],["Talents","Risky Behavior","Agent › Thief","vocations/agent.html#thief-risky-behavior","Empathy 3+ · Take 1 Stress to raise the stakes on your roll.",""],["Talents","Cheap Shot","Agent › Thief","vocations/agent.html#thief-cheap-shot","1 action · Take 1 Stress and make an unarmed attack with Dexterity + Thievery, raising the stakes. On a hit, the target is Disoriented until the end of their next turn; on an Opportunity, they're Stunned instead.",""],["Talents","Double Down","Agent › Thief","vocations/agent.html#thief-double-down","You can use Opportunist a second time on the same roll. This is an exception to the one-reroll rule. If that reroll shows a Complication, take 2 Stress.",""],["Talents","Surefooted","Agent › Thief","vocations/agent.html#thief-surefooted","passive · +3 Speed. Reduce damage from falls and dangerous terrain by your Athletics dots.",""],["Talents","Underworld Contacts","Agent › Thief","vocations/agent.html#thief-underworld-contacts","patron in the criminal underworld · Gain a Streetwise (Criminal Groups) specialty. Take 2 Stress to gain an Opportunity on a Social roll against criminals.",""],["Talents","Shadow Step","Agent › Thief","vocations/agent.html#thief-shadow-step","free action · Thievery 4+ · After you disengage, take 2 Stress and roll Dexterity + Thievery against each enemy's Wits + Composure to hide from them. +1d if you're in cover or obscured.",""],["Talents","Fast Talker","Agent › Thief","vocations/agent.html#thief-fast-talker","free action · Empathy 4+ · Once per round, take 2 Stress to gain 2 extra actions this turn, usable only for Social rolls to use a skill, take a set-up action or use an Agent talent that isn't an attack. These can repeat actions you've already taken, but set-ups against the same target don't stack.",""],["Talents","Trickster's Hand","Agent › Thief","vocations/agent.html#thief-tricksters-hand","free action · Thievery 4+ · Once per round, take 2 Stress to gain 2 extra actions this turn, usable only for Physical rolls to use a skill, take a set-up action or use an Agent talent that isn't an attack. These can repeat actions you've already taken, but set-ups against the same target don't stack.",""],["Talents","Steadfast Challenge","Envoy › Diplomat","vocations/envoy.html#diplomat-steadfast-challenge","1 action · Resolve 2+ · Take 1 Stress and roll Resolve + Intimidation, contested by Wits + Composure. On a success, the enemy is Disoriented until the end of their next turn, and has −1d on rolls against you for a number of rounds equal to your successes.",""],["Talents","Collected","Envoy › Diplomat","vocations/envoy.html#diplomat-collected","passive · +1d to resist Mental and Social rolls.",""],["Talents","Withering Retort","Envoy › Diplomat","vocations/envoy.html#diplomat-withering-retort","Resolve 3+ · When you react to an attack, you can use Steadfast Challenge as a free action. If you succeed, you get +2d on your reaction, but the attacker doesn't suffer Steadfast Challenge's other effects.",""],["Talents","Well Dressed","Envoy › Diplomat","vocations/envoy.html#diplomat-well-dressed","passive · Gain a Socialize (Fashion) specialty. While dressed for the occasion, +1d on your first Subterfuge, Expression or Persuasion roll each hour.",""],["Talents","Calm Appeal","Envoy › Diplomat","vocations/envoy.html#diplomat-calm-appeal","Resolve 3+ · When Steadfast Challenge leaves a target Disoriented, take 1 Stress to calm them: for as long as the challenge's −1d lasts, they also take −1d on all attacks and hostile actions, not only against you. A target who wins the contest against your Steadfast Challenge still takes 1 Stress.",""],["Talents","High Society Contacts","Envoy › Diplomat","vocations/envoy.html#diplomat-high-society-contacts","patron in high society · Gain a Socialize (High Society) specialty. Take 2 Stress to gain an Opportunity on a roll to interact in high society.",""],["Talents","Peaceful Solution","Envoy › Diplomat","vocations/envoy.html#diplomat-peaceful-solution","passive · Resolve 4+ · Attacks made by enemies you've calmed against allies close to you take a further −1d.",""],["Talents","Practiced Oratory","Envoy › Diplomat","vocations/envoy.html#diplomat-practiced-oratory","Persuasion 4+ · When you use Rousing Presence or Steadfast Challenge, take Stress up to your Persuasion dots. Each point adds one more target. For Steadfast Challenge, make one roll; each target contests it separately.",""],["Talents","Customary Garb","Envoy › Faithful","vocations/envoy.html#faithful-customary-garb","passive · While dressed appropriately, +1d to Social rolls and to resist Social rolls.",""],["Talents","Galvanize","Envoy › Faithful","vocations/envoy.html#faithful-galvanize","2 actions · An ally close to you makes a recovery roll. Each ally can benefit from Galvanize once per day.",""],["Talents","Devoted Presence","Envoy › Faithful","vocations/envoy.html#faithful-devoted-presence","Academics 2+ · When you use Rousing Presence, take 1 Stress to remove Prone, Slowed, Stunned and Surprised from the target.",""],["Talents","Composed","Envoy › Faithful","vocations/envoy.html#faithful-composed","passive · +2 maximum Stress.",""],["Talents","Stalwart Presence","Envoy › Faithful","vocations/envoy.html#faithful-stalwart-presence","Resolve 3+ · When you use Rousing Presence, take 1 Stress to give the target +1d to defense rolls, or +1d to resist, for one hour.",""],["Talents","Applied Motivation","Envoy › Faithful","vocations/envoy.html#faithful-applied-motivation","passive · Academics 3+ · When you use Galvanize, the ally clears 1 extra Stress.",""],["Talents","Sage Counsel","Envoy › Faithful","vocations/envoy.html#faithful-sage-counsel","Academics 4+ · When you help a character with teamwork, take 1 Stress to also make them Inspired.",""],["Talents","Inspired Zeal","Envoy › Faithful","vocations/envoy.html#faithful-inspired-zeal","Resolve 4+ · When an ally uses Inspired, choose other allies, up to your Resolve dots. Each becomes Determined.",""],["Talents","Sound Advice","Envoy › Mentor","vocations/envoy.html#mentor-sound-advice","reaction · Use Rousing Presence as a reaction on an ally near you who just failed a roll. They can use Inspired on that roll right away.",""],["Talents","Practical Demonstration","Envoy › Mentor","vocations/envoy.html#mentor-practical-demonstration","free action · Expression 2+ · When you take a set-up action or hit with an attack, use Rousing Presence as a free action.",""],["Talents","Lessons in Patience","Envoy › Mentor","vocations/envoy.html#mentor-lessons-in-patience","passive · Resolve 3+ · Gain an Expression (Motivational Speech) specialty. The target of your Rousing Presence also clears 1 Stress.",""],["Talents","Mighty","Envoy › Mentor","vocations/envoy.html#mentor-mighty","passive · When you hit with a weapon or unarmed attack, deal +1 damage per action spent on the attack.",""],["Talents","Instill Confidence","Envoy › Mentor","vocations/envoy.html#mentor-instill-confidence","Rousing Presence can make an ally Determined instead of Inspired.",""],["Talents","Guiding Oration","Envoy › Mentor","vocations/envoy.html#mentor-guiding-oration","passive · Expression 3+ · After you take a set-up action against a target, an ally close to that target gets +1d on their next roll against it.",""],["Talents","Foresight","Envoy › Mentor","vocations/envoy.html#mentor-foresight","passive · Resolve 4+ · You get 2 reactions per round.",""],["Talents","Rallying Shout","Envoy › Mentor","vocations/envoy.html#mentor-rallying-shout","Expression 4+ · Rousing Presence can wake an Unconscious ally: they heal 2 damage, least severe first.",""],["Talents","Tagging Shot","Hunter › Archer","vocations/hunter.html#archer-tagging-shot","2 actions · Wits 3+ · Move a short distance and make a ranged attack. On a hit or a graze, the target becomes your quarry.",""],["Talents","Combat Training","Hunter › Archer","vocations/hunter.html#archer-combat-training","passive · Gain a weapon specialty and a Socialize (Military Life) specialty, and ignore the penalties of one armor type. Once per round, you can graze without taking Stress.",""],["Talents","Sharp Eye","Hunter › Archer","vocations/hunter.html#archer-sharp-eye","1 action · Observe a target and roll Wits + Investigation, contested by Intelligence + Resolve. On a success, learn their rating, one of their proficiencies, or whether their Health, Stress or power pool is below half.",""],["Talents","Steady Aim","Hunter › Archer","vocations/hunter.html#archer-steady-aim","1 action · Dexterity 2+ · Your next ranged attack this turn has its range increased by half and deals +2 damage.",""],["Talents","Exploit Weakness","Hunter › Archer","vocations/hunter.html#archer-exploit-weakness","free action · Wits 4+ · Take a set-up action against your quarry without spending an action.",""],["Talents","Backstep","Hunter › Archer","vocations/hunter.html#archer-backstep","free action · Dexterity 3+ · After a ranged attack, take 2 Stress to disengage without spending an action. Brace if you reach cover or an obscured area.",""],["Talents","Unrelenting Salvo","Hunter › Archer","vocations/hunter.html#archer-unrelenting-salvo","passive · Dexterity 4+ · You can attack your quarry more than once per turn with the same ranged weapon. Each attack costs an action and takes the usual penalty for extra attacks.",""],["Talents","Hardy","Hunter › Archer","vocations/hunter.html#archer-hardy","passive · +2 Health.",""],["Talents","Startling Blow","Hunter › Assassin","vocations/hunter.html#assassin-startling-blow","1 action · Stealth 2+ · Take 2 Stress and make an attack. On a hit, the target is Surprised until the end of their next turn, as if ambushed. A character can be startled this way once per minute.",""],["Talents","Killing Edge","Hunter › Assassin","vocations/hunter.html#assassin-killing-edge","passive · Wits 3+ · Gain Weaponry (Knives) and Ranged (Thrown Weapons) specialties. These weapons gain the Deadly and Quickdraw traits, and you can attack with them using Dexterity instead of Strength.",""],["Talents","Fatal Thrust","Hunter › Assassin","vocations/hunter.html#assassin-fatal-thrust","2 actions · Wits 4+ · Attack an unsuspecting target with a Light melee weapon. Deal +2 damage, and gain +2d if the weapon is Discreet. On an Opportunity, choose one: the target is Bleeding for 3 turns, Slowed for one minute, or Stunned until the end of their next turn.",""],["Talents","Shadowing","Hunter › Assassin","vocations/hunter.html#assassin-shadowing","Stealth 3+ · Your quarry gets −1d to notice you, and you get +1d to avoid their notice. When your Stealth beats someone's Wits + Composure while you're in cover or obscured, take 3 Stress to make them your quarry.",""],["Talents","Mighty","Hunter › Assassin","vocations/hunter.html#assassin-mighty","passive · When you hit with a weapon or unarmed attack, deal +1 damage per action spent on the attack.",""],["Talents","Cold Eyes","Hunter › Assassin","vocations/hunter.html#assassin-cold-eyes","passive · When you defeat your quarry, choose a new quarry without spending an action. Once per day, you also clear 1 Stress.",""],["Talents","Swift Strikes","Hunter › Assassin","vocations/hunter.html#assassin-swift-strikes","1 action · Weaponry, Brawl or Ranged 3+ (the weapon's skill) · Take 2 Stress to make an extra attack with the same weapon (see Extra attacks). This is an exception to the one-attack rule.",""],["Talents","Sidestep","Hunter › Assassin","vocations/hunter.html#assassin-sidestep","Stealth 4+ · Your first Dodge each round doesn't spend your Defense, while you're not wearing armor rated 2 or higher.",""],["Talents","Deadly Trap","Hunter › Tracker","vocations/hunter.html#tracker-deadly-trap","2 actions · Survival 2+ · Conceal a trap covering a small area (see Ranges). You can have one trap set at a time, and its effects last 1 turn.",""],["Talents","Animal Bond","Hunter › Tracker","vocations/hunter.html#tracker-animal-bond","Gain a rating 2 animal companion of your choice. While your companion is close, you get +1d to defense rolls. As a free action on your turn, give your companion 1 or 2 actions. Your companion can track (2 actions): one target near it becomes your quarry.",""],["Talents","Experienced Trapper","Hunter › Tracker","vocations/hunter.html#tracker-experienced-trapper","Wits 3+ · You can forage for as many people as your Survival dots and make natural tools with Survival. You can have two traps set at once. Your traps deal 2 damage and their effects last 2 turns.",""],["Talents","Protective Bond","Hunter › Tracker","vocations/hunter.html#tracker-protective-bond","1 action · Your companion protects an ally close to you. That ally gets the Animal Bond bonus instead of you.",""],["Talents","Surefooted","Hunter › Tracker","vocations/hunter.html#tracker-surefooted","passive · +3 Speed. Reduce damage from falls and dangerous terrain by your Athletics dots.",""],["Talents","Feral Connection","Hunter › Tracker","vocations/hunter.html#tracker-feral-connection","passive · Survival 3+ · Gain an Animal Ken specialty. Your companion gains +3 Health and +1d to its defense rolls, and extra dice on its rolls equal to half your Survival, rounded up.",""],["Talents","Hunter's Edge","Hunter › Tracker","vocations/hunter.html#tracker-hunters-edge","passive · Survival 4+ · Your companion gets +1d against your quarry. Your traps deal 3 damage and their effects last 3 turns.",""],["Talents","Pack Hunting","Hunter › Tracker","vocations/hunter.html#tracker-pack-hunting","reaction · Wits 4+ · Take 1 Stress to give an ally +2d on their attack against your quarry.",""],["Talents","Combat Coordination","Leader › Champion","vocations/leader.html#champion-combat-coordination","free action · Expression 3+ · Once per turn, after you attack, use Decisive Command without spending an action. If the attack missed, it also costs no Stress.",""],["Talents","Valiant Intervention","Leader › Champion","vocations/leader.html#champion-valiant-intervention","1 action · Athletics 2+ · Take 1 Stress, move up to half your Speed, then roll Presence + Athletics against an enemy in your reach, contested by its Wits + Composure. On a success, it gets −1d on rolls against your allies until the end of its next turn.",""],["Talents","Imposing Posture","Leader › Champion","vocations/leader.html#champion-imposing-posture","passive · Athletics 3+ · When an enemy within your weapon's reach wins a contested Social roll against you, they still become Disoriented until the end of their next turn.",""],["Talents","Hardy","Leader › Champion","vocations/leader.html#champion-hardy","passive · +2 Health.",""],["Talents","Mighty","Leader › Champion","vocations/leader.html#champion-mighty","passive · When you hit with a weapon or unarmed attack, deal +1 damage per action spent on the attack.",""],["Talents","Resolute Stand","Leader › Champion","vocations/leader.html#champion-resolute-stand","Athletics 2+ · Valiant Intervention doesn't provoke Reactive Strikes. Take Stress up to your Expression dots to add that many targets to it.",""],["Talents","Resilient Hero","Leader › Champion","vocations/leader.html#champion-resilient-hero","reaction · Athletics 4+ · Once per day, when damage would fill your last Health box, instead leave empty boxes equal to your Athletics dots.",""],["Talents","Demonstrative Command","Leader › Champion","vocations/leader.html#champion-demonstrative-command","Expression 3+ · +1d to your command dice. Take 1 Stress to add your command dice to your own Athletics or Expression roll, or to any roll using Dexterity.",""],["Talents","Composed","Leader › Officer","vocations/leader.html#officer-composed","passive · +2 maximum Stress.",""],["Talents","Through the Fray","Leader › Officer","vocations/leader.html#officer-through-the-fray","1 action · Persuasion 2+ · An ally close to you can disengage or take a set-up action as a reaction.",""],["Talents","Well Supplied","Leader › Officer","vocations/leader.html#officer-well-supplied","Persuasion 3+ · Gain a Politics (Military Logistics) specialty. Take 2 Stress to gain an Opportunity on a roll to requisition resources.",""],["Talents","Customary Garb","Leader › Officer","vocations/leader.html#officer-customary-garb","passive · While dressed appropriately, +1d to Social rolls and to resist Social rolls.",""],["Talents","Relentless March","Leader › Officer","vocations/leader.html#officer-relentless-march","passive · Persuasion 4+ · Decisive Command also gives the target +3 Speed. Until their next turn, they ignore Exhausted, Slowed and Surprised.",""],["Talents","Confident Command","Leader › Officer","vocations/leader.html#officer-confident-command","Expression 3+ · +1d to your command dice. Take 1 Stress to add your command dice to your own Intimidation, Expression or Persuasion roll.",""],["Talents","Synchronized Assault","Leader › Officer","vocations/leader.html#officer-synchronized-assault","3 actions · Expression 4+ · Roll Presence + Expression, contested by the target's Intelligence + Resolve. On a success, choose allies up to your Expression dots and take 1 Stress for each; on their next turns, each gains an extra action for one more attack against the target (see Extra attacks). This is an exception to the one-attack rule. On a failure, only one ally gains it, for 1 Stress.",""],["Talents","Authority","Leader › Officer","vocations/leader.html#officer-authority","rank commanding 5+ people · Double the range of your Leader talents that affect allies, and the number of allies they affect.",""],["Talents","Cutthroat Tactics","Leader › Politico","vocations/leader.html#politico-cutthroat-tactics","Subterfuge 2+ · An ally can raise the stakes instead of using your command dice. If the stakes die shows a Complication, you clear 1 Stress.",""],["Talents","Tactical Ploy","Leader › Politico","vocations/leader.html#politico-tactical-ploy","1 action · Take 1 Stress and roll Manipulation + Subterfuge, contested by Intelligence + Resolve. On a success, the target loses their reaction and gets −1d on their next Mental or Social roll.",""],["Talents","Rumormonger","Leader › Politico","vocations/leader.html#politico-rumormonger","requires a patron · Gain a Politics (Scandal) specialty. Take 2 Stress to gain an Opportunity on a roll to spread misinformation or gather rumors.",""],["Talents","Well Dressed","Leader › Politico","vocations/leader.html#politico-well-dressed","passive · Gain a Socialize (Fashion) specialty. While dressed for the occasion, +1d on your first Subterfuge, Expression or Persuasion roll each hour.",""],["Talents","Baleful","Leader › Politico","vocations/leader.html#politico-baleful","passive · When you win a contested Mental or Social roll against a target, they also take 1 Stress.",""],["Talents","Set at Odds","Leader › Politico","vocations/leader.html#politico-set-at-odds","2 actions · Expression 4+ · Take Stress equal to the number of targets. Roll Presence + Expression, contested by the highest Wits + Composure among them. On a success, until the end of their next turn, they're Disoriented, can't use teamwork or aid each other, and take −1d on rolls that would help one another. Outside a fight, they distrust each other for one hour instead.",""],["Talents","Shrewd Command","Leader › Politico","vocations/leader.html#politico-shrewd-command","Expression 3+ · +1d to your command dice. Take 1 Stress to add your command dice to your own Subterfuge, Empathy or Expression roll.",""],["Talents","Grand Deception","Leader › Politico","vocations/leader.html#politico-grand-deception","3 actions · Subterfuge 4+ · Take 3 Stress and roll Manipulation + Subterfuge, needing 3 successes. On a success, reveal a ruse you set up earlier that changes one detail of the situation.",""],["Talents","Efficient Engineer","Scholar › Engineer","vocations/scholar.html#engineer-efficient-engineer","Crafts 2+ · Gain a Crafts (Weapon Crafting, Armor Crafting or Equipment Crafting) specialty. When crafting, your Opportunity range expands by 1 and material costs are halved.",""],["Talents","Field Repair","Scholar › Engineer","vocations/scholar.html#engineer-field-repair","2 actions · Crafts 2+ · Once per minute, roll Intelligence + Crafts (or Technology, for a Device), needing 2 successes, to clear Wear from an ally's armor up to your Crafts dots, make a broken item work until the end of the fight, or restore 1 charge to a Device.",""],["Talents","Deep Study","Scholar › Engineer","vocations/scholar.html#engineer-deep-study","passive · Erudition gives you one more specialty and two more skill dots.",""],["Talents","Inventive Design","Scholar › Engineer","vocations/scholar.html#engineer-inventive-design","Technology 3+ · Gain a Crafts (Device Crafting) specialty. You can craft Devices rated 1 higher than usual.",""],["Talents","Fine Handiwork","Scholar › Engineer","vocations/scholar.html#engineer-fine-handiwork","When crafting, advanced upgrades cost the same as ordinary upgrades.",""],["Talents","Overcharge","Scholar › Engineer","vocations/scholar.html#engineer-overcharge","Technology 4+ · Once per turn, as a free action, push a Device close to you: raise the stakes on its next roll. On an Opportunity, it acts again right away at −2d; that roll can't trigger Overcharge.",""],["Talents","Master Artisan","Scholar › Engineer","vocations/scholar.html#engineer-master-artisan","When crafting, you get 2 extra attempts and the interval is halved.",""],["Talents","Overwhelm with Details","Scholar › Engineer","vocations/scholar.html#engineer-overwhelm-with-details","Academics 4+ · Take 2 Stress to use Academics in place of another skill on a Mental or Social roll.",""],["Talents","Strategize","Scholar › Strategist","vocations/scholar.html#strategist-strategize","Investigation 2+ · After a set-up action using an Erudition skill, you can give the +1d to an ally instead. Take 2 Stress to also stop the target from reacting against that ally.",""],["Talents","Mind and Body","Scholar › Strategist","vocations/scholar.html#strategist-mind-and-body","passive · Gain a weapon specialty and one more Erudition skill. Erudition can now pick Physical skills.",""],["Talents","Composed","Scholar › Strategist","vocations/scholar.html#strategist-composed","passive · +2 maximum Stress.",""],["Talents","Know Your Moment","Scholar › Strategist","vocations/scholar.html#strategist-know-your-moment","passive · Investigation 3+ · +1d to defense rolls from the start of each round until you take your turn.",""],["Talents","Deep Contemplation","Scholar › Strategist","vocations/scholar.html#strategist-deep-contemplation","2 actions · Academics 3+ · Reassign up to 2 of your Erudition skills or specialties.",""],["Talents","Keen Insight","Scholar › Strategist","vocations/scholar.html#strategist-keen-insight","passive · When your set-up action against a target succeeds, they also get −1d on their next roll.",""],["Talents","Contingency","Scholar › Strategist","vocations/scholar.html#strategist-contingency","reaction · Academics 4+ · Take 2 Stress to remove a Complication from the roll of an ally close to you.",""],["Talents","Turning Point","Scholar › Strategist","vocations/scholar.html#strategist-turning-point","2 actions · Investigation 4+ · Once per minute, take 2 Stress and roll Wits + Investigation, contested by the enemy leader's Intelligence + Resolve (or the strongest enemy's, if there's no leader), with +1d if you took a slow turn. On a success, you and your allies gain 1 extra action on your next turns.",""],["Talents","Field Medicine","Scholar › Surgeon","vocations/scholar.html#surgeon-field-medicine","1 action · Medicine 2+ · Take 2 Stress and roll Intelligence + Medicine, needing 2 successes. On a success, a conscious character close to you heals 2 damage, least severe first, and stops Bleeding.",""],["Talents","Emotional Intelligence","Scholar › Surgeon","vocations/scholar.html#surgeon-emotional-intelligence","passive · Gain a Medicine (Diagnosis) specialty and one more Erudition skill. Erudition can now pick Social skills.",""],["Talents","Anatomical Insight","Scholar › Surgeon","vocations/scholar.html#surgeon-anatomical-insight","Once per round, when you hit with an unarmed attack, take 1 Stress or spend an Opportunity to give the target 1 stack of Exhausted. Stacks from this talent wear off after one minute.",""],["Talents","Collected","Scholar › Surgeon","vocations/scholar.html#surgeon-collected","passive · +1d to resist Mental and Social rolls.",""],["Talents","Swift Healer","Scholar › Surgeon","vocations/scholar.html#surgeon-swift-healer","Medicine 3+ · Field Medicine becomes a free action and heals 1 extra damage.",""],["Talents","Applied Medicine","Scholar › Surgeon","vocations/scholar.html#surgeon-applied-medicine","passive · Academics 3+ · Your Field Medicine heals 1 extra damage.",""],["Talents","Ongoing Care","Scholar › Surgeon","vocations/scholar.html#surgeon-ongoing-care","Academics 4+ · Gain a Medicine (Mental Health Care) specialty. When you Recover a patient, they also lose one condition caused by injury, such as Bleeding, Slowed or 1 stack of Exhausted.",""],["Talents","Resuscitation","Scholar › Surgeon","vocations/scholar.html#surgeon-resuscitation","Medicine 4+ · Field Medicine can revive a character who is Unconscious, or who died from damage within the last round, for 3 Stress instead of 2. They heal the usual amount, aggravated damage first if their track is full of it, and wake up. It can't undo death from a Bout of Madness or a Death's Mark.",""],["Talents","Practiced Kata","Warrior › Duelist","vocations/warrior.html#duelist-practiced-kata","You know subtle ways to use your stances outside of combat, and gain their bonuses there too. Unless Surprised or already in a stance, you start every fight in Vigilant Stance.",""],["Talents","Challenger's Stance","Warrior › Duelist","vocations/warrior.html#duelist-challengers-stance","1 action · Intimidation 2+ · Enter Challenger's Stance: +1d to Intimidation. When only one enemy is in your reach and no allies are, you can either attack or take a set-up action without spending an action. The one-attack rule still applies.",""],["Talents","Counter Stance","Warrior › Duelist","vocations/warrior.html#duelist-counter-stance","1 action · Athletics 3+ · Enter Counter Stance: +1d to Empathy. When an attacker misses or only grazes you, you can make a Reactive Strike against them.",""],["Talents","Signature Weapon","Warrior › Duelist","vocations/warrior.html#duelist-signature-weapon","passive · Gain a weapon specialty. Choose one of your weapon specialties: attacks with that weapon get an Opportunity on 4+ successes. If it's a Light weapon, you can attack with Dexterity instead of Strength.",""],["Talents","Surefooted","Warrior › Duelist","vocations/warrior.html#duelist-surefooted","passive · +3 Speed. Reduce damage from falls and dangerous terrain by your Athletics dots.",""],["Talents","Feinting Strike","Warrior › Duelist","vocations/warrior.html#duelist-feinting-strike","1 action · Intimidation 3+ · Take 2 Stress and make a melee weapon attack, contested by Intelligence + Resolve. On a hit, the target loses their reaction and takes Stress equal to your net successes, up to your Intimidation. On a graze, they take 1 Stress and keep their reaction.",""],["Talents","Windstance","Warrior › Duelist","vocations/warrior.html#duelist-windstance","1 action · Wits 3+ · Enter Windstance: +1d to rolls using Dexterity. While multiple enemies are in your reach, you can either disengage or attack without spending an action. The one-attack rule still applies.",""],["Talents","Wit's End","Warrior › Duelist","vocations/warrior.html#duelist-wits-end","2 actions · Intimidation 4+ · Take 1 Stress, move up to half your Speed, then make a melee weapon attack against a target with a full Stress track. The attack ignores armor, deals +3 damage and can't graze.",""],["Talents","Heavy Training","Warrior › Juggernaut","vocations/warrior.html#juggernaut-heavy-training","requires Heavy armor and a Heavy weapon · Gain an Athletics (Heavy Armor) specialty and a weapon specialty in one heavy weapon. Your armor's Wear track has 2 extra boxes. When you attack with the heavy weapon, you also graze extra targets in reach, up to your weapon skill dots.",""],["Talents","Stonestance","Warrior › Juggernaut","vocations/warrior.html#juggernaut-stonestance","1 action · Enter Stonestance: +1 armor rating. Enemies in your reach must spend 1 extra action to attack an ally who isn't in Stonestance.",""],["Talents","Repelling Stance","Warrior › Juggernaut","vocations/warrior.html#juggernaut-repelling-stance","1 action · Athletics 3+ · Enter Repelling Stance: +1d to Parry and +1d to resist Mental rolls. After you're hit or grazed in melee, use your reaction to roll Strength + Athletics, contested by Dexterity + Stamina. On a success, the attacker takes Stress equal to your net successes, up to your Athletics, and is pushed back 1 meter.",""],["Talents","Mighty","Warrior › Juggernaut","vocations/warrior.html#juggernaut-mighty","passive · When you hit with a weapon or unarmed attack, deal +1 damage per action spent on the attack.",""],["Talents","Shattering Blow","Warrior › Juggernaut","vocations/warrior.html#juggernaut-shattering-blow","Wits 3+ · When you hit with a melee attack, take 2 Stress. The target's armor takes 1 Wear (or loses 1 rating for one hour if its Wear track is already full), and every target you damage is pushed back a number of meters equal to the damage it took, of any type (bashing, lethal or aggravated). Count the hit's damage once, even if it lands across several severities. A graze or a hit stopped by armor still pushes 1 meter.",""],["Talents","Bloodstance","Warrior › Juggernaut","vocations/warrior.html#juggernaut-bloodstance","1 action · Athletics 3+ · Enter Bloodstance: your attacks get an Opportunity on 4+ successes, but your Defense is 0 and you take −1d to resist Mental and Social rolls.",""],["Talents","Shrug It Off","Warrior › Juggernaut","vocations/warrior.html#juggernaut-shrug-it-off","free action · Wits 4+ · When a melee attack hits you, whether or not you tried to Parry, take Stress to ignore that much damage, up to your Weaponry dots.",""],["Talents","Unstoppable Charge","Warrior › Juggernaut","vocations/warrior.html#juggernaut-unstoppable-charge","2 actions · Athletics 4+ · Take 2 Stress and move up to your Speed in a straight line. Make a heavy-weapon attack against each enemy you pass within reach; each defends separately. This is an exception to the one-attack rule. Each enemy you hit is pushed aside 1 meter or falls Prone, your choice. Reactive Strikes don't stop your movement. +1d in heavy armor.",""],["Talents","Cautious Advance","Warrior › Soldier","vocations/warrior.html#soldier-cautious-advance","2 actions · Resolve 2+ · Move up to half your Speed, ignoring difficult terrain. Then Brace or take a set-up action without spending an action.",""],["Talents","Combat Training","Warrior › Soldier","vocations/warrior.html#soldier-combat-training","passive · Gain a weapon specialty and a Socialize (Military Life) specialty, and ignore the penalties of one armor type. Once per round, you can graze without taking Stress.",""],["Talents","Defensive Position","Warrior › Soldier","vocations/warrior.html#soldier-defensive-position","passive · Athletics 3+ · Your Brace gives +2d instead of +1d, and allies can Brace behind you.",""],["Talents","Devastating Blow","Warrior › Soldier","vocations/warrior.html#soldier-devastating-blow","2 actions · Athletics 4+ · Make a melee weapon attack that deals +2 damage.",""],["Talents","Formation Drills","Warrior › Soldier","vocations/warrior.html#soldier-formation-drills","passive · Resolve 3+ · Allies close to you who Brace also get your Defensive Position bonus.",""],["Talents","Hardy","Warrior › Soldier","vocations/warrior.html#soldier-hardy","passive · +2 Health.",""],["Talents","Wary","Warrior › Soldier","vocations/warrior.html#soldier-wary","passive · Resolve 4+ · You can't be Surprised while your Stress track isn't full. When an effect forces Stress on you, take 1 less, to a minimum of 1.",""],["Talents","Swift Strikes","Warrior › Soldier","vocations/warrior.html#soldier-swift-strikes","1 action · Weaponry, Brawl or Ranged 3+ (the weapon's skill) · Take 2 Stress to make an extra attack with the same weapon (see Extra attacks). This is an exception to the one-attack rule.",""],["Talents","Figments","Dreamshaper › Mesmer","vocations/dreamshaper.html#mesmer-figments","2 actions · 1 Reverie. Roll Wits + Dreamshaping to create an illusion of sight and sound within 10 meters, filling up to one small area per Dreamshaping dot. Moving it takes 1 action. Anyone who touches it, or has a reason to doubt it, rolls Wits + Composure contested by your roll to see through it. A figment can hide you as if you were Obscured, and you can use it to set up anyone it fools.","duskworld"],["Talents","Mental Awakening","Dreamshaper › Mesmer","vocations/dreamshaper.html#mesmer-mental-awakening","2 actions · 1 Reverie. Roll Intelligence + Dreamshaping. Your mind opens to the Everdream: +2d to Mental skill rolls while it lasts.","duskworld"],["Talents","Dreamguise","Dreamshaper › Mesmer","vocations/dreamshaper.html#mesmer-dreamguise","2 actions · Dreamshaping 2+ · 2 Reverie. Roll Intelligence + Dreamshaping to disguise yourself, or a willing creature you touch, as anyone of about the same size. Anyone who doubts the disguise contests it with Wits + Composure. The disguised character gets +1d to Subterfuge to pass as that person.","duskworld"],["Talents","Dread","Dreamshaper › Mesmer","vocations/dreamshaper.html#mesmer-dread","2 actions · Dreamshaping 2+ · 2 Reverie. Push a nightmare into the mind of someone within 10 meters: roll Manipulation + Dreamshaping, contested by Wits + Composure. On a success, they're Frightened of you while the dreamcraft lasts.","duskworld"],["Talents","Dreamwalk","Dreamshaper › Mesmer","vocations/dreamshaper.html#mesmer-dreamwalk","Dreamshaping 3+ · Dreamed only. 2 Reverie. Roll Resolve + Dreamshaping and choose up to one sleeper per Dreamshaping dot, each someone whose face and name you know, within 10 kilometers per dot. While it lasts, whenever you sleep you can enter one of their dreams and speak with them freely; joining another means leaving the first. An unwilling dreamer contests with Intelligence + Resolve each time you enter. Your presence lingers: +1d to your next Social roll against them.","duskworld"],["Talents","Dreamsteal","Dreamshaper › Mesmer","vocations/dreamshaper.html#mesmer-dreamsteal","Dreamshaping 3+ · Dreamed or deep meditation only. 3 Reverie, instant. Choose someone you know within 10 kilometers per Dreamshaping dot: roll Manipulation + Dreamshaping, contested by Intelligence + Resolve. On a success, their next rest gives them nothing (no Stress cleared, no healing, nothing refreshed) and they gain 1 stack of Exhausted. Most dreamshapers find it vile.","duskworld"],["Talents","Dreamveil","Dreamshaper › Mesmer","vocations/dreamshaper.html#mesmer-dreamveil","Dreamshaping 4+ · Dreamed or deep meditation only. 4 Reverie. Roll Intelligence + Dreamshaping. A building, or a camp up to 20 meters across, looks and sounds empty and ordinary from outside. Finding it means beating your roll with Wits + Investigation. Hidden kehar settlements are kept this way.","duskworld"],["Talents","Enthrall","Dreamshaper › Mesmer","vocations/dreamshaper.html#mesmer-enthrall","2 actions · Dreamshaping 4+ · 3 Reverie. Roll Manipulation + Dreamshaping, contested by Intelligence + Resolve. On a success, the target obeys one short command while the dreamcraft lasts, following the compulsion rules.","duskworld"],["Talents","Dreamstuff","Dreamshaper › Maker","vocations/dreamshaper.html#maker-dreamstuff","2 actions · 1 Reverie. Roll Intelligence + Dreamshaping to shape a simple object of up to bulk 1: a rope, a tool, a key blank, or a weapon that counts as Light. Nothing with intricate moving parts. A tool works as a kit with charges equal to your successes, up to 3.","duskworld"],["Talents","Reshape","Dreamshaper › Maker","vocations/dreamshaper.html#maker-reshape","2 actions · Dreamshaping 2+ · 2 Reverie. Roll Intelligence + Dreamshaping to change one property of a non-living object you touch, up to bulk 1 per Dreamshaping dot: its shape, hardness or texture. Make a broken item work, soften a lock until it opens (your roll against the lock), or raise handholds in a wall.","duskworld"],["Talents","Dream Companion","Dreamshaper › Maker","vocations/dreamshaper.html#maker-dream-companion","2 actions · Dreamshaping 3+ · 3 Reverie. Roll Presence + Dreamshaping to call a creature from the Everdream. It's a companion with a rating of your Dreamshaping − 1, proficient in what you choose. It dissolves when the dreamcraft ends or its Health fills. You can have only one at a time.","duskworld"],["Talents","Shape Ground","Dreamshaper › Maker","vocations/dreamshaper.html#maker-shape-ground","2 actions · Dreamshaping 3+ · 2 Reverie. Roll Presence + Dreamshaping. Within 10 meters, turn a small area into difficult terrain, smooth difficult terrain away, or raise a wall that gives full cover.","duskworld"],["Talents","Unmake","Dreamshaper › Maker","vocations/dreamshaper.html#maker-unmake","2 actions · Dreamshaping 4+ · 3 Reverie, instant. Roll Intelligence + Dreamshaping to return a non-living object of up to bulk 1 per Dreamshaping dot to the Everdream. A Device contests with its own roll. Against another dreamshaper's dreamcraft or a creature of the Everdream, it's contested by their dreamcraft roll or rating; if you win, it ends.","duskworld"],["Talents","Rewrite","Dreamshaper › Maker","vocations/dreamshaper.html#maker-rewrite","3 actions · Dreamshaping 4+ · 5 Reverie, once per day. The world is only another dream. Roll Presence + Dreamshaping to change one thing about your surroundings within 10 meters, with the GM's approval: a fall becomes a slow drift, a door becomes a wall, a fire becomes cold smoke. Creatures it affects contest with Dexterity + Stamina. When the dreamcraft ends, so does the change.","duskworld"],["Rules","Reading a talent","Talents › Reading a talent","talents/index.html#reading-a-talent","Each talent is written as Name followed by tags, then its effect: after: the talent you need before you can take this one. Talents tagged after key talent start a tree and need only the vocation's key talent. Where two talents are listed (\"after A or B\"), either one is enough. Cost: 1 action, 2 actions, 3 actions, free action, reaction or passive. Prerequisites: skills, attributes, community traits or gear the talent requires, such as Wits 3+ or requires an animal companion. They apply on top of the after tag. Prerequisites count only permanent dots. Temporary dots from talents (such as the Scholar's Erudition) help your rolls but never meet a talent's or trait's prerequisite.",""],["Rules","after","Talents › Reading a talent","talents/index.html#reading-a-talent","after: the talent you need before you can take this one. Talents tagged after key talent start a tree and need only the vocation's key talent. Where two talents are listed (\"after A or B\"), either one is enough. ",""],["Rules","Cost","Talents › Reading a talent","talents/index.html#reading-a-talent","Cost: 1 action, 2 actions, 3 actions, free action, reaction or passive. ",""],["Rules","Prerequisites","Talents › Reading a talent","talents/index.html#reading-a-talent","Prerequisites: skills, attributes, community traits or gear the talent requires, such as Wits 3+ or requires an animal companion. They apply on top of the after tag. ",""],["Rules","Talent trees","Talents › Talent trees","talents/index.html#talent-trees","Each vocation has a key talent and three trees of talents. You can have more than one vocation by gaining another vocation's key talent through advancement or your free pick at character creation. After that, its trees are open to you too. Communities use the same tree structure for their traits (see Character Creation).",""],["Rules","Traits","Talents › Traits","talents/index.html#traits","Traits are where you come from; talents are what you trained. There are three kinds in the core rules: Racial traits: a race's primary and secondary trait (see Character Creation). Community traits: the key and further traits of a community (see Character Creation). Weapon traits: properties of a weapon, such as Deadly or Quickdraw (see Weapon traits). Settings can add their own kinds, always named with what they belong to, such as Duskworld's Dusk traits, which come with the stages of the Dusking Scar (see The Dusk).",""],["Traits","Racial traits","Talents › Traits","talents/index.html#traits","Racial traits: a race's primary and secondary trait (see Character Creation). ",""],["Traits","Community traits","Talents › Traits","talents/index.html#traits","Community traits: the key and further traits of a community (see Character Creation). ",""],["Traits","Weapon traits","Talents › Traits","talents/index.html#traits","Weapon traits: properties of a weapon, such as Deadly or Quickdraw (see Weapon traits). ",""],["Rules","Terms","Talents › Terms","talents/index.html#terms","Specialties from talents and traits always name their skill, written Skill (Specialty): Socialize (Military Life), Athletics (Heavy Armor). They work even with 0 dots in the skill (see Specialties). Weapon specialty: a specialty in Weaponry, Ranged or Brawl that names a weapon type, such as Weaponry (Swords), Ranged (Crossbows) or Brawl (Boxing). Usage limits (once per round, minute, hour or day) are measured in in-world time (see Core Rules). Unsuspecting: Surprised or unaware of you (see Ambush). Quarry: one character at a time, chosen with Seek Quarry and related talents. Stances: entering one takes 1 action. You're in one stance at a time; it lasts until you change it or one hour passes. Patron: contacts, allies, a mentor or standing in the relevant circle, usually from a community trait that says you count as having a patron. Wear: armor's damage track, used by the heavy-armor talents (see Armor).",""],["Rules","Specialties from talents and traits","Talents › Terms","talents/index.html#terms","Specialties from talents and traits always name their skill, written Skill (Specialty): Socialize (Military Life), Athletics (Heavy Armor). They work even with 0 dots in the skill (see Specialties). ",""],["Rules","Weapon specialty","Talents › Terms","talents/index.html#terms","Weapon specialty: a specialty in Weaponry, Ranged or Brawl that names a weapon type, such as Weaponry (Swords), Ranged (Crossbows) or Brawl (Boxing). ",""],["Rules","Usage limits","Talents › Terms","talents/index.html#terms","Usage limits (once per round, minute, hour or day) are measured in in-world time (see Core Rules). ",""],["Rules","Unsuspecting","Talents › Terms","talents/index.html#terms","Unsuspecting: Surprised or unaware of you (see Ambush). ",""],["Rules","Quarry","Talents › Terms","talents/index.html#terms","Quarry: one character at a time, chosen with Seek Quarry and related talents. ",""],["Rules","Stances","Talents › Terms","talents/index.html#terms","Stances: entering one takes 1 action. You're in one stance at a time; it lasts until you change it or one hour passes. ",""],["Rules","Patron","Talents › Terms","talents/index.html#terms","Patron: contacts, allies, a mentor or standing in the relevant circle, usually from a community trait that says you count as having a patron. ",""],["Rules","Wear","Talents › Terms","talents/index.html#terms","Wear: armor's damage track, used by the heavy-armor talents (see Armor). ",""],["Rules","Making an item","Crafting › Making an item","crafting.html#making-an-item","Choose what you're making, gather the materials, and roll at the interval below until you reach the target. Pool: Intelligence + Crafts. Use Technology for the setting's most advanced machines, and Science for chemicals and compounds. A tool kit gives +1d as usual (see Kits). Target: set by the item's complexity, plus any upgrades you declare (below). Interval: an hour for small items, a day for weapons and armor, a week for large or heavy work. Attempts: you can make a number of rolls up to your dice pool when you start (Intelligence + Crafts, plus a kit or specialty). Unlike other extended actions, crafting has this limit as well as the Stress cost below. If you run out before reaching the target, see Defects. Materials: half the item's price in wealth (see Wealth), or salvaged and found materials at the GM's call. Complexity Target Examples Simple 3 Knife, club, rope ladder, a simple trap Standard 6 Sword, spear, light armor, a kit Complex 10 Crossbow, medium armor, a lock, a rating 1–2 Device Masterwork 15 Heavy armor, a rifle, a rating 3+ Device As in any extended action, a roll with no successes costs 1 Stress to press on; if you don't pay, you stop there, as if you'd run out",""],["Rules","Pool","Crafting › Making an item","crafting.html#making-an-item","Pool: Intelligence + Crafts. Use Technology for the setting's most advanced machines, and Science for chemicals and compounds. A tool kit gives +1d as usual (see Kits). ",""],["Rules","Target","Crafting › Making an item","crafting.html#making-an-item","Target: set by the item's complexity, plus any upgrades you declare (below). ",""],["Rules","Interval","Crafting › Making an item","crafting.html#making-an-item","Interval: an hour for small items, a day for weapons and armor, a week for large or heavy work. ",""],["Rules","Attempts","Crafting › Making an item","crafting.html#making-an-item","Attempts: you can make a number of rolls up to your dice pool when you start (Intelligence + Crafts, plus a kit or specialty). Unlike other extended actions, crafting has this limit as well as the Stress cost below. If you run out before reaching the target, see Defects. ",""],["Rules","Materials","Crafting › Making an item","crafting.html#making-an-item","Materials: half the item's price in wealth (see Wealth), or salvaged and found materials at the GM's call. ",""],["Rules","Defects","Crafting › Defects","crafting.html#defects","If you run out of attempts with at least half the target (counting declared upgrades), the item is finished but defective: it gets no upgrades and one defect from the table below. With less than half, the work fails and the materials are lost. A defect can be fixed later as a repair, like broken gear: the target is half the item's crafting target, rounded up. Item Defect Weapon Unreliable: a Complication on an attack with it breaks it Armor Ill-fitting: its Stealth and Athletics penalty is 1 die worse (light armor gains −1d) Kit 2 charges instead of 3 Device 1 charge fewer, and a Complication when using it spends all its charges Other Crude: it works, but anyone who knows the craft can tell (−1d to Socialize where it's seen)",""],["Rules","Upgrades","Crafting › Upgrades","crafting.html#upgrades","An upgrade improves an item beyond the ordinary. An item can hold up to your Crafts dots in upgrades, counting an advanced upgrade as two.",""],["Rules","Declared upgrades","Crafting › Upgrades","crafting.html#declared-upgrades","Before you start, declare the upgrades you want. Each one adds 2 to the target, and each advanced upgrade adds 4. That's cheaper than refining, but you risk more: if you run out of attempts before reaching the full target, you lose every declared upgrade and the item has a defect or fails (see Defects).",""],["Rules","Refining","Crafting › Upgrades","crafting.html#refining","Once you reach the target, you can push for more with the attempts you have left: Every 3 successes from these rolls add one upgrade (an advanced upgrade needs 6). That costs more than declaring it, but the item itself is already finished and safe. A roll with no successes, or a Complication, ends refining, and the Complication costs you one upgrade gained by refining. You can stop after any roll and keep what you've gained.",""],["Rules","Upgrade list","Crafting › Upgrades","crafting.html#upgrade-list","The GM can approve others that fit the item. Item Upgrades Advanced upgrades Weapon Add Quickdraw, Discreet, Throwable or Precise Add Deadly or Piercing 1; +1 damage (once) Armor +2 Wear boxes; remove its Stealth and Athletics penalty +1 rating (once) Kit +1 charge Masterwork: +2d instead of +1d Device +1 charge; +1 to its range or area +1 rating (once) Any Fine make: +1d to Socialize rolls where it's seen Sturdy: it only breaks through deliberate effort, and repairing it takes half the usual target Weapon traits are defined in Core Rules.",""],["Rules","Repair","Crafting › Repair","crafting.html#repair","Repairing uses the same rules, including the attempt limit, at the interval for the item. Lost armor rating: target 3 for each point of rating restored. Broken gear: half the item's crafting target, rounded up. Materials: a quarter of the item's price, or salvage. Repair is how armor regains lost rating and how broken gear works again (see Armor and Rest).",""],["Rules","Lost armor rating","Crafting › Repair","crafting.html#repair","Lost armor rating: target 3 for each point of rating restored. ",""],["Rules","Broken gear","Crafting › Repair","crafting.html#repair","Broken gear: half the item's crafting target, rounded up. ",""],["Rules","Materials","Crafting › Repair","crafting.html#repair","Materials: a quarter of the item's price, or salvage. ",""],["Rules","Devices","Crafting › Devices","crafting.html#devices","A Device is a self-operating mechanism: a trap, an alarm, a launcher, an automaton. Once it's set or started, it works on its own, so the user's skill doesn't matter. Weapons, armor and tools whose use depends on the wielder's skill aren't Devices: make them as ordinary items and improve them with upgrades. What powers a Device depends on the setting: clockwork, alchemy, runes, lightning, or something stranger. Each Device has a rating from 1 to 5 and a single function. Rolling: when a Device makes a roll, it rolls twice its rating in dice, like an NPC rolling something it's proficient in (see NPCs). Setting it or starting it takes 1 action unless it says otherwise. Charges: a Device has charges equal to its rating. Each use spends 1. Each Prepare at a rest refills 1 charge, like a kit. Who can build one: Devices are complex work. You need Crafts 3+ and Technology 2+ to build one, and its rating can't be higher than your Crafts dots − 2 (rating 1 at Crafts 3, 2 at Crafts 4, 3 at Crafts 5). Crafting: a rating 1–2 Device is Complex (target 10); rating 3 or more is Masterwork (target 15). Each Device also needs a core component the setting defines, such as a power source or a focus. W",""],["Rules","Rolling","Crafting › Devices","crafting.html#devices","Rolling: when a Device makes a roll, it rolls twice its rating in dice, like an NPC rolling something it's proficient in (see NPCs). Setting it or starting it takes 1 action unless it says otherwise. ",""],["Rules","Charges","Crafting › Devices","crafting.html#devices","Charges: a Device has charges equal to its rating. Each use spends 1. Each Prepare at a rest refills 1 charge, like a kit. ",""],["Rules","Who can build one","Crafting › Devices","crafting.html#devices","Who can build one: Devices are complex work. You need Crafts 3+ and Technology 2+ to build one, and its rating can't be higher than your Crafts dots − 2 (rating 1 at Crafts 3, 2 at Crafts 4, 3 at Crafts 5). ",""],["Rules","Crafting","Crafting › Devices","crafting.html#devices","Crafting: a rating 1–2 Device is Complex (target 10); rating 3 or more is Masterwork (target 15). Each Device also needs a core component the setting defines, such as a power source or a focus. Without one, the Device can't be built. ",""],["Rules","Disabling or bypassing","Crafting › Devices","crafting.html#devices","Disabling or bypassing: roll Dexterity or Intelligence + Technology, contested by the Device's roll. On a success, it's disarmed, jammed or slipped past; on a failure, it triggers or alerts its owner, as fits the Device. ",""],["Rules","Settings","Crafting › Devices","crafting.html#devices","Settings add their own Devices, and decide what core components are and where to find them. Higher-rated Devices can also be found, bought or salvaged. ",""],["Rules","Common Devices","Crafting › Devices","crafting.html#common-devices","Device Function Grapnel launcher Fires an anchored line up to 5 meters per rating. Climbing the line needs no roll. Smoke canister Set it off, or rig it to a tripwire: it fills a small area with smoke, which is Obscured for one minute. Flash mine Set it off, or rig it to a tripwire. It rolls against the Dexterity + Stamina of everyone in a small area; each one it beats is Disoriented and takes −2d on sight-based rolls until the end of their next turn. Sentry crossbow Set it to watch a doorway or a path. Once per round, it attacks the first creature to enter, rolling its dice, for 2 lethal damage. Each shot spends a charge. Alarm ward Set it over a doorway or a path. It sounds when anyone passes; noticing and avoiding it takes a Stealth roll against the Device's roll. Lock breaker Clamp it to a lock. Over one minute, it opens the lock using its roll in place of Dexterity + Thievery.",""],["Rules","Milestones","Advancement › Milestones","advancement.html#milestones","The GM calls a milestone when a story arc closes or at a major turning point, usually once per session in a short story and every one or two sessions in a longer one. Every character advances at the same time. At each milestone, every character: Gains 1 talent. Makes 2 choices from the advancement pool.",""],["Rules","Gains 1 talent","Advancement › Milestones","advancement.html#milestones","Gains 1 talent. ",""],["Rules","Makes 2 choices","Advancement › Milestones","advancement.html#milestones","Makes 2 choices from the advancement pool. ",""],["Rules","The talent","Advancement › Milestones","advancement.html#the-talent","Each milestone gives one of: a talent from any vocation you have (its after tag and other prerequisites must be met), a new vocation's key talent, after which its trees are open to you, a further community trait from any community you belong to (its prerequisites must be met), or a new community's key trait (see Joining another community). The GM and player should find an in-world reason for it.",""],["Rules","The advancement pool","Advancement › Milestones","advancement.html#the-advancement-pool","Each choice marks one box on an option. Option Boxes You gain Skills ☐ ☐ ☐ ☐ 2 skill dots in different skills rated below 3, or 1 dot in a skill rated 3 or higher Specialties ☐ ☐ 1 specialty, or 1 improvement to a specialty you have Training ☐ ☐ Another talent (as above) Toughness ☐ +1 Health Nerve ☐ +1 Stress box Attribute [☐ ☐ ☐] [☐ ☐ ☐] +1 attribute dot Single boxes (☐) give their benefit as soon as you mark them. Chained boxes ([☐ ☐ ☐]) give their benefit only when the last box of the chain is marked. You can spread a chain across milestones: for example, mark two attribute boxes at one milestone and the third at the next. Full options: once every box of an option is marked, you can't choose it again this cycle. New cycle: the pool has 16 boxes, but a cycle is only 10 choices (5 milestones). After your 10th choice, clear the pool and start again. You'll never fill everything in one cycle, so each cycle you decide what to focus on. An unfinished chain keeps its marked boxes into the next cycle.",""],["Rules","Single boxes","Advancement › The advancement pool","advancement.html#the-advancement-pool","Single boxes (☐) give their benefit as soon as you mark them. ",""],["Rules","Chained boxes","Advancement › The advancement pool","advancement.html#the-advancement-pool","Chained boxes ([☐ ☐ ☐]) give their benefit only when the last box of the chain is marked. You can spread a chain across milestones: for example, mark two attribute boxes at one milestone and the third at the next. ",""],["Rules","Full options","Advancement › The advancement pool","advancement.html#the-advancement-pool","Full options: once every box of an option is marked, you can't choose it again this cycle. ",""],["Rules","New cycle","Advancement › The advancement pool","advancement.html#the-advancement-pool","New cycle: the pool has 16 boxes, but a cycle is only 10 choices (5 milestones). After your 10th choice, clear the pool and start again. You'll never fill everything in one cycle, so each cycle you decide what to focus on. An unfinished chain keeps its marked boxes into the next cycle. ",""],["Rules","Limits","Advancement › Milestones","advancement.html#limits","Skills: the creation cap of 3 no longer applies; skills can reach 5. Specialties: each improvement adds +1d, up to +3d and no higher than the skill's permanent dots (see Specialties). Attributes: no attribute can go above 5. Tiers stay informative only. For example, each 5-milestone cycle could mark a new tier.",""],["Rules","Skills","Advancement › Limits","advancement.html#limits","Skills: the creation cap of 3 no longer applies; skills can reach 5. ",""],["Rules","Specialties","Advancement › Limits","advancement.html#limits","Specialties: each improvement adds +1d, up to +3d and no higher than the skill's permanent dots (see Specialties). ",""],["Rules","Attributes","Advancement › Limits","advancement.html#limits","Attributes: no attribute can go above 5. ",""],["Rules","Weeks of downtime","Downtime › Weeks of downtime","downtime.html#weeks-of-downtime","Downtime is counted in weeks. The GM decides how many weeks the party has before the next adventure starts, or before something interrupts them. A week of downtime includes its nights' rests; don't run them separately. Each week, every character takes two downtime actions, then the week ends. Repeating actions: you can take the same action more than once in a week, but each character can receive Treat only once per week. Teamwork works on downtime rolls as usual. No Stress: downtime rolls span days of steady work, not a burst of effort. You can't push yourself on them or pay any other Stress cost on them, including talents and racial traits. Complications in downtime can follow the characters into the next adventure: a rival, a debt, a rumor, an unhappy client.",""],["Rules","Repeating actions","Downtime › Weeks of downtime","downtime.html#weeks-of-downtime","Repeating actions: you can take the same action more than once in a week, but each character can receive Treat only once per week. ",""],["Rules","Teamwork","Downtime › Weeks of downtime","downtime.html#weeks-of-downtime","Teamwork works on downtime rolls as usual. ",""],["Rules","No Stress","Downtime › Weeks of downtime","downtime.html#weeks-of-downtime","No Stress: downtime rolls span days of steady work, not a burst of effort. You can't push yourself on them or pay any other Stress cost on them, including talents and racial traits. ",""],["Rules","Complications","Downtime › Weeks of downtime","downtime.html#weeks-of-downtime","Complications in downtime can follow the characters into the next adventure: a rival, a debt, a rumor, an unhappy client. ",""],["Rules","Lifestyle","Downtime › Weeks of downtime","downtime.html#lifestyle","Each week, every character chooses how they live and pays its upkeep. A character with a home in the settlement (their community lives there, or a patron houses them) lives Modestly for free, and pays only the difference to live better. Lifestyle Upkeep per week Effect Rough Free A stable loft, a doorway, a friend's floor. The week clears only 1 Stress, and refills only 1 charge on each kit and Device. Modest 1 handful A boarding house or a cheap inn. The week ends as normal. Comfortable 3 handfuls A good inn or rented rooms, with quiet and space to work. +1d to Project rolls. If it's your last week of downtime, you start the next adventure Determined. Lavish 1 bag A fine house, servants, the best tables in town. Everything Comfortable gives, and +1 downtime action that week: servants and hired hands take care of what would otherwise eat your days. You can also pay 1 more bag for one more extra action that week, but no more: money can hire help, but it can't add days to the week. +1d to Cultivate and Gather information among the wealthy and powerful.",""],["Rules","End of the week","Downtime › Weeks of downtime","downtime.html#end-of-the-week","At the end of each week of downtime, every character: clears all Stress (only 1 when living Rough), heals all bashing and lethal damage and 1 aggravated wound (natural healing), removes all stacks of Exhausted, gets everything Prepare gives, and more: consumables restocked, all armor Wear cleared, and every kit and Device refilled to full charges, refreshes their once-per-day abilities. Lost armor rating, broken gear and defects aren't fixed on their own: they need a Project (below).",""],["Rules","Downtime actions","Downtime › Downtime actions","downtime.html#downtime-actions","Treat: Roll Intelligence + Medicine on a patient, giving them long-term care. On a success, they heal 1 aggravated wound. On a success with Opportunity, they heal 2 aggravated wounds instead. A medical kit gives +1d as usual. You can treat yourself. Recuperate: Roll Resolve + Composure as you let go of what you've been through. On a success, heal 1 Tenacity. On a success with Opportunity, heal 2 Tenacity instead. This is the only way Tenacity heals (see Tenacity and madness). It never reduces a Scar, including Dusking. Project: Work on an extended action: crafting or repairing an item, research in an archive, forging papers, training an animal. Each Project action gives rolls according to the task's interval (below). All the extended action's rules apply, including crafting's attempt limit, except one: a roll with no successes doesn't cost Stress. It ends this action's rolls instead, and you can pick the work up again with a later Project action. Work: Roll an Attribute + Skill that fits a trade you can ply: Crafts for selling goods, Expression for performing, Medicine for tending the sick, Streetwise for running errands no one asks about. Each success earns 1 handful. On a success",""],["Rules","Treat","Downtime › Downtime actions","downtime.html#downtime-actions","Treat: Roll Intelligence + Medicine on a patient, giving them long-term care. On a success, they heal 1 aggravated wound. On a success with Opportunity, they heal 2 aggravated wounds instead. A medical kit gives +1d as usual. You can treat yourself. ",""],["Rules","Recuperate","Downtime › Downtime actions","downtime.html#downtime-actions","Recuperate: Roll Resolve + Composure as you let go of what you've been through. On a success, heal 1 Tenacity. On a success with Opportunity, heal 2 Tenacity instead. This is the only way Tenacity heals (see Tenacity and madness). It never reduces a Scar, including Dusking. ",""],["Rules","Project","Downtime › Downtime actions","downtime.html#downtime-actions","Project: Work on an extended action: crafting or repairing an item, research in an archive, forging papers, training an animal. Each Project action gives rolls according to the task's interval (below). All the extended action's rules apply, including crafting's attempt limit, except one: a roll with no successes doesn't cost Stress. It ends this action's rolls instead, and you can pick the work up again with a later Project action. ",""],["Rules","Work","Downtime › Downtime actions","downtime.html#downtime-actions","Work: Roll an Attribute + Skill that fits a trade you can ply: Crafts for selling goods, Expression for performing, Medicine for tending the sick, Streetwise for running errands no one asks about. Each success earns 1 handful. On a success with Opportunity, you also make a useful contact or hear a lead, at the GM's call. ",""],["Rules","Gather information","Downtime › Downtime actions","downtime.html#downtime-actions","Gather information: Roll Wits or Intelligence + Investigation, Academics, Streetwise or Socialize, whichever fits how you ask around. On a success, the GM answers one question about a person, place, group or threat truthfully. On a success with Opportunity, also gain an edge: +1d on your first roll that acts on what you learned. ",""],["Rules","Cultivate","Downtime › Downtime actions","downtime.html#downtime-actions","Cultivate: Roll Manipulation or Presence + Socialize, Persuasion or Streetwise, spending time with a person or group: a patron, a guild, your community. On a success, gain 1 favor with them. On a success with Opportunity, gain 2 favors, or 1 favor and a lasting contact. ",""],["Rules","Project intervals","Downtime › Downtime actions","downtime.html#project-intervals","Task's interval Rolls per Project action Hour As many as the task needs Day Up to 3 Week 1",""],["Rules","Favors","Downtime › Downtime actions","downtime.html#favors","A favor is something a person or group owes you. Spend it, with the GM's approval, for one piece of help they can reasonably give: an introduction, a loan of gear, a door opened, a blind eye turned, a night's shelter on the run. A favor doesn't make anyone act against their own interests. Favors last until they're spent, unless the story breaks the relationship.",""],["Setting","Duskworld primer","Duskworld","settings/duskworld/index.html","Long ago, humans and kehars shared Eladria in uneasy peace, until a handful of kehars reached for power the world couldn't give them and something answered. The krens came. What began as gifts became corruption, and for a hundred years, remembered as the Era of Sorrow, Eladria belonged to them: people enslaved, hunted, sacrificed, while kehars fled to the fringes and hid. Then the Allmother rose out of the last of humanity's hope and drove the krens back far enough to raise Dawnkeep, the first Free City. Her own First Followers murdered her for more power than she'd give them, an act so vile it left them wandering shells of themselves, still out there somewhere. In her place, the Dusk came. The Dusk The Dusk isn't simple darkness. It's corruption that spreads through reality itself. Wherever it reaches, color drains out, the land withers, and magic and runetech turn unpredictable: a ward flickers, a silo spoils its grain, a machine runs harder, weaker, or does something its makers never meant. Stay in it too long and it changes you, if it doesn't kill you first. Plants grow strange and enormous, animals turn monstrous, people lose pieces of their mind, or themselves. The ruined lan","duskworld"],["Setting","History","Duskworld › History","settings/duskworld/overview.html#history","Once, the world was full of light and color. Then Eladria succumbed to the Dusk and the grey. Long ago, humans and kehars lived throughout Eladria, mostly in peace. But some kehars sought powers beyond what the world could offer, and strange beings from the unknown answered them: the krens. Their gifts became corruption, and the krens spread across the world, turning Eladria into a realm of demons and monsters. During the Era of Sorrow, the people of Eladria were enslaved and hunted. Humans were gathered into camps and prisons and sacrificed in dark rituals. Kehars fled to the fringes of civilization and hid from their new masters. When all seemed lost, the Light appeared. The Allmother manifested from the hopes and prayers of the people and drove back the krens. The survivors pushed the krens far enough away to found Dawnkeep, the first Free City. The victory didn't last. The Allmother's First Followers, unsatisfied with the power she had given them, murdered her. The act stripped them of what remained of their humanity, and they still wander somewhere as shells of their former selves. With the Allmother's death, the Dusk came.","duskworld"],["Setting","The Dusk","Duskworld › The Dusk","settings/duskworld/overview.html#the-dusk","The Dusk isn't simply darkness. It corrupts reality itself. Where it spreads, the world loses its color. Light dims, the land withers, and magic and runetech turn unpredictable: wards flicker, silos spoil their grain, and machines run harder, weaker, or do things their makers never meant. It twists living things. Plants mutate, animals become monstrous, and people exposed to it for too long may lose their minds or their humanity. Few understand what the Dusk is, or why it came with the Allmother's death.","duskworld"],["Setting","The Dusklands","Duskworld › The Dusk","settings/duskworld/overview.html#the-dusklands","The lands touched by the Dusk. The ground is dark grey, the vegetation black and twisted, and everything that survives has changed. Plants may grow enormous, grow strange materials in place of wood, or gain rudimentary sentience. Dusktouched: animals and people changed by the Dusk's corruption. Krens and their creations still roam the wilds. Duskborn: powerful horrors born from the Dusk itself. Some people live in the Dusklands, surviving alongside the monsters, protected from corruption by magic and sacrifice. They're called Duskroamers.","duskworld"],["Setting","Dusktouched","Duskworld › The Dusklands","settings/duskworld/overview.html#the-dusklands","Dusktouched: animals and people changed by the Dusk's corruption. ","duskworld"],["Setting","Krens","Duskworld › The Dusklands","settings/duskworld/overview.html#the-dusklands","Krens and their creations still roam the wilds. ","duskworld"],["Setting","Duskborn","Duskworld › The Dusklands","settings/duskworld/overview.html#the-dusklands","Duskborn: powerful horrors born from the Dusk itself. ","duskworld"],["Setting","Mendris","Duskworld › Mendris","settings/duskworld/overview.html#mendris","One of the last great bastions of civilization: the capital Dawnkeep and the fortified farmsteads around it, the Frontiers. The Great Houses hold the real power. Beacons: massive towers that combine the Allmother's Light with ancient kren technology. They keep the Dusk away from Dawnkeep and the farmsteads. Rigid society: social status is hard to change. Farmsteaders stay farmsteaders and cityfolk stay cityfolk. In Dawnkeep, your Sector decides much of your standing, your work and your circles. Controlled travel: movement between regions is tightly controlled, for order and for protection from what lies beyond the Beacons.","duskworld"],["Setting","Beacons","Duskworld › Mendris","settings/duskworld/overview.html#mendris","Beacons: massive towers that combine the Allmother's Light with ancient kren technology. They keep the Dusk away from Dawnkeep and the farmsteads. ","duskworld"],["Setting","Rigid society","Duskworld › Mendris","settings/duskworld/overview.html#mendris","Rigid society: social status is hard to change. Farmsteaders stay farmsteaders and cityfolk stay cityfolk. In Dawnkeep, your Sector decides much of your standing, your work and your circles. ","duskworld"],["Setting","Controlled travel","Duskworld › Mendris","settings/duskworld/overview.html#mendris","Controlled travel: movement between regions is tightly controlled, for order and for protection from what lies beyond the Beacons. ","duskworld"],["Setting","The Frontiers","Duskworld › Mendris","settings/duskworld/overview.html#the-frontiers","Simple, communal and dangerous. Most people work the fields or serve in the security forces that protect the settlements. Others keep the farmsteads running: runesmiths, wardwrights, artisans, hunters, and engineers who maintain the Beacons, machinery and grain silos. The silos use runetech to preserve and purify food, so the Frontiers are largely self-sufficient. Humans and kehars in the Frontiers lead similar lives. After generations of shared hardship, kehars are usually treated like their human neighbors, though old prejudices remain.","duskworld"],["Setting","Dawnkeep","Duskworld › Mendris","settings/duskworld/overview.html#dawnkeep","Broader, wealthier and more complicated. A city of rivers, canals, factories, markets, trains, Beacons and ancient technology, divided into Sectors with their own character and hierarchy. Most residents are laborers or artisans. Those born in prominent Sectors have better access to administrative, clerical, logistical and governmental positions. With wealth, connections or the right family name, people rise further: merchants who travel across Mendris, shareholders in trading companies, members of the institutions that control the city. The world is dying, but Dawnkeep is still alive.","duskworld"],["Setting","Travel papers","Duskworld › Mendris","settings/duskworld/overview.html#travel-papers","Movement between regions of Mendris is controlled, and travel papers say where you may go. Most people only hold papers for their own region: Frontier folk for the Frontiers, guildfolk for the working Sectors, caravaners for the trade routes. Gentry hold full papers. Duskroamers have none. Checkpoints guard Beacon gates, Sector boundaries, train stations and the roads between settlements. Without the right papers, you're turned back, or detained if you're caught somewhere you shouldn't be. Ways around it: a bribe (a handful, more for a serious checkpoint), forged papers (an extended action with Academics or Expression, helped by a writing case), sneaking past (Stealth), or a patron who vouches for you. Firearms are legal only for Gentry, the Houses and the security forces. Anyone else caught with one faces the same trouble as having no papers, or worse.","duskworld"],["Setting","Checkpoints","Duskworld › Travel papers","settings/duskworld/overview.html#travel-papers","Checkpoints guard Beacon gates, Sector boundaries, train stations and the roads between settlements. ","duskworld"],["Setting","Without the right papers,","Duskworld › Travel papers","settings/duskworld/overview.html#travel-papers","Without the right papers, you're turned back, or detained if you're caught somewhere you shouldn't be. Ways around it: a bribe (a handful, more for a serious checkpoint), forged papers (an extended action with Academics or Expression, helped by a writing case), sneaking past (Stealth), or a patron who vouches for you. ","duskworld"],["Setting","Firearms","Duskworld › Travel papers","settings/duskworld/overview.html#travel-papers","Firearms are legal only for Gentry, the Houses and the security forces. Anyone else caught with one faces the same trouble as having no papers, or worse. ","duskworld"],["Setting","Life in Eladria","Duskworld › Life in Eladria","settings/duskworld/overview.html#life-in-eladria","Everyone knows civilization lives on borrowed time. The Beacons hold back the Dusk, the Frontiers provide food, hunters venture into the Dusklands for resources, soldiers protect the settlements, artisans maintain the machinery, and merchants move goods between distant communities. Beyond all of it lies the Dusk. Some stay behind the walls. Others venture beyond them. The Allmother's mantra. Said at every curfew bell: \"Her light is our home. Her light protects.\"","duskworld"],["Setting","Institutions and places (working canon)","Duskworld › Institutions and places (working canon)","settings/duskworld/overview.html#institutions-and-places-working-canon","Established through play. Treat as a strong starting point rather than fixed canon. House Callarain: one of the Great Houses. Its engineers' guild builds and maintains Beacon technology and controls apprenticeships into the trade. House Shantari: one of the Great Houses, a shanat family. Its kren blood passed down the old codes that let them read and run kren factories nobody else understands. The Sixth House (the Syndicate): an underworld power beside, or beneath, the official Houses. Crime, debts, favors and leverage; it hires people for jobs it can't be seen doing. The Gutterlord: a crime lord of the Fork, with an estate where he holds boxing nights. The IEE: investigates and reports on deaths, disappearances and accidents across Mendris. Thorough when the people involved are well connected; thin or silent when the missing are poor, or broke curfew. Curfew: settlements ring a curfew bell at dusk. People who go out after it often don't come back. Whether that's the Dusk reaching past the Beacons or something else is unknown; it's treated as grim common knowledge, not superstition. The curse of the Dusk: a recognized affliction from prolonged exposure or corruption, distinct from ","duskworld"],["Setting","House Callarain","Duskworld › Institutions and places (working canon)","settings/duskworld/overview.html#institutions-and-places-working-canon","House Callarain: one of the Great Houses. Its engineers' guild builds and maintains Beacon technology and controls apprenticeships into the trade. ","duskworld"],["Setting","House Shantari","Duskworld › Institutions and places (working canon)","settings/duskworld/overview.html#institutions-and-places-working-canon","House Shantari: one of the Great Houses, a shanat family. Its kren blood passed down the old codes that let them read and run kren factories nobody else understands. ","duskworld"],["Setting","The Sixth House (the Syndicate)","Duskworld › Institutions and places (working canon)","settings/duskworld/overview.html#institutions-and-places-working-canon","The Sixth House (the Syndicate): an underworld power beside, or beneath, the official Houses. Crime, debts, favors and leverage; it hires people for jobs it can't be seen doing. ","duskworld"],["Setting","The Gutterlord","Duskworld › Institutions and places (working canon)","settings/duskworld/overview.html#institutions-and-places-working-canon","The Gutterlord: a crime lord of the Fork, with an estate where he holds boxing nights. ","duskworld"],["Setting","The IEE","Duskworld › Institutions and places (working canon)","settings/duskworld/overview.html#institutions-and-places-working-canon","The IEE: investigates and reports on deaths, disappearances and accidents across Mendris. Thorough when the people involved are well connected; thin or silent when the missing are poor, or broke curfew. ","duskworld"],["Setting","Curfew","Duskworld › Institutions and places (working canon)","settings/duskworld/overview.html#institutions-and-places-working-canon","Curfew: settlements ring a curfew bell at dusk. People who go out after it often don't come back. Whether that's the Dusk reaching past the Beacons or something else is unknown; it's treated as grim common knowledge, not superstition. ","duskworld"],["Setting","The curse of the Dusk","Duskworld › Institutions and places (working canon)","settings/duskworld/overview.html#institutions-and-places-working-canon","The curse of the Dusk: a recognized affliction from prolonged exposure or corruption, distinct from being taken outright. It can be formally tested for. Those who fail, or are suspected, are usually sent somewhere like Brightgate. In the rules, the curse is the Dusking Scar. ","duskworld"],["Setting","Taken by the Dusk","Duskworld › Institutions and places (working canon)","settings/duskworld/overview.html#institutions-and-places-working-canon","Taken by the Dusk: any death that leaves no sign of physical harm, when the victim had the curse, is said to be the Dusk taking them. ","duskworld"],["Setting","Brightgate","Duskworld › Institutions and places (working canon)","settings/duskworld/overview.html#institutions-and-places-working-canon","Brightgate: a mental institution in a rehabilitated old mansion, where people suspected of the curse or deemed unwell are confined, sometimes for years. It's said to hold more restless presences than it was meant to treat. ","duskworld"],["Setting","The Fork","Duskworld › Institutions and places (working canon)","settings/duskworld/overview.html#institutions-and-places-working-canon","The Fork: a poor, rough district of tenements, workhouses and gang territory, where parish churches do more good than the authorities. Smaller supporting Beacons hold it against the Dusk. ","duskworld"],["Setting","Southbank","Duskworld › Institutions and places (working canon)","settings/duskworld/overview.html#institutions-and-places-working-canon","Southbank: Dawnkeep's docklands. Trade, smuggling, and the place goods (sometimes whole convoys) go missing before anyone official notices. ","duskworld"],["Setting","Premise","Duskworld › Premise","settings/duskworld/stories/the-dimming.html#premise","One of the smaller supporting Beacons in the Fork has been waning for weeks and threatens to go out, which would let the Dusk into the district. A repair device was sent to fix it, but it was stolen by the Gutterlord, a crime lord of the Fork. The party is sent to steal it back during a boxing night he holds at the back of his estate, then get it to the Beacon and repair it.","duskworld"],["Setting","Structure","Duskworld › Structure","settings/duskworld/stories/the-dimming.html#structure","Part Sessions Content Part one: Fight night 1–2 The heist at the Gutterlord's estate during the boxing night: get in, find the device, get out. Part two: Into the grey 1–2 Carry the device through the Fork to the failing Beacon and repair it, while the Dusk looms over them.","duskworld"],["Setting","Open questions","Duskworld › Open questions","settings/duskworld/stories/the-dimming.html#open-questions","Who hires the party. The primer leaves it vague on purpose. Why the Gutterlord stole the device. The repair device's rules: whether it's a Device (Duskworld core components are still undecided) or a story item, and how the repair is rolled (likely an extended action with Technology or Crafts). Dusk exposure in the Fork: how the exposure level rises as the Beacon fails (for example, Faint after curfew rising toward Heavy). Shanat powers: not written yet; a shanat character currently only has Night Sight.","duskworld"],["Setting","Who hires the party","Duskworld › Open questions","settings/duskworld/stories/the-dimming.html#open-questions","Who hires the party. The primer leaves it vague on purpose. ","duskworld"],["Setting","Why the Gutterlord stole the device","Duskworld › Open questions","settings/duskworld/stories/the-dimming.html#open-questions","Why the Gutterlord stole the device. ","duskworld"],["Setting","The repair device's rules","Duskworld › Open questions","settings/duskworld/stories/the-dimming.html#open-questions","The repair device's rules: whether it's a Device (Duskworld core components are still undecided) or a story item, and how the repair is rolled (likely an extended action with Technology or Crafts). ","duskworld"],["Setting","Dusk exposure in the Fork","Duskworld › Open questions","settings/duskworld/stories/the-dimming.html#open-questions","Dusk exposure in the Fork: how the exposure level rises as the Beacon fails (for example, Faint after curfew rising toward Heavy). ","duskworld"],["Setting","Shanat powers","Duskworld › Open questions","settings/duskworld/stories/the-dimming.html#open-questions","Shanat powers: not written yet; a shanat character currently only has Night Sight. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Glints and boxings","Character Creation › Wealth › Glints and boxings","character-creation.html#glints-and-boxings","The money of Mendris comes from glints: cube-like sources of power built by the krens. There are a great many of them, but no one can make true glints. Mendris's workshops make weaker copies, forged glints; the true kren glints are found only in the abandoned kren factories of the Dusklands. Glints also power every Device (see Glints and Devices). Mendris's currency is cut from empty glints. The cut pieces are called boxings. Duskworld names the three wealth units after them: Unit Duskworld name Worth Handful A handful of boxings Everyday money Bag A slab: a large uncut piece of an empty glint 10 handfuls of boxings Chest A shell: a whole empty glint, never cut 10 slabs Most people only ever handle boxings. Merchants and caravans deal in slabs. Shells belong to the Houses and the trading companies.","duskworld"],["Rules","Human","Character Creation › Human › Human","character-creation.html#duskworld-human","Humans are found throughout Mendris and much of Eladria. They come in many shapes and forms, but their greatest strength is adaptability. They thrive in communities, use whatever resources are available, and have repeatedly survived circumstances that should have destroyed them. Humans use the racial traits in the core rules (Driven and Fortune's Favor).","duskworld"],["Rules","Kehar","Character Creation › Human › Kehar","character-creation.html#kehar","Kehars are said to have been born from dreams, and a rare few can shape their dreams into reality. In a world that is steadily losing its color, kehars remain vibrant and colorful. Their appearance and culture vary between communities. In Mendris, kehars are sometimes regarded with suspicion: some believe they're still connected to the krens because of what their ancestors did. As a result, kehars often form close-knit communities and rely heavily on one another. In the Frontiers, generations of shared hardship mean they're usually treated like their human neighbors. Primary: Lucid. To a kehar, a bad outcome is only a dream that can be dreamed again. After you roll, you can reroll any number of dice that didn't succeed, taking 1 Stress per die. You can't reroll the stakes die, or a chance die that rolled a 1. Secondary: Dreaming Calm. A part of every kehar is always dreaming, and the waking world weighs on them a little less. Once per hour, you can become Determined (your next Stress cost is reduced by 1).","duskworld"],["Traits","Lucid","Character Creation › Human › Kehar","character-creation.html#kehar","Primary: Lucid. To a kehar, a bad outcome is only a dream that can be dreamed again. After you roll, you can reroll any number of dice that didn't succeed, taking 1 Stress per die. You can't reroll the stakes die, or a chance die that rolled a 1. ","duskworld"],["Traits","Dreaming Calm","Character Creation › Human › Kehar","character-creation.html#kehar","Secondary: Dreaming Calm. A part of every kehar is always dreaming, and the waking world weighs on them a little less. Once per hour, you can become Determined (your next Stress cost is reduced by 1). ","duskworld"],["Rules","Shanat","Character Creation › Human › Shanat","character-creation.html#shanat","Shanats descend from humans and kehars touched by kren blood. Their ancestry is complicated, and most shanats don't discuss their origins openly. The blood manifests in different ways: some shanats develop supernatural abilities tied to aspects of darkness, while others show only subtle physical peculiarities. Kren blood is feared in Mendris, and using its powers is forbidden, so shanats who have them use them in secret. The Shantari, one of the Great Houses, are a shanat family. Their kren connection passed down the old codes: secret codes and keys that let them read and run kren factories and machines that remain a mystery to the rest of Mendris. This knowledge is taught, not inherited: a shanat raised outside the family doesn't have it. Shanat blood is dominant. A child with any Shanat parent is Shanat, and the bloodline never shows up as a mere heritage. Every Shanat still carries a human or kehar bloodline, from a parent or from older ancestry resurfacing. Shanat have only a primary trait. A Shanat character always takes a different race as their heritage, and Shanat can't be chosen as a heritage. Primary: Night Sight. You ignore dice penalties from dim light and darkness, but","duskworld"],["Traits","Night Sight","Character Creation › Human › Shanat","character-creation.html#shanat","Primary: Night Sight. You ignore dice penalties from dim light and darkness, but not from total darkness. The deep Dusklands count as total darkness. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Frontier Folk","Character Creation › 2. Community › Frontier Folk","character-creation.html#frontier-folk","The people of the fortified farmsteads around Dawnkeep: working the fields, standing watch, and living close to the edge of the Dusklands. Community skills: Survival, Athletics or Animal Ken. Starting gear: Work tools, a lantern, and travel papers for the Frontiers. Key: Farmstead Kin. The farmsteads look after their own. You count as having a patron among Frontier folk, and at any farmstead you can find food, shelter and a sympathetic ear. Watchful Sleeper after Farmstead Kin Wits 3+: Nights on the edge of the Dusklands taught you to wake at the wrong sound. You can't be Surprised while resting, and once per hour you can react while Surprised. Field-Hardened after Farmstead Kin Stamina 3+: Once per day, remove 1 stack of Exhausted.","duskworld"],["Traits","Farmstead Kin","Character Creation › 2. Community › Frontier Folk","character-creation.html#farmstead-kin","Key trait · The farmsteads look after their own. You count as having a patron among Frontier folk, and at any farmstead you can find food, shelter and a sympathetic ear.","duskworld"],["Traits","Watchful Sleeper","Character Creation › 2. Community › Frontier Folk","character-creation.html#frontier-folk-watchful-sleeper","Wits 3+ · Nights on the edge of the Dusklands taught you to wake at the wrong sound. You can't be Surprised while resting, and once per hour you can react while Surprised.","duskworld"],["Traits","Field-Hardened","Character Creation › 2. Community › Frontier Folk","character-creation.html#frontier-folk-field-hardened","Stamina 3+ · Once per day, remove 1 stack of Exhausted.","duskworld"],["Rules","Gentry","Character Creation › 2. Community › Gentry","character-creation.html#gentry","The administrators, officials and old families of Dawnkeep's prominent Sectors. Community skills: Politics, Academics or Persuasion. Starting gear: Fine clothes, full travel papers, and firearm permission: when your vocation gives you a weapon, you can take a pistol or rifle for one of them. Key: Sector Standing. You count as having a patron in your Sector's circles, and in high society. Clerks, officials and guards treat you as someone who matters: you can get an audience, a permit or a hearing that others would wait weeks for. Family Purse after Sector Standing: You live comfortably and don't track ordinary expenses: anything that costs a handful or less costs you nothing (see Wealth). Take 2 Stress to gain an Opportunity on a roll to get something with money or influence. Educated after Sector Standing Intelligence 3+: Gain an Academics or Politics specialty. When you roll an untrained Mental skill, you take −2d instead of −3d.","duskworld"],["Traits","Sector Standing","Character Creation › 2. Community › Gentry","character-creation.html#sector-standing","Key trait · You count as having a patron in your Sector's circles, and in high society. Clerks, officials and guards treat you as someone who matters: you can get an audience, a permit or a hearing that others would wait weeks for.","duskworld"],["Traits","Family Purse","Character Creation › 2. Community › Gentry","character-creation.html#gentry-family-purse","You live comfortably and don't track ordinary expenses: anything that costs a handful or less costs you nothing (see Wealth). Take 2 Stress to gain an Opportunity on a roll to get something with money or influence.","duskworld"],["Traits","Educated","Character Creation › 2. Community › Gentry","character-creation.html#gentry-educated","Intelligence 3+ · Gain an Academics or Politics specialty. When you roll an untrained Mental skill, you take −2d instead of −3d.","duskworld"],["Rules","Guildfolk","Character Creation › 2. Community › Guildfolk","character-creation.html#guildfolk","The laborers, artisans and guild members of the working Sectors that keep Dawnkeep running: factories, workshops, canals and markets. Community skills: Crafts, Athletics or Streetwise. Starting gear: A tool kit, a guild token, and travel papers for the working Sectors. Key: Guild Ties. You count as having a patron among the laborers, guilds and unions of the working Sectors. You know how to find work, a bed for the night, or someone who owes the guild a favor. Iron Back after Guild Ties Stamina 3+: Years of hard labor made you tough. Your Beaten Down threshold is 1 higher. Street Sense after Guild Ties Wits 3+: Gain a Streetwise (Dawnkeep) specialty. You always know the fastest way through the working Sectors, and you get +1d to notice when you're being followed or cheated.","duskworld"],["Traits","Guild Ties","Character Creation › 2. Community › Guildfolk","character-creation.html#guild-ties","Key trait · You count as having a patron among the laborers, guilds and unions of the working Sectors. You know how to find work, a bed for the night, or someone who owes the guild a favor.","duskworld"],["Traits","Iron Back","Character Creation › 2. Community › Guildfolk","character-creation.html#guildfolk-iron-back","Stamina 3+ · Years of hard labor made you tough. Your Beaten Down threshold is 1 higher.","duskworld"],["Traits","Street Sense","Character Creation › 2. Community › Guildfolk","character-creation.html#guildfolk-street-sense","Wits 3+ · Gain a Streetwise (Dawnkeep) specialty. You always know the fastest way through the working Sectors, and you get +1d to notice when you're being followed or cheated.","duskworld"],["Rules","Dusklanders","Character Creation › 2. Community › Dusklanders","character-creation.html#dusklanders","The few communities beyond the Beacons that survive in the Dusklands through magic and sacrifice. People who live in the Dusklands are called Duskroamers. Community skills: Survival, Occult or Stealth. Starting gear: A wardwright charm (+1d to Endure the Dusk) and Dusklands clothing. No travel papers. Born under the Dusk: if you grew up among the Dusklanders, you start play with Dusking 1 and the Grey-Weaned Dusk trait (see Born under the Dusk). Every curse test in Mendris finds it. Key: Beyond the Beacons. You count as having a patron among the Dusklands communities, and they treat you as one of their own. You know their safe paths and hidden refuges, and where to find shelter in the Dusklands. Eye for the Strange after Beyond the Beacons Intelligence 3+: You get +1d to identify dusktouched creatures, mutations and Dusk phenomena. On a success, you also learn one weakness or danger. Dusk Sense after Beyond the Beacons Wits 3+: You feel the Dusk. You know an area's exposure level before you enter it, and you can't be Surprised by dusktouched or duskborn creatures.","duskworld"],["Traits","Beyond the Beacons","Character Creation › 2. Community › Dusklanders","character-creation.html#beyond-the-beacons","Key trait · You count as having a patron among the Dusklands communities, and they treat you as one of their own. You know their safe paths and hidden refuges, and where to find shelter in the Dusklands.","duskworld"],["Traits","Eye for the Strange","Character Creation › 2. Community › Dusklanders","character-creation.html#dusklanders-eye-for-the-strange","Intelligence 3+ · You get +1d to identify dusktouched creatures, mutations and Dusk phenomena. On a success, you also learn one weakness or danger.","duskworld"],["Traits","Dusk Sense","Character Creation › 2. Community › Dusklanders","character-creation.html#dusklanders-dusk-sense","Wits 3+ · You feel the Dusk. You know an area's exposure level before you enter it, and you can't be Surprised by dusktouched or duskborn creatures.","duskworld"],["Rules","The Wards","Character Creation › 2. Community › The Wards","character-creation.html#the-wards","Those who passed through an institution rather than a family: an orphanage, a workhouse, a parish home, or a military school. Community skills: Streetwise, Thievery or Academics. Starting gear: One kit of your choice (your institution taught a trade), worn clothes, and papers from your institution. Key: Fellow Wards. You count as having a patron among the others who passed through your institution. They're scattered across Mendris, in every kind of position, and they remember where they came from. Indomitable after Fellow Wards Resolve 3+: You learned not to break. Once per hour, after you lose a contested roll against a Mental or Social effect, reroll your defending dice that didn't succeed. Safe Place after Fellow Wards: You have a hidden place in Dawnkeep that only you know about. When you rest there, you clear 1 extra Stress.","duskworld"],["Traits","Fellow Wards","Character Creation › 2. Community › The Wards","character-creation.html#fellow-wards","Key trait · You count as having a patron among the others who passed through your institution. They're scattered across Mendris, in every kind of position, and they remember where they came from.","duskworld"],["Traits","Indomitable","Character Creation › 2. Community › The Wards","character-creation.html#the-wards-indomitable","Resolve 3+ · You learned not to break. Once per hour, after you lose a contested roll against a Mental or Social effect, reroll your defending dice that didn't succeed.","duskworld"],["Traits","Safe Place","Character Creation › 2. Community › The Wards","character-creation.html#the-wards-safe-place","You have a hidden place in Dawnkeep that only you know about. When you rest there, you clear 1 extra Stress.","duskworld"],["Rules","Caravaners","Character Creation › 2. Community › Caravaners","character-creation.html#caravaners","The merchants and drivers of the caravans that carry goods between the settlements of Mendris, always on the move. Community skills: Persuasion, Drive or Survival. Starting gear: A survival kit or climbing gear, and travel papers for the trade routes. Key: Road Contacts. You count as having a patron along the trade routes. In any settlement a caravan stops at, you know someone who can offer news, lodging or passage. Tongues of the Road after Road Contacts Persuasion 2+: Gain a Persuasion (Bargaining) specialty, and +1d to Socialize. Road-Wise after Road Contacts Survival 2+: You get +1d when you Forage or Scout.","duskworld"],["Traits","Road Contacts","Character Creation › 2. Community › Caravaners","character-creation.html#road-contacts","Key trait · You count as having a patron along the trade routes. In any settlement a caravan stops at, you know someone who can offer news, lodging or passage.","duskworld"],["Traits","Tongues of the Road","Character Creation › 2. Community › Caravaners","character-creation.html#caravaners-tongues-of-the-road","Persuasion 2+ · Gain a Persuasion (Bargaining) specialty, and +1d to Socialize.","duskworld"],["Traits","Road-Wise","Character Creation › 2. Community › Caravaners","character-creation.html#caravaners-road-wise","Survival 2+ · You get +1d when you Forage or Scout.","duskworld"],["Rules","Exposure","Core Rules › Hazards › Exposure","rules/damage-and-health.html#exposure","Every place has an exposure level. While characters are in it, they Endure the Dusk at the interval shown. Level Where Endure every Successes needed None Behind the Beacons, in daylight — — Faint Outside after curfew, the edges of the Beacons' reach Day 1 Heavy The Dusklands 4 hours 2 Deep The deep Dusklands, duskborn lairs Hour 3 Enduring the Dusk: roll Resolve + Composure, needing the level's successes. Success: nothing happens. Failure: take Stress equal to the shortfall (the successes you were missing). It's the grey pressing on your mind. Like any Stress, it can overflow into Tenacity damage, but overflow never counts as damage from the Dusk. Complication, or any failure at Deep: also take 1 Tenacity damage from the Dusk (see Dusking). Duskborn and some kren creations can also deal Tenacity damage from the Dusk with their abilities. Protection: Grey-Weaned (Dusk trait, Dusking 1): Stress from Dusk exposure is reduced by 1. Hollow Calm (Dusk trait): once per day, a failed check costs no Stress. Dusk Sense (Dusklanders): you know an area's exposure level before you enter it. Wards and the Allmother's light: a wardwright's charm, or light from the Allmother, gives +1d to Endure t","duskworld"],["Rules","Success","Core Rules › Hazards › Exposure","rules/damage-and-health.html#exposure","Success: nothing happens. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Failure","Core Rules › Hazards › Exposure","rules/damage-and-health.html#exposure","Failure: take Stress equal to the shortfall (the successes you were missing). It's the grey pressing on your mind. Like any Stress, it can overflow into Tenacity damage, but overflow never counts as damage from the Dusk. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Complication, or any failure at Deep","Core Rules › Hazards › Exposure","rules/damage-and-health.html#exposure","Complication, or any failure at Deep: also take 1 Tenacity damage from the Dusk (see Dusking). ","duskworld"],["Rules","Grey-Weaned","Core Rules › Hazards › Exposure","rules/damage-and-health.html#exposure","Grey-Weaned (Dusk trait, Dusking 1): Stress from Dusk exposure is reduced by 1. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Hollow Calm","Core Rules › Hazards › Exposure","rules/damage-and-health.html#exposure","Hollow Calm (Dusk trait): once per day, a failed check costs no Stress. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Dusk Sense","Core Rules › Hazards › Exposure","rules/damage-and-health.html#exposure","Dusk Sense (Dusklanders): you know an area's exposure level before you enter it. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Wards and the Allmother's light","Core Rules › Hazards › Exposure","rules/damage-and-health.html#exposure","Wards and the Allmother's light: a wardwright's charm, or light from the Allmother, gives +1d to Endure the Dusk. Ordinary light (a lamp, a torch, a fire) helps you see, but doesn't hold back the Dusk. A dawnlamp lowers the exposure level around it. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Dusking","Core Rules › Scars › Dusking","rules/damage-and-health.html#dusking","Dusking is the Scar the Dusk leaves (see Scars). It's written on the sheet with its stage, such as Dusking 2, and never heals with rest or downtime. The Dusk takes, but it also gives: each stage brings a Dusk trait. Gaining Dusking: each point of Tenacity damage from the Dusk raises Dusking by 1. Mark the new stage, gain what it brings (below), then roll the Bout of Madness. Penalty to Bouts: Dusking is subtracted from every Bout of Madness roll, whatever caused it: 3d6 − missing Tenacity − Dusking. The curse of the Dusk: having Dusking 1 or more is what Mendris calls the curse. The formal test detects it from stage 1. Stage Effect 1 Nothing visible, but the curse test detects it. Gain the Grey-Weaned Dusk trait. 2 Visible. Choose one Dusk quirk and one Dusk trait (below). Each stage after 2 Choose another quirk and another Dusk trait. Equal to Resolve + Composure Dusktouched. You become one of the Dusk's creatures. The character leaves play and becomes the GM's.","duskworld"],["Rules","Gaining Dusking","Core Rules › Scars › Dusking","rules/damage-and-health.html#dusking","Gaining Dusking: each point of Tenacity damage from the Dusk raises Dusking by 1. Mark the new stage, gain what it brings (below), then roll the Bout of Madness. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Penalty to Bouts","Core Rules › Scars › Dusking","rules/damage-and-health.html#dusking","Penalty to Bouts: Dusking is subtracted from every Bout of Madness roll, whatever caused it: 3d6 − missing Tenacity − Dusking. ","duskworld"],["Rules","The curse of the Dusk","Core Rules › Scars › Dusking","rules/damage-and-health.html#dusking","The curse of the Dusk: having Dusking 1 or more is what Mendris calls the curse. The formal test detects it from stage 1. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Dusk traits","Core Rules › Scars › Dusking","rules/damage-and-health.html#dusk-traits","Dusk traits are gifts of the curse. Once you have every trait below, the GM and player invent new ones that fit. Grey-Weaned (stage 1): The Dusk has less hold on you. Stress you take from Dusk exposure is reduced by 1. Grey Sight: Your eyes have learned the grey. In the deep Dusklands, you see as if in darkness rather than total darkness (−3d to sight rolls instead of a chance die). Night Sight cancels that penalty. Hollow Calm: Something in you has gone quiet. Once per day, when you fail to Endure the Dusk, you take no Stress from it. A Complication or a failure at Deep still deals Tenacity damage. Kindred Dread: Dusktouched and duskborn creatures sense something of their own in you. You get +1d to Stealth to avoid their notice, and the first attack each of them makes against you in a fight takes −1d. Unsettling: People feel the grey on you before they see it. +1d to Intimidation, but anyone who knows the signs recognizes the curse. Dusk quirks. The GM and player choose or invent one that fits. Each is a sign of the curse and comes with a drawback: Drained colour: your eyes, hair or skin turn grey. Anyone who knows the signs recognizes the curse. Wrong shadow: your shadow lags or ","duskworld"],["Traits","Grey-Weaned","Core Rules › Scars › Dusk traits","rules/damage-and-health.html#dusk-traits","Grey-Weaned (stage 1): The Dusk has less hold on you. Stress you take from Dusk exposure is reduced by 1. ","duskworld"],["Traits","Grey Sight","Core Rules › Scars › Dusk traits","rules/damage-and-health.html#dusk-traits","Grey Sight: Your eyes have learned the grey. In the deep Dusklands, you see as if in darkness rather than total darkness (−3d to sight rolls instead of a chance die). Night Sight cancels that penalty. ","duskworld"],["Traits","Hollow Calm","Core Rules › Scars › Dusk traits","rules/damage-and-health.html#dusk-traits","Hollow Calm: Something in you has gone quiet. Once per day, when you fail to Endure the Dusk, you take no Stress from it. A Complication or a failure at Deep still deals Tenacity damage. ","duskworld"],["Traits","Kindred Dread","Core Rules › Scars › Dusk traits","rules/damage-and-health.html#dusk-traits","Kindred Dread: Dusktouched and duskborn creatures sense something of their own in you. You get +1d to Stealth to avoid their notice, and the first attack each of them makes against you in a fight takes −1d. ","duskworld"],["Traits","Unsettling","Core Rules › Scars › Dusk traits","rules/damage-and-health.html#dusk-traits","Unsettling: People feel the grey on you before they see it. +1d to Intimidation, but anyone who knows the signs recognizes the curse. ","duskworld"],["Traits","Drained colour","Core Rules › Scars › Dusk traits","rules/damage-and-health.html#dusk-traits","Drained colour: your eyes, hair or skin turn grey. Anyone who knows the signs recognizes the curse. ","duskworld"],["Traits","Wrong shadow","Core Rules › Scars › Dusk traits","rules/damage-and-health.html#dusk-traits","Wrong shadow: your shadow lags or moves on its own. Animals shy away from you (−1d to Animal Ken). ","duskworld"],["Traits","Cold blood","Core Rules › Scars › Dusk traits","rules/damage-and-health.html#dusk-traits","Cold blood: you're cold to the touch and feel little warmth. −1d to Empathy rolls to comfort or connect. ","duskworld"],["Traits","Beacon-shy","Core Rules › Scars › Dusk traits","rules/damage-and-health.html#dusk-traits","Beacon-shy: a Beacon's light makes your skin crawl. −1d to all rolls while in a Beacon's direct light. ","duskworld"],["Traits","Grey dreams","Core Rules › Scars › Dusk traits","rules/damage-and-health.html#dusk-traits","Grey dreams: you dream of the Dusk. The first time each day you clear Stress at a rest, clear 1 less. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Born under the Dusk","Core Rules › Scars › Dusking","rules/damage-and-health.html#born-under-the-dusk","Characters who grew up among the Dusklanders start play with Dusking 1, and so with Grey-Weaned. They carry the curse from birth: every curse test finds it. Joining the Dusklanders later doesn't give Dusking.","duskworld"],["Rules","Taken by the Dusk","Core Rules › Tenacity and madness › Taken by the Dusk","rules/stress-and-madness.html#taken-by-the-dusk","A character can be lost to the Dusk in two ways: Taken by the Dusk: a character with Dusking 1 or more who dies on a Bout of Madness. In Mendris, any death that leaves no sign of physical harm, while the victim had at least Dusking 1, is said to be taken by the Dusk. Dusktouched: a character whose Dusking reaches their Resolve + Composure without dying first. They don't die. They change. Because Dusking only rises with Tenacity damage from the Dusk, missing Tenacity and Dusking tend to rise together during a trip, so the Bout penalty effectively counts twice. Chance of death on a Bout: Missing Tenacity + Dusking Death chance 1 + 1 0% 2 + 2 2% 3 + 3 9% 4 + 4 26% Tenacity heals in downtime; Dusking doesn't. A character who recovers between trips still carries their Dusking into every Bout.","duskworld"],["Rules","Taken by the Dusk","Core Rules › Tenacity and madness › Taken by the Dusk","rules/stress-and-madness.html#taken-by-the-dusk","Taken by the Dusk: a character with Dusking 1 or more who dies on a Bout of Madness. In Mendris, any death that leaves no sign of physical harm, while the victim had at least Dusking 1, is said to be taken by the Dusk. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Dusktouched","Core Rules › Tenacity and madness › Taken by the Dusk","rules/stress-and-madness.html#taken-by-the-dusk","Dusktouched: a character whose Dusking reaches their Resolve + Composure without dying first. They don't die. They change. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Glints","Crafting › Devices › Glints","crafting.html#glints","Glints are the core component of every Duskworld Device. Each glint has a rating from 1 to 5, which is its number of charges. The Device's own rating still sets its dice and the crafting limit, and any glint fits any Device. Glint Rating Where it comes from Refill Forged glint 1–3 Mendris's workshops, copying kren tech Each Prepare at a rest refills 1 charge Kren glint 4–5 Storage compartments in the abandoned kren factories of the Dusklands Refills to full at the end of each rest Charges: a Device spends 1 charge from its glint each time it's used. A Complication on a Device roll spends 1 more. Running dry: a glint that reaches 0 charges is empty for good, and the Device stops working until it's refitted. Neither rest, downtime nor Field Repair revives an empty glint. A rating 1 glint is single-use. Downtime: the end of a week of downtime refills every glint that isn't empty. Burning out: when a Device spends its glint's last charge, you can burn it out on purpose: the Device rolls with +2d and raises the stakes. Refitting: putting a new glint into a Device takes an hour of work and needs Technology 2+. Empty glints: an empty forged glint is scrap. An empty kren glint becomes a sh","duskworld"],["Rules","Charges","Crafting › Devices › Glints","crafting.html#glints","Charges: a Device spends 1 charge from its glint each time it's used. A Complication on a Device roll spends 1 more. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Running dry","Crafting › Devices › Glints","crafting.html#glints","Running dry: a glint that reaches 0 charges is empty for good, and the Device stops working until it's refitted. Neither rest, downtime nor Field Repair revives an empty glint. A rating 1 glint is single-use. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Downtime","Crafting › Devices › Glints","crafting.html#glints","Downtime: the end of a week of downtime refills every glint that isn't empty. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Burning out","Crafting › Devices › Glints","crafting.html#glints","Burning out: when a Device spends its glint's last charge, you can burn it out on purpose: the Device rolls with +2d and raises the stakes. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Refitting","Crafting › Devices › Glints","crafting.html#glints","Refitting: putting a new glint into a Device takes an hour of work and needs Technology 2+. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Empty glints","Crafting › Devices › Glints","crafting.html#glints","Empty glints: an empty forged glint is scrap. An empty kren glint becomes a shell, Mendris's largest unit of money. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Buying and trading","Crafting › Devices › Buying and trading","crafting.html#buying-and-trading","Forged glints are legal, and are bought and sold like other gear. Forged glint Price Rating 1 2 handfuls of boxings Rating 2 1 slab Rating 3 3 slabs Kren glints are another matter. Selling one is illegal: they belong to the Houses. Surrendering a kren glint to a House earns favors equal to its rating with that House (see Favors). Fencing one gets its rating in slabs, a fraction of what it's worth. Finding a buyer is a Manipulation + Streetwise roll; on a Complication, someone notices.","duskworld"],["Rules","Surrendering","Crafting › Devices › Buying and trading","crafting.html#buying-and-trading","Surrendering a kren glint to a House earns favors equal to its rating with that House (see Favors). ","duskworld"],["Rules","Fencing","Crafting › Devices › Buying and trading","crafting.html#buying-and-trading","Fencing one gets its rating in slabs, a fraction of what it's worth. Finding a buyer is a Manipulation + Streetwise roll; on a Complication, someone notices. ","duskworld"],["Rules","Duskworld Devices","Crafting › Common Devices › Duskworld Devices","crafting.html#duskworld-devices","These follow the Device rules, powered by a glint. Device Function Dawnlamp Rating 3+. Set it and spend a charge: for an hour, everyone within 5 meters treats the exposure level as one lower. It can't lower Deep. Purifier Spend a charge to make food and water from the Dusklands safe for a day (see Dusklands food), for up to twice its rating in people. Shock plate Hide it in a floor or path. It rolls against the Dexterity + Stamina of the first creature to step on it: on a hit, 1 bashing damage and Stunned until the end of its next turn. Dusk chime Set it over a camp or path. It rings when a dusktouched or duskborn creature comes within 20 meters, rolling against their Stealth. Kren seal Seal a door or container with a charge. Opening it takes Technology against its roll, or breaking it by force.","duskworld"],["Conditions","Beaten Down","Conditions","conditions.html#beaten-down","Making an attack costs 1 Stress, unless you have no chance to surrender (the enemy won't or can't accept it, such as a mindless beast). If you surrender, clear 1 Stress. Ends when your total damage drops to your Beaten Down threshold or below.",""],["Conditions","Bleeding","Conditions","conditions.html#bleeding","Persistent damage: 1 lethal damage at the end of each of your turns, until the duration runs out or the wound is treated (with First aid, Field Medicine or Avoid Death).",""],["Conditions","Blinded","Conditions","conditions.html#blinded","Rolls that rely on sight use a chance die. Attacking or acting against a target you can hear, such as an adjacent enemy, takes −3d instead, as in darkness. You can't Dodge, and your other defense rolls take −2d.",""],["Conditions","Deafened","Conditions","conditions.html#deafened","Rolls that rely on hearing use a chance die, and you take −1d to notice anything around you. You can't benefit from anything that needs you to hear someone, such as a shouted order or a command, unless you can see them.",""],["Conditions","Determined","Conditions","conditions.html#determined","Your next Stress cost is reduced by 1.",""],["Conditions","Disoriented","Conditions","conditions.html#disoriented","You can't use reactions.",""],["Conditions","Exhausted","Conditions","conditions.html#exhausted","−1d to all rolls. Exhausted can stack, giving −1d for each stack. When your stacks reach your Beaten Down threshold (Stamina + Resolve), you collapse: you fall Unconscious until your stacks drop below it, usually at the end of a rest. If you gain another stack while collapsed, you die.",""],["Conditions","Frightened","Conditions","conditions.html#frightened","−1d to all rolls, or −2d instead on rolls against the source of your fear. Moving closer to the source of your fear costs 1 Stress each time you choose to do it.",""],["Conditions","Grappled","Conditions","conditions.html#grappled","You can't Move, Dodge or Disengage, and you can only attack unarmed, with a Light weapon, or with the Grapple action. Ends when someone breaks free.",""],["Conditions","Immobilized","Conditions","conditions.html#immobilized","You can't move.",""],["Conditions","Inspired","Conditions","conditions.html#inspired","After a failed roll, reroll every die that didn't succeed. Inspired ends when used.",""],["Conditions","Obscured","Conditions","conditions.html#obscured","Rolls to see or target you take −1d, and you can try to hide with Stealth. Smoke, fog, shadow and some powers cause it.",""],["Conditions","Prone","Conditions","conditions.html#prone","You're on the ground: melee attacks against you get +1d, ranged attacks −1d (see Position). Standing up takes 1 action.",""],["Conditions","Slowed","Conditions","conditions.html#slowed","Your Speed is halved.",""],["Conditions","Stunned","Conditions","conditions.html#stunned","In combat, you lose any reactions, and on your turn you gain two fewer actions and don't gain a reaction. Out of combat, you're overwhelmed: you move and react more slowly to your situation, at the GM's discretion.",""],["Conditions","Surprised","Conditions","conditions.html#surprised","You must take a slow turn and can't react.",""],["Conditions","Unconscious","Conditions","conditions.html#unconscious","You can't act or react. If damage knocked you out, see Avoiding death and Waking up.",""]]};
